{"parentUuid":"7f09852a-cbb6-4cde-97be-fb55f86086b5","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"5f5c15a1-f104-4841-968c-d1e3de6d4476","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"<ide_opened_file>The user opened the file /home/blu-bridge25/CP/.mdfiles/CREAM.md in the IDE. This may or may not be related to the current task.</ide_opened_file>"},{"type":"text","text":"@browser:https://github.com/bigai-nlco/CREAM.git  look into this repo and this helper md file @.mdfiles/CREAM.md   and help me understand if our @Scripts/Blutrain/bluscriptCP.cpp   can implement the same CREAM method, and if so what exactly with filenames would be required to changed."}]},"uuid":"0f959542-01da-415f-a1a4-66ee1bb3ed89","timestamp":"2026-07-20T13:00:16.295Z","permissionMode":"default","origin":{"kind":"human"},"promptSource":"sdk","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.212","gitBranch":"main"}
{"type":"file-history-snapshot","messageId":"0f959542-01da-415f-a1a4-66ee1bb3ed89","snapshot":{"messageId":"0f959542-01da-415f-a1a4-66ee1bb3ed89","trackedFileBackups":{"/home/blu-bridge25/.claude/plans/polished-cuddling-graham.md":{"backupFileName":null,"version":1,"backupTime":"2026-07-20T13:14:17.482Z"}},"timestamp":"2026-07-20T13:00:16.297Z"},"isSnapshotUpdate":false}
{"parentUuid":"0f959542-01da-415f-a1a4-66ee1bb3ed89","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"file","filename":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP/.mdfiles/CREAM.md","content":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP/.mdfiles/CREAM.md","content":"The constraint CREAM breaks\n\nNormally, position values and sequence length are locked together. With sequential positions:\n\nTo see position m=31999, you need a sequence of ≥32000 tokens.\n\nThere's no other way — if positions are always [0, 1, 2, ..., n-1], then to reach position 31999 you must have 32000 tokens in the sequence. That means 32K-length attention matrices, which cost O(32K²) memory.\nWhat CREAM does\n\nCREAM breaks that lock. It takes a 4K-token sequence and assigns it position labels from the 32K range:\n\nSequence length: 4000 tokens  ← memory cost stays here\nPosition values: [0..512, 14000..14511, 31500..31999]  ← model sees these m values\n\nThe attention matrix is still 4K×4K. But the model is forced to compute rotations at m=14000 and m=31999 — values it would otherwise only encounter in a 32K-token sequence.\nSo where is the saving?\n\nThe saving is: you never need to allocate a 32K-token sequence during training. Instead of:\n\nOption A (naive): train on 32K sequences → O(32K²) attention cost\nOption B (CREAM): train on 4K sequences with 32K position labels → O(4K²) attention cost\n\nThe 4K tokens are not fewer than before — they're the same count. What's eliminated is the need to pad/extend those sequences to 32K to expose the model to large m values. CREAM delivers the large m values to a short sequence directly, by relabeling.\n\nThe token count per sequence is unchanged. The sequence length needed to cover the full position range drops from 32K to 4K. That's where the 64x attention memory reduction comes from. train.py:196-210","numLines":25,"startLine":1,"totalLines":25}},"displayPath":".mdfiles/CREAM.md"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"c8f073c9-f678-44e6-acd0-805c2bec0575","timestamp":"2026-07-20T13:00:16.295Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.212","gitBranch":"main"}
