{"type":"file-history-snapshot","messageId":"b2c1ea6b-a2ec-4537-bdff-c1707367384f","snapshot":{"messageId":"b2c1ea6b-a2ec-4537-bdff-c1707367384f","trackedFileBackups":{"/home/blu-bridge25/.claude/plans/polished-cuddling-graham.md":{"backupFileName":"f0ab2c3c5549592b@v2","version":2,"backupTime":"2026-07-20T13:25:02.731Z"}},"timestamp":"2026-07-20T13:25:02.731Z"},"isSnapshotUpdate":false}
{"parentUuid":"b2c1ea6b-a2ec-4537-bdff-c1707367384f","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"file","filename":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP/.mdfiles/creamplanfix.md","content":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP/.mdfiles/creamplanfix.md","content":"Same rigor as the prior four, but this one benefits from an actual source-of-truth check, since CREAM is a real, verifiable published method (NeurIPS 2024, Wu et al. — \"Never Miss A Beat\"). I checked the plan's description against the paper directly, and found one thing worth catching before implementation, plus the usual seam-level items.\n\n**Core mechanism verification — this is correct and genuinely elegant.** The insight that `pos = local_idx + delta` collapsing to `pos = local_idx` at `CP_SIZE=1` means a pre-gathered `cache[L[i]]` table is indistinguishable to the kernel from \"real\" RoPE at position `L[i]` is exactly right — the kernel has no notion of \"true\" vs. \"relabeled\" position, it just indexes a row. Deferring CP>1 to an explicit position-array kernel change (rather than trying to force Gaussian-sampled non-linear labels through the linear delta formula) is the right call — the delta scheme is fundamentally a two-segment-linear representation and can't express an arbitrary label sequence, so there's no clever way to avoid that kernel change later; good that the plan says so rather than overreaching now. The causal-mask independence argument also correctly carries over from the earlier CP plans' established contract (local order drives masking, RoPE label is a separate concern) — CREAM's monotonicity requirement (head<middle<tail) is exactly what's needed to keep that decoupling valid, and the plan test-covers monotonicity explicitly rather than just asserting it.\n\n**The one thing worth checking against the actual mirrored source before coding, not just trusting the plan's summary:** the paper fixes head/tail length to a **small constant** — it states `0 < k << N` explicitly and uses `k=32` in their reported experiments — specifically *because* the method's benefit comes from maximizing how much of the sequence gets the Gaussian-sampled \"exploratory middle\" treatment; head/tail are just enough to preserve local continuity at the two ends. The plan's formula, `head_len=tail_len=model_max/3`, is a completely different regime — for a 4K model that's ~1365 tokens of head plus ~1365 of tail, consuming two-thirds of the physical sequence in fixed, non-exploratory continuity blocks and leaving only ~1/3 for the actual middle sampling the method is built around. That's not a crash-level bug — the three-block structure stays well-formed, labels stay monotonic, tests would still pass — it's a silently-different training regime that would likely blunt most of CREAM's advantage over plain PoSE without anything in the verification plan (loss decreases, no NaN) catching it. There is a real alternate branch in the follow-up literature where two segmentation methods are randomly chosen between (which may explain the plan's \"`or 4*factor` (random branch)\" aside), so this may be a legitimate ported branch from the actual `/tmp/cream_train.py` rather than a misreading — but given the magnitude of the discrepancy from the paper's stated `k=32`-style design, I'd explicitly diff the `head_len`/`tail_len` formula in `generate_cream_positions` against the mirrored source line-by-line before trusting it, rather than treating \"matches the plan's prose\" as sufficient — this is exactly the kind of thing worth a `bash`-level side-by-side check against `/tmp/cream_train.py` before or during the unit test, not just eyeballing.\n\n**That discrepancy compounds with a very concrete collision risk from this project's own established conventions.** Every prior CP smoke test in this thread has deliberately used a tiny physical `T` (256, in the bluscriptCP verification plan) to keep bring-up runs cheap on 2×RTX 3060. If `head_len=tail_len=model_max/3` is taken literally and `model_max` is the model's real pretrain length (e.g. 4096), then at `T=256` you get `head_len+tail_len ≈ 2730 > T` — a negative or degenerate middle-block length, likely violating the monotonicity invariant the whole causal-masking argument depends on. This will very plausibly fire on the very first smoke run given the project's established habit of using small `T` for bring-up, so I'd add an explicit guard (`assert(head_len + tail_len < T)`, or scale head/tail proportionally to physical `T` rather than to `model_max` when `T` is small) rather than relying on the boundary-case unit tests to catch it only in isolation — it needs to be caught at the integration point where a real (possibly tiny) `T` is chosen, not just in `cream_positions_test`'s synthetic inputs.\n\n**The batch-shared, resampled-per-step simplification is honestly flagged, but worth naming the specific consequence.** Applying one label draw to the whole batch each step (rather than per-row, as the reference does) means you lose exactly the intra-batch diversity of position draws the original method gets \"for free\" within a single gradient step — you're trading it for diversity across steps instead. That's a reasonable trade for a v1, but it means \"loss decreases, no NaN\" isn't a strong enough pass criterion on its own: a subtly-wrong truncated-Gaussian inverse-CDF (the `std::erf`-based sampling) could still produce a healthy-looking loss curve while systematically over- or under-sampling parts of the target range across steps — silently defeating the \"focus on the middle\" property that's the entire point of CREAM over plain PoSE. Worth adding a coarse distributional check to the verification plan: log the sampled middle-block start position across a few hundred steps and confirm the histogram approximates the intended truncated-Gaussian shape, not just that training doesn't blow up.\n\n**The `T != context_length` guard relaxation needs a replacement invariant, and it's the same bug class as before, one level up.** Removing that guard is correct and necessary since `scaled_max` is now deliberately decoupled from physical `T` — but per the pattern in every one of these plans so far, a removed guard should be replaced by a narrower one, not just deleted: `assert(scaled_max >= T)` at minimum. More importantly, the plan's aside that `CP_CREAM_SCALED_MAX` is \"also usable as `YARN_SCALE * orig`\" is exactly the two-independently-set-values-that-must-agree pattern flagged in the original YaRN plan review, now recurring one layer up: if CREAM's `scaled_max` and YaRN's `YARN_SCALE` are meant to be locked together, either derive one from the other at startup or add the explicit consistency assert — don't let them be two separately-settable env vars that happen to usually agree.\n\n**Related, and not addressed in the plan at all: is the \"full cache\" being gathered from actually YaRN-scaled, or plain extrapolated RoPE?** The cache-build bullet just says \"build the full cache at `scaled_max` instead of `context_length`\" — it doesn't say whether `YARN_SCALE` is expected to be simultaneously set above 1.0. If it's left at the default while `scaled_max` grows large, CREAM would be training the model against unmitigated high-frequency aliasing at the extreme labels — precisely the numerically-degenerate regime YaRN exists to avoid, and precisely the opposite of what CREAM is supposed to be teaching the model to handle well. Given the two features are clearly meant to compose (that's what the `YARN_SCALE * orig` aside implies), I'd make the composition explicit in the plan and add a cache spot-check — mirroring the original YaRN plan's own recommended spot-check — confirming a handful of `cream_cache` rows match the expected YaRN-interpolated values at their corresponding labels, not just that the gather mechanism itself is wired correctly.\n\n**Smaller items, quick to close out:**\n- Confirm `set_rope_cache`'s stored tensor is genuinely gradient-free end to end (not just \"the cache carries no grad\" as stated) — same one-line storage-identity habit this thread has applied to every other shared-buffer question so far.\n- The per-step install reads as gather-once-then-fan-out-to-all-layers, which is right — just worth a comment making that explicit so a future edit doesn't accidentally move the gather inside the per-layer loop and pay it `n_layers` times for no benefit.\n- The off-mode bit-for-bit regression check (verification step 2) is explicitly scoped to the training run — the `CP_EVAL_PPL` path gets its own separate install logic per the plan, so it should get the same explicit off-mode regression check, not inherit training's coverage by assumption.\n\nBottom line: the structural design (cache-row gather exploiting the existing delta-collapse-to-zero behavior, decoupling from causal masking, deferring the real per-token position array to CP>1) is sound and well-scoped. The one thing I'd actually resolve before writing code is the head/tail proportion — it's the single detail most likely to silently diverge from the published method's actual behavior while still passing every test in the plan as written, so it's worth the five minutes to diff against the mirrored source directly.","numLines":20,"startLine":1,"totalLines":20}},"displayPath":".mdfiles/creamplanfix.md"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"0c632732-0083-4f4f-8741-ff26f427bb0d","timestamp":"2026-07-20T13:25:02.712Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.212","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
