{"parentUuid":"b4937ea4-4396-4754-93fa-88f89e9bd8de","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CdDUGBusN4zU5LxUYNGN1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Short answer: **the metric is right (PPL vs. position index on long sequences), but the comparison as you phrased it is asymmetric — and that asymmetry would invalidate it.** Let me untangle it.\n\n## The key principle\n\nCREAM is a **training-time** technique. At **inference/eval you never use CREAM indices** — you feed a real long sequence with *natural* contiguous positions `[0..16k)` on the YaRN-scaled cache. Feeding a CREAM-trained model its own CREAM indices on 4k data just re-tests the training regime; it tells you nothing about whether it generalizes to real 16k context.\n\nSo the thing you're testing is: *does cheap CREAM training (4k attention, relabeled positions) produce a model that handles real 16k as well as expensive full-16k YaRN training does?*\n\n## The correct eval — hold everything equal except the training recipe\n\nAll models evaluated **identically**: real held-out 16k sequences, natural positions `[0..16k)`, `CP_CREAM_MODE=off`, `YARN_SCALE=4` (16k/4k), same windows.\n\n| Model | Training | Eval input | What its curve tells you |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **A — CREAM** | base(4k) + CREAM fine-tune, 4k physical attention, labels over [0,16k), YaRN cache | real 16k, natural pos | Did cheap training generalize? |\n| **B — YaRN full LCE** | base(4k) + YaRN fine-tune at **actual 16k** (full 16k attention) | real 16k, natural pos | The expensive gold standard |\n| **C — naive (baseline)** | base(4k), **no** long fine-tune | real 16k, natural pos + YaRN | The \"blows up past 4k\" reference |\n\n- **A ≈ B** (both flat/low PPL across positions, both beating **C**) is the CREAM win: same quality as full-16k training at a fraction of the FLOPs, since A never allocated a 16k attention matrix during training.\n- If A tracks C (blows up past ~4k) while B stays flat → CREAM didn't transfer.\n\nThe `end_id`/middle-block distribution log I added is for confirming the *training* sampler is healthy — not part of this eval.\n\n## Concretely in this codebase\n\nRun `CP_EVAL_PPL` per checkpoint with CREAM **off** and YaRN on:\n\n```bash\nCP_SIZE=1 CP_EVAL_PPL=1 CP_CREAM_MODE=off \\\nCP_T=16384 YARN_SCALE=4 YARN_ORIG_MAXPOS=4096 \\\nCP_CKPT_RESUME=<run> CP_EVAL_OUT=ppl_A_cream.csv CP_EVAL_WINDOWS=<n> \\\n    make CP_FUSED_ROPE=1 run-bluscript-cp NP=1\n```\n\nRepeat with each checkpoint (`ppl_B_yarn.csv`, `ppl_C_naive.csv`), then plot PPL vs. position from the CSVs (the existing notebook cell).\n\nTwo caveats specific to here:\n1. **Memory:** `CP_EVAL_PPL` requires `CP_SIZE=1`, so a true 16k eval is one 16k×16k attention on a single 3060 — forward-only, but it may not fit. That's exactly the case that would need CP for eval (deferred). Start smaller (e.g. 4k→8k, `YARN_SCALE=2`) to prove the shape, then scale.\n2. **Data:** you need held-out documents **≥16k tokens** so positions near 16k actually carry real signal.\n\nTerminology nit: it's base(4k) **fine-tuned** with CREAM (not \"pretrained in CREAM\"), and \"16k interval\" = labels spanning `[0,16k)` with `factor = YARN_SCALE = 4`.\n\nWant me to add a small A/B/C eval driver script (loops the three checkpoints and emits the three CSVs) so this is one command?"}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1112,"cache_read_input_tokens":277742,"output_tokens":4045,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1112,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":4045,"cache_read_input_tokens":277742,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1112,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1112},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdDUG8Xy8Dvah63MqGDS4","type":"assistant","uuid":"11838be0-65da-4149-b89a-b36d4c6ea25f","timestamp":"2026-07-20T14:32:13.572Z","effort":"high","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.212","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
