{"parentUuid":"8520fd53-2e0c-486f-a706-4eec897e8b4b","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CdEdFo6EKUYrmRhnc9uWm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Right — but be precise about what's varying, because CREAM and YaRN aren't competitors; **CREAM sits on top of YaRN.**\n\n- **YaRN** = the RoPE *frequency* interpolation (NTK-by-parts). It fixes *how* rotations are computed so large positions don't alias. It's the same in both arms.\n- **CREAM** = a *training-time position-index* scheme. In my implementation the CREAM cache is always gathered from the YaRN-scaled cache, so your CREAM arm **is** CREAM+YaRN.\n\nSo the head-to-head is:\n\n| Arm | Interpolation | Training positions | Training attention cost |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **A — CREAM+YaRN** | YaRN | relabeled, 4k physical → labels span 16k | **4k×4k** (cheap) |\n| **B — YaRN LCE** | YaRN | natural, real 16k sequences | **16k×16k** (expensive) |\n| **C — naive YaRN** | YaRN | none (inference-only) | — |\n\nBoth A and B use YaRN identically — you're **not** testing YaRN vs no-YaRN. You're isolating the *one* variable that CREAM changes: the training-position strategy and the attention cost that comes with it.\n\nThe claim you're validating: **A matches B on real-16k PPL, without ever allocating a 16k attention matrix during training.** C is the floor both should beat.\n\nSo don't read it as \"CREAM vs YaRN.\" Read it as: *given YaRN, does cheap CREAM relabeling (4k) buy you the same long-context quality as expensive full-16k fine-tuning?*\n\nOne caveat for arm B in this codebase: training at real 16k needs a 16k×16k attention, which on a single 3060 likely needs CP (multi-GPU) — the very cost CREAM avoids. That asymmetry is exactly CREAM's selling point, but it means B may be the harder run to produce on your hardware."}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1569,"cache_read_input_tokens":285743,"output_tokens":1593,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1569,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1593,"cache_read_input_tokens":285743,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1569,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1569},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdEdFjUwfGgP6tPBEHB4H","type":"assistant","uuid":"f019f14c-86b9-4be8-a884-364a46cbbb29","timestamp":"2026-07-21T05:10:02.737Z","effort":"low","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.215","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
{"parentUuid":"f019f14c-86b9-4be8-a884-364a46cbbb29","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"Stop","toolUseID":"b262ca36-1052-4e95-8c2d-93bb3ffd5923","hookEvent":"Stop","content":"","stdout":"{\"session_id\":\"7f89f0ac-da79-42e5-975f-b63bab3c65d2\",\"transcript_path\":\"/home/blu-bridge25/.claude/projects/-home-blu-bridge25-CP/7f89f0ac-da79-42e5-975f-b63bab3c65d2.jsonl\",\"cwd\":\"/home/blu-bridge25/CP\",\"prompt_id\":\"429fe629-bbe4-4bb1-9269-43d9c0bd2445\",\"permission_mode\":\"acceptEdits\",\"effort\":{\"level\":\"low\"},\"hook_event_name\":\"Stop\",\"stop_hook_active\":false,\"last_assistant_message\":\"Right — but be precise about what's varying, because CREAM and YaRN aren't competitors; **CREAM sits on top of YaRN.**\\n\\n- **YaRN** = the RoPE *frequency* interpolation (NTK-by-parts). It fixes *how* rotations are computed so large positions don't alias. It's the same in both arms.\\n- **CREAM** = a *training-time position-index* scheme. In my implementation the CREAM cache is always gathered from the YaRN-scaled cache, so your CREAM arm **is** CREAM+YaRN.\\n\\nSo the head-to-head is:\\n\\n| Arm | Interpolation | Training positions | Training attention cost |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| **A — CREAM+YaRN** | YaRN | relabeled, 4k physical → labels span 16k | **4k×4k** (cheap) |\\n| **B — YaRN LCE** | YaRN | natural, real 16k sequences | **16k×16k** (expensive) |\\n| **C — naive YaRN** | YaRN | none (inference-only) | — |\\n\\nBoth A and B use YaRN identically — you're **not** testing YaRN vs no-YaRN. You're isolating the *one* variable that CREAM changes: the training-position strategy and the attention cost that comes with it.\\n\\nThe claim you're validating: **A matches B on real-16k PPL, without ever allocating a 16k attention matrix during training.** C is the floor both should beat.\\n\\nSo don't read it as \\\"CREAM vs YaRN.\\\" Read it as: *given YaRN, does cheap CREAM relabeling (4k) buy you the same long-context quality as expensive full-16k fine-tuning?*\\n\\nOne caveat for arm B in this codebase: training at real 16k needs a 16k×16k attention, which on a single 3060 likely needs CP (multi-GPU) — the very cost CREAM avoids. That asymmetry is exactly CREAM's selling point, but it means B may be the harder run to produce on your hardware.\",\"background_tasks\":[],\"session_crons\":[]}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"node \"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/scripts/hooks/run-with-flags.js\" \"stop:check-console-log\" \"scripts/hooks/check-console-log.js\" \"standard,strict\"","durationMs":46},"type":"attachment","uuid":"6d5470da-8fbf-463d-8550-13ce058ba406","timestamp":"2026-07-21T05:10:02.872Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.215","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
