{"parentUuid":"38f0242d-8509-43f0-96f2-9f2284280d69","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CdEdyWJc4mxNBs83PNHxX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Not by the same *mechanism* — and that's the crux of what your eval is actually testing.\n\n**How the two get coverage differs fundamentally:**\n\n- **YaRN at real 16k:** every training sequence contains *all* positions 0…16k−1. Coverage is **uniform and complete every single step**.\n- **CREAM at 4k:** each step the model sees only ~4k positions — head `[0,head)` (always), a **stochastically sampled** middle block, and tail near 16k (always). Full-range coverage is built up **across many steps**, not within one.\n\nSo CREAM never gives per-step uniform coverage. With **σ=3** the middle-block sampling is deliberately **centered on the middle** of the range (μ=1+factor): the center gets dense exposure, the two ends are always present, and the **\"shoulders\"** (between head and the Gaussian's reach, and between its reach and tail) get the *least*. That non-uniformity is intentional — models are weakest in the middle, so CREAM over-invests there rather than spreading thin like uniform PoSE/RandPos.\n\n**So will it produce the same understanding as YaRN-16k?** That's an **empirical** question — it is exactly what \"PPL vs. position index\" measures, and why you plot against *position* rather than a single average:\n\n- A **flat** CREAM curve across [0,16k) that tracks the YaRN-16k curve → yes, the stochastic + middle-weighted coverage was enough; you got the \"radar\" at a fraction of the training cost.\n- **Bumps** at specific position bands → CREAM under-covered those bands. σ too small starves the shoulders; σ too large loses the middle advantage. The bump *location* tells you which.\n\nThat's the payoff of the whole design: the position-resolved PPL curve is your radar. σ=3 is the paper's tuned default for making the middle strong while keeping the ends pinned — but on your model/data you confirm it by checking the curve is flat, not by assuming coverage equals YaRN's.\n\nSo: **run A (CREAM, σ=3) and B (YaRN-16k) with matched config, plot both PPL-vs-position, and overlay them.** If A ≈ B and flat, σ=3 gave you equivalent understanding cheaply. If A has bumps B doesn't, sweep σ."}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":563,"cache_read_input_tokens":292376,"output_tokens":2258,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":563,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2258,"cache_read_input_tokens":292376,"cache_creation_input_tokens":563,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":563},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdEdyTRiN4UKQ2YHG6ot6","type":"assistant","uuid":"0698f92b-ec0f-41ac-8d3a-427ae7f623c1","timestamp":"2026-07-21T05:19:41.324Z","effort":"low","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.215","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
{"parentUuid":"0698f92b-ec0f-41ac-8d3a-427ae7f623c1","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"Stop","toolUseID":"5cb5e91b-f792-492f-bb51-e7468dde751e","hookEvent":"Stop","content":"","stdout":"{\"session_id\":\"7f89f0ac-da79-42e5-975f-b63bab3c65d2\",\"transcript_path\":\"/home/blu-bridge25/.claude/projects/-home-blu-bridge25-CP/7f89f0ac-da79-42e5-975f-b63bab3c65d2.jsonl\",\"cwd\":\"/home/blu-bridge25/CP\",\"prompt_id\":\"0d7818ec-3758-43cb-bcae-49cab1659736\",\"permission_mode\":\"acceptEdits\",\"effort\":{\"level\":\"low\"},\"hook_event_name\":\"Stop\",\"stop_hook_active\":false,\"last_assistant_message\":\"Not by the same *mechanism* — and that's the crux of what your eval is actually testing.\\n\\n**How the two get coverage differs fundamentally:**\\n\\n- **YaRN at real 16k:** every training sequence contains *all* positions 0…16k−1. Coverage is **uniform and complete every single step**.\\n- **CREAM at 4k:** each step the model sees only ~4k positions — head `[0,head)` (always), a **stochastically sampled** middle block, and tail near 16k (always). Full-range coverage is built up **across many steps**, not within one.\\n\\nSo CREAM never gives per-step uniform coverage. With **σ=3** the middle-block sampling is deliberately **centered on the middle** of the range (μ=1+factor): the center gets dense exposure, the two ends are always present, and the **\\\"shoulders\\\"** (between head and the Gaussian's reach, and between its reach and tail) get the *least*. That non-uniformity is intentional — models are weakest in the middle, so CREAM over-invests there rather than spreading thin like uniform PoSE/RandPos.\\n\\n**So will it produce the same understanding as YaRN-16k?** That's an **empirical** question — it is exactly what \\\"PPL vs. position index\\\" measures, and why you plot against *position* rather than a single average:\\n\\n- A **flat** CREAM curve across [0,16k) that tracks the YaRN-16k curve → yes, the stochastic + middle-weighted coverage was enough; you got the \\\"radar\\\" at a fraction of the training cost.\\n- **Bumps** at specific position bands → CREAM under-covered those bands. σ too small starves the shoulders; σ too large loses the middle advantage. The bump *location* tells you which.\\n\\nThat's the payoff of the whole design: the position-resolved PPL curve is your radar. σ=3 is the paper's tuned default for making the middle strong while keeping the ends pinned — but on your model/data you confirm it by checking the curve is flat, not by assuming coverage equals YaRN's.\\n\\nSo: **run A (CREAM, σ=3) and B (YaRN-16k) with matched config, plot both PPL-vs-position, and overlay them.** If A ≈ B and flat, σ=3 gave you equivalent understanding cheaply. If A has bumps B doesn't, sweep σ.\",\"background_tasks\":[],\"session_crons\":[]}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"node \"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/scripts/hooks/run-with-flags.js\" \"stop:check-console-log\" \"scripts/hooks/check-console-log.js\" \"standard,strict\"","durationMs":52},"type":"attachment","uuid":"19c8725a-a166-4337-94ed-7b316dd0a4b4","timestamp":"2026-07-21T05:19:41.728Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.215","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
