{"parentUuid":"efba4f94-7231-448a-a4a9-fe032c3e689c","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CdFGsso4jcQsvrxRitRB3","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Correct — and that's deliberate, not a mistake.\n\n**CREAM lives in the weights, not in the eval.** Arm A's \"CREAM-ness\" is baked into checkpoint `run 101` during *training* — that's where the manipulated position labels did their job (teaching the weights to handle positions out to 16k). Once trained, the model is just a set of weights.\n\nAt eval you're asking: *does this trained model handle a real 16k sequence?* So every model — C, A, and B — is fed the **same** real 16k input with **natural** positions `[0..16k)` on the YaRN cache, i.e. `CP_CREAM_MODE=off`. The only thing that differs is which checkpoint you load.\n\nIf you turned CREAM **on** at eval for Arm A, you'd be feeding it synthetic relabeled positions (head + Gaussian-middle + tail) on short data — that just replays the training regime and tells you nothing about real long-context generalization. It would also make A's eval incomparable to B's (which never had CREAM), breaking the head-to-head.\n\nSo the rule:\n- **Training:** A = CREAM on, B = CREAM off, C = no long fine-tune.\n- **Eval:** CREAM off for **all three**, identical real-16k input.\n\nThe fact that A was *trained* with CREAM is entirely captured by loading `CP_CKPT_RESUME=101`. That's exactly what the eval is measuring."}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2702,"cache_read_input_tokens":305258,"output_tokens":881,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2702,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":881,"cache_read_input_tokens":305258,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2702,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2702},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdFGspZbio3DxQ3iAgke5","type":"assistant","uuid":"4442ac34-b246-4adc-a44b-750ac38acb56","timestamp":"2026-07-21T13:23:16.033Z","effort":"low","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.215","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
{"parentUuid":"4442ac34-b246-4adc-a44b-750ac38acb56","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"Stop","toolUseID":"f66c59c8-abdf-4a0e-885e-d46f25737943","hookEvent":"Stop","content":"","stdout":"{\"session_id\":\"7f89f0ac-da79-42e5-975f-b63bab3c65d2\",\"transcript_path\":\"/home/blu-bridge25/.claude/projects/-home-blu-bridge25-CP/7f89f0ac-da79-42e5-975f-b63bab3c65d2.jsonl\",\"cwd\":\"/home/blu-bridge25/CP\",\"prompt_id\":\"6837183e-6be3-43c2-8de4-4fa996d55516\",\"permission_mode\":\"acceptEdits\",\"effort\":{\"level\":\"low\"},\"hook_event_name\":\"Stop\",\"stop_hook_active\":false,\"last_assistant_message\":\"Correct — and that's deliberate, not a mistake.\\n\\n**CREAM lives in the weights, not in the eval.** Arm A's \\\"CREAM-ness\\\" is baked into checkpoint `run 101` during *training* — that's where the manipulated position labels did their job (teaching the weights to handle positions out to 16k). Once trained, the model is just a set of weights.\\n\\nAt eval you're asking: *does this trained model handle a real 16k sequence?* So every model — C, A, and B — is fed the **same** real 16k input with **natural** positions `[0..16k)` on the YaRN cache, i.e. `CP_CREAM_MODE=off`. The only thing that differs is which checkpoint you load.\\n\\nIf you turned CREAM **on** at eval for Arm A, you'd be feeding it synthetic relabeled positions (head + Gaussian-middle + tail) on short data — that just replays the training regime and tells you nothing about real long-context generalization. It would also make A's eval incomparable to B's (which never had CREAM), breaking the head-to-head.\\n\\nSo the rule:\\n- **Training:** A = CREAM on, B = CREAM off, C = no long fine-tune.\\n- **Eval:** CREAM off for **all three**, identical real-16k input.\\n\\nThe fact that A was *trained* with CREAM is entirely captured by loading `CP_CKPT_RESUME=101`. That's exactly what the eval is measuring.\",\"background_tasks\":[],\"session_crons\":[]}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"node \"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/scripts/hooks/run-with-flags.js\" \"stop:check-console-log\" \"scripts/hooks/check-console-log.js\" \"standard,strict\"","durationMs":46},"type":"attachment","uuid":"85072e25-e47e-4d6f-a849-eaaa6d6320a0","timestamp":"2026-07-21T13:23:16.107Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.215","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
