{"parentUuid":"d742d873-c842-44d4-9a61-fd578362a849","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CdFLT2DqpZPibYwVpWJwQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3756,"cache_read_input_tokens":317162,"output_tokens":2382,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3756,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2382,"cache_read_input_tokens":317162,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3756,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3756},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdFLSvT5CJwvJ1ZrmLCxT","type":"assistant","uuid":"f0d92e8c-aa04-4502-a3db-1db10b9512f2","timestamp":"2026-07-21T14:10:15.618Z","effort":"low","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.215","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
{"parentUuid":"f0d92e8c-aa04-4502-a3db-1db10b9512f2","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CdFLT2DqpZPibYwVpWJwQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This is the healthy version of the experiment — and it reads as a **success** for CREAM.\n\n## What the three curves say now\n- **C (blue, naive) is consistently the highest** (~150–250), sitting clearly above the other two across the whole 16k.\n- **A (CREAM, orange) and B (YaRN-LCE, green) track each other closely** (~90–170), both below C. B dips slightly lower than A in places.\n- **All three are flat** — no blow-up past the 4096 line.\n\n## Interpretation\n1. **A ≈ B → CREAM matched full YaRN fine-tuning.** The orange and green curves are essentially the same shape and level across all 16k positions. That's the headline result: CREAM, trained with only 4k×4k attention, reproduced what B got from full 16k fine-tuning.\n2. **C > (A ≈ B) everywhere → fine-tuning helped, uniformly.** Both extended models beat inference-only YaRN by a consistent margin at every position, not just past 4096. That margin is the value the fine-tune added.\n3. **C doesn't explode past 4096 — and that's expected.** C is base + *YaRN at inference*; YaRN's frequency interpolation already prevents the catastrophic aliasing you'd see with plain RoPE. So the naive baseline here is \"works but suboptimal,\" not \"broken.\" The story is therefore *\"fine-tuning (A or B) gives a steady PPL improvement over inference-only YaRN, and CREAM gets that improvement cheaply,\"* — not *\"CREAM prevents a blow-up\"* (YaRN does that part).\n4. **The curve is flat all the way to 16k → CREAM's coverage held.** This directly answers your earlier \"radar\" question: σ=3's Gaussian-middle sampling left no visible position band under-trained — no worsening toward the ends or middle. Good.\n\n## Honest caveats\n- **B occasionally edges below A** — full LCE being marginally better than the CREAM approximation is exactly what you'd expect. The point is *how small* that gap is: near-identical for a fraction of B's training FLOPs.\n- **The C→A/B gap is modest** (~30–40% PPL). That's a reasonable 300-step fine-tune gain; if you want a more dramatic separation, run the base longer and give A/B more fine-tune steps — the relative ordering should hold and widen.\n- Edge dips at pos 0 / 16383 are still the smoothing boundary artifact — ignore.\n\n## Confirm it numerically\nPaste the **verdict table** (`PPL<4096 / PPL≥4096 / ratio`). You want:\n- All three ratios ≈ 1.0 (flat — confirms no position blow-up).\n- `before`/`after` means with **C highest, A ≈ B lower** — quantifying the gap your eye sees.\n\nIf those hold, you've demonstrated the CREAM claim on your 48M model: **cheap 4k-attention training ≈ full 16k fine-tuning across the entire extended context.**"}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3756,"cache_read_input_tokens":317162,"output_tokens":2382,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3756,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2382,"cache_read_input_tokens":317162,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3756,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3756},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdFLSvT5CJwvJ1ZrmLCxT","type":"assistant","uuid":"a3133a19-47fb-4d43-aa1c-ce98322b3442","timestamp":"2026-07-21T14:10:29.451Z","effort":"low","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.215","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
{"parentUuid":"a3133a19-47fb-4d43-aa1c-ce98322b3442","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"Stop","toolUseID":"feb192ad-653d-4206-ba82-a3028112fdb7","hookEvent":"Stop","content":"","stdout":"{\"session_id\":\"7f89f0ac-da79-42e5-975f-b63bab3c65d2\",\"transcript_path\":\"/home/blu-bridge25/.claude/projects/-home-blu-bridge25-CP/7f89f0ac-da79-42e5-975f-b63bab3c65d2.jsonl\",\"cwd\":\"/home/blu-bridge25/CP\",\"prompt_id\":\"b3e73e16-8111-49f9-907e-06f77d139c0e\",\"permission_mode\":\"acceptEdits\",\"effort\":{\"level\":\"low\"},\"hook_event_name\":\"Stop\",\"stop_hook_active\":false,\"last_assistant_message\":\"This is the healthy version of the experiment — and it reads as a **success** for CREAM.\\n\\n## What the three curves say now\\n- **C (blue, naive) is consistently the highest** (~150–250), sitting clearly above the other two across the whole 16k.\\n- **A (CREAM, orange) and B (YaRN-LCE, green) track each other closely** (~90–170), both below C. B dips slightly lower than A in places.\\n- **All three are flat** — no blow-up past the 4096 line.\\n\\n## Interpretation\\n1. **A ≈ B → CREAM matched full YaRN fine-tuning.** The orange and green curves are essentially the same shape and level across all 16k positions. That's the headline result: CREAM, trained with only 4k×4k attention, reproduced what B got from full 16k fine-tuning.\\n2. **C > (A ≈ B) everywhere → fine-tuning helped, uniformly.** Both extended models beat inference-only YaRN by a consistent margin at every position, not just past 4096. That margin is the value the fine-tune added.\\n3. **C doesn't explode past 4096 — and that's expected.** C is base + *YaRN at inference*; YaRN's frequency interpolation already prevents the catastrophic aliasing you'd see with plain RoPE. So the naive baseline here is \\\"works but suboptimal,\\\" not \\\"broken.\\\" The story is therefore *\\\"fine-tuning (A or B) gives a steady PPL improvement over inference-only YaRN, and CREAM gets that improvement cheaply,\\\"* — not *\\\"CREAM prevents a blow-up\\\"* (YaRN does that part).\\n4. **The curve is flat all the way to 16k → CREAM's coverage held.** This directly answers your earlier \\\"radar\\\" question: σ=3's Gaussian-middle sampling left no visible position band under-trained — no worsening toward the ends or middle. Good.\\n\\n## Honest caveats\\n- **B occasionally edges below A** — full LCE being marginally better than the CREAM approximation is exactly what you'd expect. The point is *how small* that gap is: near-identical for a fraction of B's training FLOPs.\\n- **The C→A/B gap is modest** (~30–40% PPL). That's a reasonable 300-step fine-tune gain; if you want a more dramatic separation, run the base longer and give A/B more fine-tune steps — the relative ordering should hold and widen.\\n- Edge dips at pos 0 / 16383 are still the smoothing boundary artifact — ignore.\\n\\n## Confirm it numerically\\nPaste the **verdict table** (`PPL<4096 / PPL≥4096 / ratio`). You want:\\n- All three ratios ≈ 1.0 (flat — confirms no position blow-up).\\n- `before`/`after` means with **C highest, A ≈ B lower** — quantifying the gap your eye sees.\\n\\nIf those hold, you've demonstrated the CREAM claim on your 48M model: **cheap 4k-attention training ≈ full 16k fine-tuning across the entire extended context.**\",\"background_tasks\":[],\"session_crons\":[]}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"node \"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/scripts/hooks/run-with-flags.js\" \"stop:check-console-log\" \"scripts/hooks/check-console-log.js\" \"standard,strict\"","durationMs":51},"type":"attachment","uuid":"41b2b2b8-3e2c-4344-8c94-9a0160067e7b","timestamp":"2026-07-21T14:10:29.605Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.215","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
