{"parentUuid":"075e3e47-8c03-42d6-932a-f8cc1f412d91","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"async_hook_response","processId":"async_hook_53719","hookName":"Stop","hookEvent":"Stop","response":{},"stdout":"","stderr":"[ContinuousLearning] Session has 132 messages - evaluate for extractable patterns\n[ContinuousLearning] Save learned skills to: /home/blu-bridge25/.claude/skills/learned/\n","exitCode":0},"type":"attachment","uuid":"7b1a3982-abe6-48f9-804f-794d950eaf4b","timestamp":"2026-07-21T14:13:39.800Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.215","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
{"parentUuid":"7b1a3982-abe6-48f9-804f-794d950eaf4b","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CdFLjthsogNS6HzuYcm8n","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2399,"cache_read_input_tokens":320918,"output_tokens":1910,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2399,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1910,"cache_read_input_tokens":320918,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2399,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2399},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdFLjqqjVu4wKuDcHiiFB","type":"assistant","uuid":"97992321-28fe-4ce8-a892-6e69fc27cf04","timestamp":"2026-07-21T14:14:01.623Z","effort":"low","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.215","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
{"parentUuid":"97992321-28fe-4ce8-a892-6e69fc27cf04","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CdFLjthsogNS6HzuYcm8n","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"No — the opposite. **CREAM runs *on top of* YaRN; it doesn't replace it.** Your Arm A is literally \"CREAM+YaRN\" — the cache CREAM gathers from is the YaRN-interpolated cache (in the code, `factor = YARN_SCALE` and the startup asserts require `YARN_SCALE > 1`). Turn YaRN off and CREAM would be gathering rows from a *plain-extrapolated* cache, whose high-position rotations alias — you'd lose exactly what the flat curve gave you.\n\nThey operate at different layers:\n\n| | Role | What it does |\n|---|---|---|\n| **YaRN** | frequency interpolation | Makes the RoPE rotations at large positions well-behaved (no aliasing). *How* positions are encoded. |\n| **CREAM** | training-position sampling | Exposes a short (4k) sequence to those large positions cheaply. *Which* positions the model trains on. |\n\n**What CREAM replaces is the expensive part of B — not YaRN.** B got its long-context ability by fine-tuning on *real 16k sequences* (16k×16k attention, 16k-token data). CREAM got the *same* ability while only ever running 4k×4k attention. So the experiment says:\n\n> Given YaRN, you don't need long-sequence training data or 16k attention to fine-tune — CREAM's relabeling substitutes for that.\n\nIt does **not** say you can drop the frequency interpolation. YaRN is still the backbone both A and B stand on.\n\nIf you actually want to test \"is YaRN needed,\" that's a *separate ablation*: CREAM relabeling on a **plain** RoPE cache (no NTK-by-parts) vs on the YaRN cache. My current implementation couples them (`factor = YARN_SCALE`), so that would need a small code change to build the gather-source cache with interpolation off. Prediction: without YaRN, the far-position PPL degrades — because relabeling alone puts the model at frequencies it was never taught to represent, which is the aliasing YaRN exists to fix.\n\nSo: CREAM removes the need for **expensive long-context training**, YaRN removes the need for **frequency aliasing to break** — different jobs, and you kept both."}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2399,"cache_read_input_tokens":320918,"output_tokens":1910,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2399,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1910,"cache_read_input_tokens":320918,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2399,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2399},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdFLjqqjVu4wKuDcHiiFB","type":"assistant","uuid":"ba48af72-ec4e-4f94-a73a-2956964eaacd","timestamp":"2026-07-21T14:14:11.439Z","effort":"low","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.215","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
{"parentUuid":"ba48af72-ec4e-4f94-a73a-2956964eaacd","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"Stop","toolUseID":"184d1aaf-b554-4597-95a9-1e3074071dd4","hookEvent":"Stop","content":"","stdout":"{\"session_id\":\"7f89f0ac-da79-42e5-975f-b63bab3c65d2\",\"transcript_path\":\"/home/blu-bridge25/.claude/projects/-home-blu-bridge25-CP/7f89f0ac-da79-42e5-975f-b63bab3c65d2.jsonl\",\"cwd\":\"/home/blu-bridge25/CP\",\"prompt_id\":\"91d4c8e1-2464-41e4-910e-020bf80d93ec\",\"permission_mode\":\"acceptEdits\",\"effort\":{\"level\":\"low\"},\"hook_event_name\":\"Stop\",\"stop_hook_active\":false,\"last_assistant_message\":\"No — the opposite. **CREAM runs *on top of* YaRN; it doesn't replace it.** Your Arm A is literally \\\"CREAM+YaRN\\\" — the cache CREAM gathers from is the YaRN-interpolated cache (in the code, `factor = YARN_SCALE` and the startup asserts require `YARN_SCALE > 1`). Turn YaRN off and CREAM would be gathering rows from a *plain-extrapolated* cache, whose high-position rotations alias — you'd lose exactly what the flat curve gave you.\\n\\nThey operate at different layers:\\n\\n| | Role | What it does |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| **YaRN** | frequency interpolation | Makes the RoPE rotations at large positions well-behaved (no aliasing). *How* positions are encoded. |\\n| **CREAM** | training-position sampling | Exposes a short (4k) sequence to those large positions cheaply. *Which* positions the model trains on. |\\n\\n**What CREAM replaces is the expensive part of B — not YaRN.** B got its long-context ability by fine-tuning on *real 16k sequences* (16k×16k attention, 16k-token data). CREAM got the *same* ability while only ever running 4k×4k attention. So the experiment says:\\n\\n> Given YaRN, you don't need long-sequence training data or 16k attention to fine-tune — CREAM's relabeling substitutes for that.\\n\\nIt does **not** say you can drop the frequency interpolation. YaRN is still the backbone both A and B stand on.\\n\\nIf you actually want to test \\\"is YaRN needed,\\\" that's a *separate ablation*: CREAM relabeling on a **plain** RoPE cache (no NTK-by-parts) vs on the YaRN cache. My current implementation couples them (`factor = YARN_SCALE`), so that would need a small code change to build the gather-source cache with interpolation off. Prediction: without YaRN, the far-position PPL degrades — because relabeling alone puts the model at frequencies it was never taught to represent, which is the aliasing YaRN exists to fix.\\n\\nSo: CREAM removes the need for **expensive long-context training**, YaRN removes the need for **frequency aliasing to break** — different jobs, and you kept both.\",\"background_tasks\":[],\"session_crons\":[]}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"node \"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/scripts/hooks/run-with-flags.js\" \"stop:check-console-log\" \"scripts/hooks/check-console-log.js\" \"standard,strict\"","durationMs":44},"type":"attachment","uuid":"d858cd37-6ea0-4e9c-9fd7-28eb47fec4ae","timestamp":"2026-07-21T14:14:11.518Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.215","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
