{"parentUuid":"c5e969dd-a77a-494c-97bf-858419368b76","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"async_hook_response","processId":"async_hook_53973","hookName":"Stop","hookEvent":"Stop","response":{},"stdout":"","stderr":"[SessionEnd] Updated session file: /home/blu-bridge25/.claude/sessions/2026-07-21-CP-session.tmp\n","exitCode":0},"type":"attachment","uuid":"dcd7a851-6d6e-44ab-9f46-c526524c1668","timestamp":"2026-07-21T14:15:18.912Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.215","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
{"parentUuid":"dcd7a851-6d6e-44ab-9f46-c526524c1668","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CdFLsDTguoHERGqH1btHv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2495,"cache_read_input_tokens":323317,"output_tokens":2268,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2495,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2268,"cache_read_input_tokens":323317,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2495,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2495},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdFLs9ezbcmm92DK2Y7cd","type":"assistant","uuid":"0bba19a2-854f-4b6a-aacb-a1155485a4c7","timestamp":"2026-07-21T14:15:42.858Z","effort":"low","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.215","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
{"parentUuid":"0bba19a2-854f-4b6a-aacb-a1155485a4c7","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CdFLsDTguoHERGqH1btHv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Because the thing YaRN fixes is driven by the position **labels**, not by how many tokens are physically present. CREAM's whole point is to put **label 16000** on a token even though there are only 4k tokens in memory — and computing RoPE at position 16000 is exactly what needs YaRN.\n\nWalk through what the kernel does for a token CREAM labeled `m = 16000`:\n\nRoPE rotates each dim pair by an angle `θ_i = m · base^(-2i/d)`. Plug in `m = 16000` on a model pretrained at 4k, and the higher-frequency dims spin through **many more cycles than the model ever saw** (it only ever experienced `m ≤ 4096`). Those large-`m` rotation patterns are out-of-distribution — the classic RoPE extrapolation/aliasing failure. The token being one of only 4k physical tokens doesn't change its angle at all; the angle depends only on the **label value 16000**.\n\nSo there are two separate costs, driven by two separate quantities:\n\n| Cost | Driven by | CREAM's effect |\n|---|---|---|\n| Attention **memory** (T×T) | physical token count (4k) | shrinks it — stays 4k×4k |\n| RoPE **rotation frequencies** | position **label** magnitude (16k) | **pushes it to 16k** |\n\nCREAM attacks the first and *makes the second worse* — it deliberately creates 16k-scale rotations on a short sequence. Nothing about a 4k physical sequence tames the `m=16000` angles. **That taming is YaRN's job**: NTK-by-parts rescales the per-dim frequencies so `m=16000` lands on rotation angles the 4k-pretrained model can actually interpret (interpolated, not extrapolated).\n\nSo:\n- **CREAM** = \"expose the model to position 16000 cheaply (no 16k attention, no 16k data).\"\n- **YaRN** = \"make position 16000 *representable* so that exposure teaches something useful instead of garbage.\"\n\nWithout YaRN, CREAM would still feed label 16000 — but through a plain cache whose `cos/sin[16000]` are aliased. You'd be training the model on rotations it can't cleanly encode; the far-position PPL would degrade. That's the ablation I mentioned: CREAM on a plain cache vs on the YaRN cache. The flat curve you got is *because* the labels went through YaRN.\n\nOne-liner: **physical length sets the memory bill; label magnitude sets the frequency problem. CREAM cuts the bill, YaRN solves the frequency problem — and CREAM is the very thing that raises the frequency problem to 16k.**"}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2495,"cache_read_input_tokens":323317,"output_tokens":2268,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2495,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2268,"cache_read_input_tokens":323317,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2495,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2495},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdFLs9ezbcmm92DK2Y7cd","type":"assistant","uuid":"0357cd4b-c228-44c1-a1dd-4e8dd27e623a","timestamp":"2026-07-21T14:15:55.162Z","effort":"low","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.215","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
{"parentUuid":"0357cd4b-c228-44c1-a1dd-4e8dd27e623a","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"Stop","toolUseID":"810914af-36ff-48ac-8e91-a1cbe6621748","hookEvent":"Stop","content":"","stdout":"{\"session_id\":\"7f89f0ac-da79-42e5-975f-b63bab3c65d2\",\"transcript_path\":\"/home/blu-bridge25/.claude/projects/-home-blu-bridge25-CP/7f89f0ac-da79-42e5-975f-b63bab3c65d2.jsonl\",\"cwd\":\"/home/blu-bridge25/CP\",\"prompt_id\":\"38547984-7d0f-471b-9a8f-53f1ef08bd3e\",\"permission_mode\":\"acceptEdits\",\"effort\":{\"level\":\"low\"},\"hook_event_name\":\"Stop\",\"stop_hook_active\":false,\"last_assistant_message\":\"Because the thing YaRN fixes is driven by the position **labels**, not by how many tokens are physically present. CREAM's whole point is to put **label 16000** on a token even though there are only 4k tokens in memory — and computing RoPE at position 16000 is exactly what needs YaRN.\\n\\nWalk through what the kernel does for a token CREAM labeled `m = 16000`:\\n\\nRoPE rotates each dim pair by an angle `θ_i = m · base^(-2i/d)`. Plug in `m = 16000` on a model pretrained at 4k, and the higher-frequency dims spin through **many more cycles than the model ever saw** (it only ever experienced `m ≤ 4096`). Those large-`m` rotation patterns are out-of-distribution — the classic RoPE extrapolation/aliasing failure. The token being one of only 4k physical tokens doesn't change its angle at all; the angle depends only on the **label value 16000**.\\n\\nSo there are two separate costs, driven by two separate quantities:\\n\\n| Cost | Driven by | CREAM's effect |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Attention **memory** (T×T) | physical token count (4k) | shrinks it — stays 4k×4k |\\n| RoPE **rotation frequencies** | position **label** magnitude (16k) | **pushes it to 16k** |\\n\\nCREAM attacks the first and *makes the second worse* — it deliberately creates 16k-scale rotations on a short sequence. Nothing about a 4k physical sequence tames the `m=16000` angles. **That taming is YaRN's job**: NTK-by-parts rescales the per-dim frequencies so `m=16000` lands on rotation angles the 4k-pretrained model can actually interpret (interpolated, not extrapolated).\\n\\nSo:\\n- **CREAM** = \\\"expose the model to position 16000 cheaply (no 16k attention, no 16k data).\\\"\\n- **YaRN** = \\\"make position 16000 *representable* so that exposure teaches something useful instead of garbage.\\\"\\n\\nWithout YaRN, CREAM would still feed label 16000 — but through a plain cache whose `cos/sin[16000]` are aliased. You'd be training the model on rotations it can't cleanly encode; the far-position PPL would degrade. That's the ablation I mentioned: CREAM on a plain cache vs on the YaRN cache. The flat curve you got is *because* the labels went through YaRN.\\n\\nOne-liner: **physical length sets the memory bill; label magnitude sets the frequency problem. CREAM cuts the bill, YaRN solves the frequency problem — and CREAM is the very thing that raises the frequency problem to 16k.**\",\"background_tasks\":[],\"session_crons\":[]}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"node \"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/scripts/hooks/run-with-flags.js\" \"stop:check-console-log\" \"scripts/hooks/check-console-log.js\" \"standard,strict\"","durationMs":49},"type":"attachment","uuid":"039f977e-2112-40df-b6bc-98287ba66522","timestamp":"2026-07-21T14:15:55.243Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.215","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
