{"parentUuid":"beb119ba-2b1b-4c7b-bd2d-aa9757df9b0b","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"async_hook_response","processId":"async_hook_54108","hookName":"Stop","hookEvent":"Stop","response":{},"stdout":"","stderr":"[SessionEnd] Updated session file: /home/blu-bridge25/.claude/sessions/2026-07-21-CP-session.tmp\n","exitCode":0},"type":"attachment","uuid":"b533581c-8d8e-4b0a-a030-58dfb7efb798","timestamp":"2026-07-21T14:20:43.915Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.215","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
{"parentUuid":"b533581c-8d8e-4b0a-a030-58dfb7efb798","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CdFMHATH8Y95AZD17z9LX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2328,"cache_read_input_tokens":325812,"output_tokens":3312,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2328,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3312,"cache_read_input_tokens":325812,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2328,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2328},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdFMH777uAxA3oftTXct7","type":"assistant","uuid":"abcf7eca-5431-4854-b1bf-f934fa684285","timestamp":"2026-07-21T14:21:13.498Z","effort":"low","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.215","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
{"parentUuid":"abcf7eca-5431-4854-b1bf-f934fa684285","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CdFMHATH8Y95AZD17z9LX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Great question — this is the actual mechanism. Let me make it concrete with your numbers: **4096 physical tokens**, `factor = 4`, so `scaled_max = 16384`.\n\n## The core trick: labels ≠ slots\n\nYou have 4096 physical token *slots* (`0..4095`) — that's all that ever enters attention. CREAM just assigns each slot a **position label**, and those labels are allowed to jump up to 16383. RoPE rotates each token by its *label*, so the model experiences 16k-scale positions while attention stays 4096×4096.\n\n## The head / middle / tail split\n\nCREAM cuts the 4096 slots into three blocks and labels each from a different part of the `[0, 16384)` range (using `branch 0`, `head=tail=T/3≈1365`, `middle=1366`):\n\n```\nphysical slot:  0 ......... 1364 | 1365 ....... 2730 | 2731 ....... 4095\n                └── HEAD ──┘       └── MIDDLE ──┘       └── TAIL ──┘\nlabel:          0 ......... 1364 |  a ...... a+1365  | 15019 ..... 16383\n                (true start)       (a sampled ~mid)    (the far end)\n                            ↑ big gap ↑        ↑ big gap ↑\n```\n\n- **HEAD** → labels `0..1364`: the true beginning. Always present.\n- **TAIL** → labels `15019..16383`: the extreme far end of the 16k range. Always present.\n- **MIDDLE** → a *contiguous* run of 1366 labels starting at some `a` placed **randomly in the middle** of the range (e.g. `a ≈ 8000`).\n\nLabels stay **monotonic** (head < middle < tail), so causal ordering is intact — but there are two big **jumps** between blocks.\n\n## Where the \"16k experience\" actually comes from — the gaps\n\nThe teaching signal is in those jumps. Slot 1364 (label 1364) and slot 1365 (label ~8000) are physically *adjacent* but ~6600 apart in label space. So when attention computes their interaction, RoPE must handle a **relative distance of ~6600** — a long-range rotation the model would normally only meet in a real long sequence. The head→middle and middle→tail gaps inject exactly the large relative distances that full-16k training would provide, at 4k attention cost.\n\nWithin each block the labels are contiguous, so local structure is still trained normally.\n\n## Why head and tail are fixed (continuity)\n\nAnchoring the two ends every step preserves local continuity where it matters most:\n- **Head** keeps position 0 and the sequence-start structure stable.\n- **Tail** guarantees the model sees the *extreme* far positions (near 16k) on every single step, so the very end of the range is never under-trained.\n\nThese fixed ends are the **\"Continuity\"** in CREAM's name.\n\n## Why the middle is sampled (relativity) — and why Gaussian\n\nThe middle block **moves** across training steps (`a` is re-sampled each step). Over many steps its union sweeps the whole intermediate range, so the model gradually sees *all* the interior positions and a wide variety of gap sizes. That's the **\"Relativity\"**.\n\nThe **Gaussian** (\"gaussian Middle\") is *importance sampling* on where `a` lands, centered on the middle of the range:\n\n- **Empirical fact:** LLMs are worst at using information in the **middle** of a long context (strong at the start and end — \"lost in the middle\"). \n- So CREAM deliberately **over-samples middle positions** during training to strengthen precisely that weak spot, instead of spreading exposure uniformly (which under-trains the middle, like plain PoSE/RandPos do).\n- `CP_CREAM_SIGMA` is how tightly it concentrates: small σ → heavy middle focus; large σ → approaches uniform coverage. σ=3 is the paper's balance.\n\n## One-sentence summary\n\nCREAM keeps 4096 real tokens but **relabels** them as three blocks — a true-start head, a far-end tail (both always on for continuity), and a **roaming, Gaussian-centered middle** — so the gaps between blocks force the model to practice 16k-scale relative positions (with extra practice in the middle, where models are weakest), all without ever building a 16k attention matrix."}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2328,"cache_read_input_tokens":325812,"output_tokens":3312,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2328,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3312,"cache_read_input_tokens":325812,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2328,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2328},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdFMH777uAxA3oftTXct7","type":"assistant","uuid":"6fcd5aaf-8f99-4d0e-bf0e-2ba1d7419054","timestamp":"2026-07-21T14:21:33.552Z","effort":"low","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.215","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
{"parentUuid":"6fcd5aaf-8f99-4d0e-bf0e-2ba1d7419054","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"Stop","toolUseID":"ff49bb45-83c8-4c02-99f5-871e3451e882","hookEvent":"Stop","content":"","stdout":"{\"session_id\":\"7f89f0ac-da79-42e5-975f-b63bab3c65d2\",\"transcript_path\":\"/home/blu-bridge25/.claude/projects/-home-blu-bridge25-CP/7f89f0ac-da79-42e5-975f-b63bab3c65d2.jsonl\",\"cwd\":\"/home/blu-bridge25/CP\",\"prompt_id\":\"1cc56190-7ad8-4f4b-8188-ad7377d64c5b\",\"permission_mode\":\"acceptEdits\",\"effort\":{\"level\":\"low\"},\"hook_event_name\":\"Stop\",\"stop_hook_active\":false,\"last_assistant_message\":\"Great question — this is the actual mechanism. Let me make it concrete with your numbers: **4096 physical tokens**, `factor = 4`, so `scaled_max = 16384`.\\n\\n## The core trick: labels ≠ slots\\n\\nYou have 4096 physical token *slots* (`0..4095`) — that's all that ever enters attention. CREAM just assigns each slot a **position label**, and those labels are allowed to jump up to 16383. RoPE rotates each token by its *label*, so the model experiences 16k-scale positions while attention stays 4096×4096.\\n\\n## The head / middle / tail split\\n\\nCREAM cuts the 4096 slots into three blocks and labels each from a different part of the `[0, 16384)` range (using `branch 0`, `head=tail=T/3≈1365`, `middle=1366`):\\n\\n```\\nphysical slot:  0 ......... 1364 | 1365 ....... 2730 | 2731 ....... 4095\\n                └── HEAD ──┘       └── MIDDLE ──┘       └── TAIL ──┘\\nlabel:          0 ......... 1364 |  a ...... a+1365  | 15019 ..... 16383\\n                (true start)       (a sampled ~mid)    (the far end)\\n                            ↑ big gap ↑        ↑ big gap ↑\\n```\\n\\n- **HEAD** → labels `0..1364`: the true beginning. Always present.\\n- **TAIL** → labels `15019..16383`: the extreme far end of the 16k range. Always present.\\n- **MIDDLE** → a *contiguous* run of 1366 labels starting at some `a` placed **randomly in the middle** of the range (e.g. `a ≈ 8000`).\\n\\nLabels stay **monotonic** (head < middle < tail), so causal ordering is intact — but there are two big **jumps** between blocks.\\n\\n## Where the \\\"16k experience\\\" actually comes from — the gaps\\n\\nThe teaching signal is in those jumps. Slot 1364 (label 1364) and slot 1365 (label ~8000) are physically *adjacent* but ~6600 apart in label space. So when attention computes their interaction, RoPE must handle a **relative distance of ~6600** — a long-range rotation the model would normally only meet in a real long sequence. The head→middle and middle→tail gaps inject exactly the large relative distances that full-16k training would provide, at 4k attention cost.\\n\\nWithin each block the labels are contiguous, so local structure is still trained normally.\\n\\n## Why head and tail are fixed (continuity)\\n\\nAnchoring the two ends every step preserves local continuity where it matters most:\\n- **Head** keeps position 0 and the sequence-start structure stable.\\n- **Tail** guarantees the model sees the *extreme* far positions (near 16k) on every single step, so the very end of the range is never under-trained.\\n\\nThese fixed ends are the **\\\"Continuity\\\"** in CREAM's name.\\n\\n## Why the middle is sampled (relativity) — and why Gaussian\\n\\nThe middle block **moves** across training steps (`a` is re-sampled each step). Over many steps its union sweeps the whole intermediate range, so the model gradually sees *all* the interior positions and a wide variety of gap sizes. That's the **\\\"Relativity\\\"**.\\n\\nThe **Gaussian** (\\\"gaussian Middle\\\") is *importance sampling* on where `a` lands, centered on the middle of the range:\\n\\n- **Empirical fact:** LLMs are worst at using information in the **middle** of a long context (strong at the start and end — \\\"lost in the middle\\\"). \\n- So CREAM deliberately **over-samples middle positions** during training to strengthen precisely that weak spot, instead of spreading exposure uniformly (which under-trains the middle, like plain PoSE/RandPos do).\\n- `CP_CREAM_SIGMA` is how tightly it concentrates: small σ → heavy middle focus; large σ → approaches uniform coverage. σ=3 is the paper's balance.\\n\\n## One-sentence summary\\n\\nCREAM keeps 4096 real tokens but **relabels** them as three blocks — a true-start head, a far-end tail (both always on for continuity), and a **roaming, Gaussian-centered middle** — so the gaps between blocks force the model to practice 16k-scale relative positions (with extra practice in the middle, where models are weakest), all without ever building a 16k attention matrix.\",\"background_tasks\":[],\"session_crons\":[]}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"node \"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/scripts/hooks/run-with-flags.js\" \"stop:check-console-log\" \"scripts/hooks/check-console-log.js\" \"standard,strict\"","durationMs":44},"type":"attachment","uuid":"b2071c72-3ccd-4152-9384-43c677348592","timestamp":"2026-07-21T14:21:33.712Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.215","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
