{"parentUuid":"404041f8-4b2c-4a76-8a06-351dda0ec3a0","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"async_hook_response","processId":"async_hook_55101","hookName":"Stop","hookEvent":"Stop","response":{},"stdout":"","stderr":"[ContinuousLearning] Session has 138 messages - evaluate for extractable patterns\n[ContinuousLearning] Save learned skills to: /home/blu-bridge25/.claude/skills/learned/\n","exitCode":0},"type":"attachment","uuid":"bbfb997f-66d7-45ea-ba46-6c71cba1b689","timestamp":"2026-07-21T14:51:32.568Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.215","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
{"parentUuid":"bbfb997f-66d7-45ea-ba46-6c71cba1b689","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CdFPdU5UYG5waS92aeMG5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1946,"cache_read_input_tokens":335102,"output_tokens":2936,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1946,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2936,"cache_read_input_tokens":335102,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1946,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1946},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdFPdPNhcak97dQciaLEc","type":"assistant","uuid":"c5658017-0cca-485c-ba20-99756a47413e","timestamp":"2026-07-21T14:52:04.585Z","effort":"low","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.215","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
{"parentUuid":"c5658017-0cca-485c-ba20-99756a47413e","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CdFPdU5UYG5waS92aeMG5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"No — and this is the right question to ask in a **CP codebase**. CREAM removes CP's necessity for exactly **one phase** (single-GPU context-extension *fine-tuning*), not for the long-context problem in general. They attack the same `O(L²)` cost but at different lifecycle stages, with different trade-offs.\n\n## What CREAM actually saved\nCREAM lets the *extension fine-tune* run at 4k physical attention instead of 16k. That's the one place you can get away without CP, because the long positions are faked by relabeling. Fine.\n\n## Where CP is still required\n\n**1. Inference / eval on real long sequences — the whole reason you extended.**\nAt deployment the user hands you a genuine 16k prompt. You **cannot relabel it** — there are 16k real tokens that must attend to each other → a real 16k×16k attention → may not fit on one GPU → CP. You already hit this: your own eval (Arm B, and even evaluating A) is a full 16k forward that we flagged might OOM on a single 3060. That's CP territory, and it's unavoidable — CREAM only touched *training*, never *use*.\n\n**2. CREAM is an approximation; full long-attention training isn't.**\nCREAM never has more than 4k tokens co-resident. It teaches the model to *handle large position labels*, but it never trains actual content-mixing across 16k **distinct** tokens simultaneously. Tasks that require integrating information spread across many distant real tokens (retrieval, aggregation over a long doc) may still need true long-context training — which needs CP. PPL being flat is encouraging but it's a *local* next-token metric; it doesn't fully stress long-range retrieval (that's why the CREAM paper also runs needle/retrieval evals, not just PPL).\n\n**3. Multi-GPU CREAM itself.**\nMy implementation is `CP_SIZE=1` only right now; the CP path was the deferred Phase 2. If the model is large enough that even 4k×4k + weights don't fit on one GPU, or you want a larger physical chunk (CREAM's middle-coverage improves as physical-T grows relative to scaled_max), you're back to CP — CREAM *rides on* the CP infrastructure, it doesn't remove it.\n\n**4. Base pretraining and scale.**\nCREAM is a fine-tune on a base. Large-scale pretraining, and CP×DP throughput scaling, are untouched by it.\n\n## The clean way to see it\n\n| | CREAM | Context Parallelism |\n|---|---|---|\n| Stage | extension fine-tune only | training **and** inference |\n| Method | relabel a *short* real sequence | split a *real long* sequence across GPUs |\n| Fidelity | approximation (≤4k tokens co-resident) | exact (all L tokens, real attention) |\n| Hardware | single GPU | multiple GPUs |\n| Can't do | process a real long prompt | (it can) |\n\nSo: CREAM says *\"you don't need 16k attention to teach position-handling.\"* CP says *\"you need 16k attention whenever there's genuinely 16k of content to attend over\"* — which is every real long-context inference and any training that must see the true pattern. CREAM makes the cheap fine-tune possible; CP is what lets you actually **run** the extended model on real long inputs.\n\nOne-liner: **CREAM removes the long sequence from *extension training*; it can't remove it from *using* the long context — and that's what CP is for.**"}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1946,"cache_read_input_tokens":335102,"output_tokens":2936,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1946,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2936,"cache_read_input_tokens":335102,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1946,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1946},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdFPdPNhcak97dQciaLEc","type":"assistant","uuid":"8ec75e29-1a3c-4b53-8613-95cf8d150dc2","timestamp":"2026-07-21T14:52:20.329Z","effort":"low","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.215","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
{"parentUuid":"8ec75e29-1a3c-4b53-8613-95cf8d150dc2","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"Stop","toolUseID":"f415a14d-1816-4a88-aa55-6ab4c47c53e2","hookEvent":"Stop","content":"","stdout":"{\"session_id\":\"7f89f0ac-da79-42e5-975f-b63bab3c65d2\",\"transcript_path\":\"/home/blu-bridge25/.claude/projects/-home-blu-bridge25-CP/7f89f0ac-da79-42e5-975f-b63bab3c65d2.jsonl\",\"cwd\":\"/home/blu-bridge25/CP\",\"prompt_id\":\"e8bab7c3-9c07-4263-8c2c-9f1bd12ef2c9\",\"permission_mode\":\"acceptEdits\",\"effort\":{\"level\":\"low\"},\"hook_event_name\":\"Stop\",\"stop_hook_active\":false,\"last_assistant_message\":\"No — and this is the right question to ask in a **CP codebase**. CREAM removes CP's necessity for exactly **one phase** (single-GPU context-extension *fine-tuning*), not for the long-context problem in general. They attack the same `O(L²)` cost but at different lifecycle stages, with different trade-offs.\\n\\n## What CREAM actually saved\\nCREAM lets the *extension fine-tune* run at 4k physical attention instead of 16k. That's the one place you can get away without CP, because the long positions are faked by relabeling. Fine.\\n\\n## Where CP is still required\\n\\n**1. Inference / eval on real long sequences — the whole reason you extended.**\\nAt deployment the user hands you a genuine 16k prompt. You **cannot relabel it** — there are 16k real tokens that must attend to each other → a real 16k×16k attention → may not fit on one GPU → CP. You already hit this: your own eval (Arm B, and even evaluating A) is a full 16k forward that we flagged might OOM on a single 3060. That's CP territory, and it's unavoidable — CREAM only touched *training*, never *use*.\\n\\n**2. CREAM is an approximation; full long-attention training isn't.**\\nCREAM never has more than 4k tokens co-resident. It teaches the model to *handle large position labels*, but it never trains actual content-mixing across 16k **distinct** tokens simultaneously. Tasks that require integrating information spread across many distant real tokens (retrieval, aggregation over a long doc) may still need true long-context training — which needs CP. PPL being flat is encouraging but it's a *local* next-token metric; it doesn't fully stress long-range retrieval (that's why the CREAM paper also runs needle/retrieval evals, not just PPL).\\n\\n**3. Multi-GPU CREAM itself.**\\nMy implementation is `CP_SIZE=1` only right now; the CP path was the deferred Phase 2. If the model is large enough that even 4k×4k + weights don't fit on one GPU, or you want a larger physical chunk (CREAM's middle-coverage improves as physical-T grows relative to scaled_max), you're back to CP — CREAM *rides on* the CP infrastructure, it doesn't remove it.\\n\\n**4. Base pretraining and scale.**\\nCREAM is a fine-tune on a base. Large-scale pretraining, and CP×DP throughput scaling, are untouched by it.\\n\\n## The clean way to see it\\n\\n| | CREAM | Context Parallelism |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| Stage | extension fine-tune only | training **and** inference |\\n| Method | relabel a *short* real sequence | split a *real long* sequence across GPUs |\\n| Fidelity | approximation (≤4k tokens co-resident) | exact (all L tokens, real attention) |\\n| Hardware | single GPU | multiple GPUs |\\n| Can't do | process a real long prompt | (it can) |\\n\\nSo: CREAM says *\\\"you don't need 16k attention to teach position-handling.\\\"* CP says *\\\"you need 16k attention whenever there's genuinely 16k of content to attend over\\\"* — which is every real long-context inference and any training that must see the true pattern. CREAM makes the cheap fine-tune possible; CP is what lets you actually **run** the extended model on real long inputs.\\n\\nOne-liner: **CREAM removes the long sequence from *extension training*; it can't remove it from *using* the long context — and that's what CP is for.**\",\"background_tasks\":[],\"session_crons\":[]}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"node \"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/scripts/hooks/run-with-flags.js\" \"stop:check-console-log\" \"scripts/hooks/check-console-log.js\" \"standard,strict\"","durationMs":49},"type":"attachment","uuid":"c7ff4f5e-8055-4a57-afbe-53fa48fa744f","timestamp":"2026-07-21T14:52:20.545Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.215","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
