{"parentUuid":"e02dda21-5d5d-4298-b1fc-cd99721aed26","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"async_hook_response","processId":"async_hook_56253","hookName":"Stop","hookEvent":"Stop","response":{},"stdout":"","stderr":"[SessionEnd] Updated session file: /home/blu-bridge25/.claude/sessions/2026-07-21-CP-session.tmp\n","exitCode":0},"type":"attachment","uuid":"93a6ba49-736a-43ed-b2a7-a42aba737ef3","timestamp":"2026-07-21T14:58:08.363Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.215","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
{"parentUuid":"93a6ba49-736a-43ed-b2a7-a42aba737ef3","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CdFQ8dVaFGu5MEkUzfb9s","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2962,"cache_read_input_tokens":337048,"output_tokens":2750,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2962,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2750,"cache_read_input_tokens":337048,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2962,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2962},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdFQ8ZWhsREBoiQkGgpWJ","type":"assistant","uuid":"c7697af5-9f0d-40c2-a1bc-a0c2f69457f7","timestamp":"2026-07-21T14:58:39.089Z","effort":"low","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.215","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
{"parentUuid":"c7697af5-9f0d-40c2-a1bc-a0c2f69457f7","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CdFQ8dVaFGu5MEkUzfb9s","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For the *extension-training* stage specifically — **yes, that's exactly what CREAM is for, and it does substitute for CP there.** That's the real win, and it's what your A≈B result demonstrated: you extended context by fine-tuning at 4k physical attention, never materializing the 16k attention, so CP (sequence parallelism) wasn't needed for that stage on a single GPU.\n\nBut \"not needed\" is too strong as a blanket claim even for training. Three honest caveats:\n\n**1. CREAM is an approximation of full-length training — and you need CP at least once to know it worked.**\nFull long-context continued-pretraining (with CP) trains the model on the *actual* attention pattern over all L real tokens — genuine content dependencies across the whole window. CREAM only ever co-locates ≤4k real tokens; it teaches the model to *handle large position labels*, not to integrate content across 16k distinct tokens simultaneously. For **position adaptation** (the dominant need in context extension) CREAM is enough — your flat PPL shows that. For **genuine long-range reasoning/retrieval**, full-length training can still add something CREAM can't. And critically: your evidence that CREAM works *is* the comparison against B — which is the CP full-length run. Drop CP entirely and you lose your ground truth; PPL alone doesn't certify long-range retrieval (that's why the CREAM paper also runs needle/retrieval evals).\n\n**2. CREAM removes the *sequence-length* pressure, not all parallelism.**\nIt cuts attention to `O(physical²)` = 4k². If the model + optimizer states + 4k activations still don't fit on one GPU, you still need parallelism — just **DP/FSDP/TP**, not sequence-CP. CREAM specifically retires *context* parallelism for this stage; it doesn't retire distributed training in general.\n\n**3. Multi-GPU CREAM is still on the CP stack.**\nMy implementation is `CP_SIZE=1` only for now. If you want CREAM with a larger physical chunk than one GPU holds (bigger physical-T improves middle coverage), you're back on the CP infrastructure — CREAM rides on it.\n\nSo the precise statement:\n\n> For the **sequence-length memory cost of context-extension training**, CREAM substitutes for CP — you can extend on a single GPU without ever building the long attention. CP is still needed to (a) produce the reference run that validates CREAM, (b) do genuine full-length training when the approximation isn't enough, (c) parallelize large models, and (d) all long-context inference.\n\nPractically: the compelling workflow is **CREAM for the routine, cheap extension training**, and **CP kept for the occasional full-length validation run + deployment**. Your experiment is exactly that pattern — CREAM did the cheap extension, CP (Arm B, and the 16k eval) is what proved it matched.\n\nSo it's not \"CP is obsolete for pretraining-extension.\" It's \"CREAM lets most of your extension-training compute avoid CP, while CP stays the ground-truth and the fallback when the short-physical approximation isn't sufficient.\""}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2962,"cache_read_input_tokens":337048,"output_tokens":2750,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2962,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2750,"cache_read_input_tokens":337048,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2962,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2962},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdFQ8ZWhsREBoiQkGgpWJ","type":"assistant","uuid":"07e46d9a-0cec-4f8d-8111-b039da744635","timestamp":"2026-07-21T14:58:52.489Z","effort":"low","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.215","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
{"parentUuid":"07e46d9a-0cec-4f8d-8111-b039da744635","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"Stop","toolUseID":"85011ca6-60fa-4aa7-8992-aefdb636c21f","hookEvent":"Stop","content":"","stdout":"{\"session_id\":\"7f89f0ac-da79-42e5-975f-b63bab3c65d2\",\"transcript_path\":\"/home/blu-bridge25/.claude/projects/-home-blu-bridge25-CP/7f89f0ac-da79-42e5-975f-b63bab3c65d2.jsonl\",\"cwd\":\"/home/blu-bridge25/CP\",\"prompt_id\":\"a0d7c4aa-03bf-490d-af48-f61b6d9e231b\",\"permission_mode\":\"acceptEdits\",\"effort\":{\"level\":\"low\"},\"hook_event_name\":\"Stop\",\"stop_hook_active\":false,\"last_assistant_message\":\"For the *extension-training* stage specifically — **yes, that's exactly what CREAM is for, and it does substitute for CP there.** That's the real win, and it's what your A≈B result demonstrated: you extended context by fine-tuning at 4k physical attention, never materializing the 16k attention, so CP (sequence parallelism) wasn't needed for that stage on a single GPU.\\n\\nBut \\\"not needed\\\" is too strong as a blanket claim even for training. Three honest caveats:\\n\\n**1. CREAM is an approximation of full-length training — and you need CP at least once to know it worked.**\\nFull long-context continued-pretraining (with CP) trains the model on the *actual* attention pattern over all L real tokens — genuine content dependencies across the whole window. CREAM only ever co-locates ≤4k real tokens; it teaches the model to *handle large position labels*, not to integrate content across 16k distinct tokens simultaneously. For **position adaptation** (the dominant need in context extension) CREAM is enough — your flat PPL shows that. For **genuine long-range reasoning/retrieval**, full-length training can still add something CREAM can't. And critically: your evidence that CREAM works *is* the comparison against B — which is the CP full-length run. Drop CP entirely and you lose your ground truth; PPL alone doesn't certify long-range retrieval (that's why the CREAM paper also runs needle/retrieval evals).\\n\\n**2. CREAM removes the *sequence-length* pressure, not all parallelism.**\\nIt cuts attention to `O(physical²)` = 4k². If the model + optimizer states + 4k activations still don't fit on one GPU, you still need parallelism — just **DP/FSDP/TP**, not sequence-CP. CREAM specifically retires *context* parallelism for this stage; it doesn't retire distributed training in general.\\n\\n**3. Multi-GPU CREAM is still on the CP stack.**\\nMy implementation is `CP_SIZE=1` only for now. If you want CREAM with a larger physical chunk than one GPU holds (bigger physical-T improves middle coverage), you're back on the CP infrastructure — CREAM rides on it.\\n\\nSo the precise statement:\\n\\n> For the **sequence-length memory cost of context-extension training**, CREAM substitutes for CP — you can extend on a single GPU without ever building the long attention. CP is still needed to (a) produce the reference run that validates CREAM, (b) do genuine full-length training when the approximation isn't enough, (c) parallelize large models, and (d) all long-context inference.\\n\\nPractically: the compelling workflow is **CREAM for the routine, cheap extension training**, and **CP kept for the occasional full-length validation run + deployment**. Your experiment is exactly that pattern — CREAM did the cheap extension, CP (Arm B, and the 16k eval) is what proved it matched.\\n\\nSo it's not \\\"CP is obsolete for pretraining-extension.\\\" It's \\\"CREAM lets most of your extension-training compute avoid CP, while CP stays the ground-truth and the fallback when the short-physical approximation isn't sufficient.\\\"\",\"background_tasks\":[],\"session_crons\":[]}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"node \"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/scripts/hooks/run-with-flags.js\" \"stop:check-console-log\" \"scripts/hooks/check-console-log.js\" \"standard,strict\"","durationMs":43},"type":"attachment","uuid":"46a2d4f9-1606-4373-93