{"parentUuid":"ffd0724c-6c66-4426-aede-5541db046865","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CdGcf4SF8vL4KznAJbvCp","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This is a **scaling sweep**: hold physical length at 4k, push `YARN_SCALE` up, and find the largest extension factor where the PPL curve still stays flat to the end. Here's the method.\n\n## The design\n\nFix everything except the extension factor `F` (= `YARN_SCALE` = target / 4k). For each `F`, do a cheap CREAM fine-tune (always 4k physical) then eval at the real target length `4096·F`:\n\n| F | target len | train cost | eval cost |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 2 | 8k | 4k×4k (cheap) | 8k forward |\n| 4 | 16k | 4k×4k | 16k forward |\n| 8 | 32k | 4k×4k | 32k forward |\n| 16 | 64k | 4k×4k | 64k forward |\n\n**Training is cheap at every F** — that's CREAM's point. **Eval is where the cost lands**, because you must run the real target-length forward.\n\n**Pass criterion:** first measure the base model's in-distribution PPL at 4k (the *floor*). Extension \"works at F\" if the CREAM-trained model's PPL stays **near that floor across all positions up to `4096·F`** — i.e. the verdict-table ratio `PPL≥4096 / PPL<4096 ≈ 1` and absolute PPL doesn't climb. The **largest F that stays flat is your answer.**\n\n## Commands\n\nShared config + base run number:\n```bash\nARCH=\"CP_SIZE=1 CP_N_EMBD=384 CP_N_LAYER=6 CP_N_HEAD=6 CP_N_KVHEAD=2 CP_FFN=1024 CP_WEIGHT_TYING=0 CP_GLOBAL_BATCH=131072 CP_DATA_ROOT=$HOME/Downloads\"\nBASE=<base_run_number>\n```\n\n**Train one CREAM model per factor (all 4k physical, single GPU):**\n```bash\nfor F in 2 4 8 16; do RUN=$((200+F)); (cd checkpoints_bluscriptcp && for f in blumodelcp_run${BASE}_*; do cp \"$f\" \"${f/run${BASE}/run${RUN}}\"; done); env $ARCH CP_T=4096 CP_B=4 CP_CREAM_MODE=cream CP_CREAM_SIGMA=3.0 YARN_SCALE=$F YARN_ORIG_MAXPOS=4096 CP_CKPT=1 CP_CKPT_RESUME=$RUN CP_MAX_STEPS=<base+K> CP_REWARMUP=100 CP_REWARMUP_PEAK=0.3 make CP_FUSED_ROPE=1 run-bluscript-cp NP=1; done\n```\n\n**Eval each at its target length (the expensive part):**\n```bash\nfor F in 2 4 8 16; do RUN=$((200+F)); T=$((4096*F)); env $ARCH CP_EVAL_PPL=1 CP_CREAM_MODE=off CP_T=$T CP_B=1 CP_EVAL_WINDOWS=32 YARN_SCALE=$F YARN_ORIG_MAXPOS=4096 CP_CKPT_RESUME=$RUN CP_EVAL_OUT=ppl_cream_x${F}.csv make CP_FUSED_ROPE=1 run-bluscript-cp NP=1; done\n```\n\nThen overlay `ppl_cream_x2/x4/x8/x16.csv` (+ the 4k base floor) in the notebook. The curve that finally develops an upward slope tells you F went too far.\n\n## The three things that will actually limit you\n\n1. **Eval memory, not training.** The fused kernel is flash-style so attention stays ~O(T), but the logits tensor `[B, T, V]` is O(T·V) and dominates — at V≈50k, `B=1`, that's ~3.3 GB at 16k, ~13 GB at 64k → OOM on a 12 GB 3060. So on one GPU you'll likely eval cleanly to ~16k–32k for the 48M model, and that's the ceiling of *this test rig*, not of CREAM. Beyond that you need CP for the eval forward (`NP>1`) — but `CP_EVAL_PPL` currently requires `CP_SIZE=1`, so long-target eval is exactly the deferred multi-GPU work. Keep `CP_B=1` at high F.\n\n2. **Coverage may need more steps at high F.** As `scaled_max` grows, the roaming middle block sweeps a larger range, so per-step coverage thins. If a high-F curve is bumpy rather than uniformly flat, give it more fine-tune steps before concluding it \"failed\" — under-training and true-extension-limit look different (bumps everywhere vs. a clean rise past a point).\n\n3. **Keep everything else identical across F** — same base, same fine-tune step budget, same `sigma`, same data, same `CP_EVAL_WINDOWS`. Only `YARN_SCALE` (train + eval) and eval `CP_T` change. Otherwise you can't attribute a PPL rise to the extension factor.\n\n## One honesty note\n\"Without increasing PPL\" is a **necessary but not sufficient** bar. Flat PPL says the model *tolerates* the long positions; it doesn't prove it *uses* long-range content (the needle/lost-in-the-middle evals do). So the sweep gives you the PPL-extension limit; if you care about real long-context capability, cap F where PPL is flat, then confirm with a retrieval eval at that F.\n\nWant me to drop this into a clean `cream_extension_sweep.sh` (train + eval loops, single-lines, `F` list at top) so you avoid the paste-mangling and can just run it?"}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":322064,"cache_read_input_tokens":20887,"output_tokens":5121,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":322064,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":5121,"cache_read_input_tokens":20887,"cache_creation_input_tokens":322064,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":322064},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":{"cache_miss_reason":{"type":"previous_message_not_found"}}},"requestId":"req_011CdGcf1goFAokhs2iUbhV1","type":"assistant","uuid":"4f3b48ff-23d8-4bbf-abc3-33768ee646ef","timestamp":"2026-07-22T06:24:12.197Z","effort":"low","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.216","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
