<base_run_number>\\n```\\n\\n**Train one CREAM model per factor (all 4k physical, single GPU):**\\n```bash\\nfor F in 2 4 8 16; do RUN=$((200+F)); (cd checkpoints_bluscriptcp && for f in blumodelcp_run${BASE}_*; do cp \\\"$f\\\" \\\"${f/run${BASE}/run${RUN}}\\\"; done); env $ARCH CP_T=4096 CP_B=4 CP_CREAM_MODE=cream CP_CREAM_SIGMA=3.0 YARN_SCALE=$F YARN_ORIG_MAXPOS=4096 CP_CKPT=1 CP_CKPT_RESUME=$RUN CP_MAX_STEPS=<base+K> CP_REWARMUP=100 CP_REWARMUP_PEAK=0.3 make CP_FUSED_ROPE=1 run-bluscript-cp NP=1; done\\n```\\n\\n**Eval each at its target length (the expensive part):**\\n```bash\\nfor F in 2 4 8 16; do RUN=$((200+F)); T=$((4096*F)); env $ARCH CP_EVAL_PPL=1 CP_CREAM_MODE=off CP_T=$T CP_B=1 CP_EVAL_WINDOWS=32 YARN_SCALE=$F YARN_ORIG_MAXPOS=4096 CP_CKPT_RESUME=$RUN CP_EVAL_OUT=ppl_cream_x${F}.csv make CP_FUSED_ROPE=1 run-bluscript-cp NP=1; done\\n```\\n\\nThen overlay `ppl_cream_x2/x4/x8/x16.csv` (+ the 4k base floor) in the notebook. The curve that finally develops an upward slope tells you F went too far.\\n\\n## The three things that will actually limit you\\n\\n1. **Eval memory, not training.** The fused kernel is flash-style so attention stays ~O(T), but the logits tensor `[B, T, V]` is O(T·V) and dominates — at V≈50k, `B=1`, that's ~3.3 GB at 16k, ~13 GB at 64k → OOM on a 12 GB 3060. So on one GPU you'll likely eval cleanly to ~16k–32k for the 48M model, and that's the ceiling of *this test rig*, not of CREAM. Beyond that you need CP for the eval forward (`NP>1`) — but `CP_EVAL_PPL` currently requires `CP_SIZE=1`, so long-target eval is exactly the deferred multi-GPU work. Keep `CP_B=1` at high F.\\n\\n2. **Coverage may need more steps at high F.** As `scaled_max` grows, the roaming middle block sweeps a larger range, so per-step coverage thins. If a high-F curve is bumpy rather than uniformly flat, give it more fine-tune steps before concluding it \\\"failed\\\" — under-training and true-extension-limit look different (bumps everywhere vs. a clean rise past a