{"parentUuid":"3a7af3e6-de1f-4f2a-aa0c-f99bb25920fc","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"async_hook_response","processId":"async_hook_19335","hookName":"Stop","hookEvent":"Stop","response":{},"stdout":"","stderr":"[ContinuousLearning] Session has 142 messages - evaluate for extractable patterns\n[ContinuousLearning] Save learned skills to: /home/blu-bridge25/.claude/skills/learned/\n","exitCode":0},"type":"attachment","uuid":"e8c330ef-ea10-47da-aa26-9c6cdb22b4ad","timestamp":"2026-07-22T06:29:29.292Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.216","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
{"parentUuid":"e8c330ef-ea10-47da-aa26-9c6cdb22b4ad","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CdGd9txnvzRoUwJo5zpDA","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5226,"cache_read_input_tokens":342951,"output_tokens":3409,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5226,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3409,"cache_read_input_tokens":342951,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5226,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5226},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdGd9njk143n74o8BSjoR","type":"assistant","uuid":"e82111de-032f-4875-944a-bb4634b6fcdd","timestamp":"2026-07-22T06:30:01.530Z","effort":"low","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.216","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
{"parentUuid":"e82111de-032f-4875-944a-bb4634b6fcdd","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CdGd9txnvzRoUwJo5zpDA","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Yes to all of it — let me answer each precisely, because the run-number handling is entirely under your control.\n\n## 1. Reuse the previous base run?\n**Yes.** The base is just a checkpoint on disk (`checkpoints_bluscriptcp/blumodelcp_run<BASE>_step_*.ckpt`). The A/B test didn't consume it — it copied from it. Set `BASE=<that number>` and the sweep branches from the same weights. Nothing else needed.\n\n## 2. Do run numbers have to start at a specific value? Can I change them?\n**No fixed value; fully arbitrary.** A run number is just the integer in the checkpoint filename prefix (`..._run<N>_step_<S>.ckpt`) and the training-log CSV index (`CP_Training_log<N>.csv`). My `RUN=$((200+F))` was *only* a convenient scheme to avoid colliding with numbers already on disk (base, and 101/102 from the A/B test). Change it to anything you like. The only rules:\n- **Unique per arm**, and\n- **Don't collide with a run you want to keep** (reusing an existing number mixes checkpoints and appends to that run's old log).\n- Gaps/order don't matter — the resume scan just looks up whatever numbers exist.\n\n## 3. Does the command auto-copy the base checkpoint to the new number?\n**Yes — that's exactly what this part does:**\n```bash\n(cd checkpoints_bluscriptcp && for f in blumodelcp_run${BASE}_*; do cp \"$f\" \"${f/run${BASE}/run${RUN}}\"; done)\n```\nIt **copies** (not moves) every base checkpoint file, renaming the prefix `run<BASE>` → `run<RUN>`, keeping the step suffix. So `run<RUN>` starts life as a clone of the base weights, and the base stays intact. Then `CP_CKPT_RESUME=<RUN>` loads that clone and continues, saving new checkpoints under `run<RUN>`. (This copy-to-new-number *is* the branch mechanism — the trainer has no \"load from X, write to Y\"; `CP_CKPT_RESUME` continues a run *in place*, which is why we clone first.)\n\n## 4. Can I choose that number?\n**Yes.** Easiest is an explicit map so there's no arithmetic guessing:\n\n```bash\nBASE=<base_run>\ndeclare -A RUN=( [2]=202 [4]=204 [8]=208 [16]=216 )   # <-- pick any unique numbers you want\n\nfor F in 2 4 8 16; do\n  R=${RUN[$F]}\n  (cd checkpoints_bluscriptcp && for f in blumodelcp_run${BASE}_*; do cp \"$f\" \"${f/run${BASE}/run${R}}\"; done)\n  env $ARCH CP_T=4096 CP_B=4 CP_CREAM_MODE=cream CP_CREAM_SIGMA=3.0 YARN_SCALE=$F YARN_ORIG_MAXPOS=4096 CP_CKPT=1 CP_CKPT_RESUME=$R CP_MAX_STEPS=<base+K> CP_REWARMUP=100 CP_REWARMUP_PEAK=0.3 make CP_FUSED_ROPE=1 run-bluscript-cp NP=1\ndone\n```\n\nand the eval loop uses the same `${RUN[$F]}`.\n\n## Two things to check before running\n- **Confirm the base's top step** (`ls checkpoints_bluscriptcp/blumodelcp_run<BASE>_*`) and set `CP_MAX_STEPS` above it, or the resumed arm has nothing to train.\n- **Pick RUN numbers not already on disk.** Do `ls checkpoints_bluscriptcp/ | grep -o 'run[0-9]*' | sort -u` first so you don't clobber base/101/102 or an earlier arm.\n\nWant me to write this as `cream_extension_sweep.sh` with `BASE`, the `RUN` map, the `F` list, and `CP_MAX_STEPS` all as editable variables at the top, plus a guard that refuses to run if a chosen RUN number already exists? That removes the paste-mangling and the collision risk in one shot."}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5226,"cache_read_input_tokens":342951,"output_tokens":3409,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5226,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3409,"cache_read_input_tokens":342951,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5226,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5226},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdGd9njk143n74o8BSjoR","type":"assistant","uuid":"8c9438ea-c2da-4cd6-952b-20cf5f5edb56","timestamp":"2026-07-22T06:30:16.801Z","effort":"low","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.216","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
