N}}\\\"; done)\\n```\\nIt **copies** (not moves) every base checkpoint file, renaming the prefix `run<BASE>` → `run<RUN>`, keeping the step suffix. So `run<RUN>` starts life as a clone of the base weights, and the base stays intact. Then `CP_CKPT_RESUME=<RUN>` loads that clone and continues, saving new checkpoints under `run<RUN>`. (This copy-to-new-number *is* the branch mechanism — the trainer has no \\\"load from X, write to Y\\\"; `CP_CKPT_RESUME` continues a run *in place*, which is why we clone first.)\\n\\n## 4. Can I choose that number?\\n**Yes.** Easiest is an explicit map so there's no arithmetic guessing:\\n\\n```bash\\nBASE=<base_run>\\ndeclare -A RUN=( [2]=202 [4]=204 [8]=208 [16]=216 )   # <-- pick any unique numbers you want\\n\\nfor F in 2 4 8 16; do\\n  R=${RUN[$F]}\\n  (cd checkpoints_bluscriptcp && for f in blumodelcp_run${BASE}_*; do cp \\\"$f\\\" \\\"${f/run${BASE}/run${R}}\\\"; done)\\n  env $ARCH CP_T=4096 CP_B=4 CP_CREAM_MODE=cream CP_CREAM_SIGMA=3.0 YARN_SCALE=$F YARN_ORIG_MAXPOS=4096 CP_CKPT=1 CP_CKPT_RESUME=$R CP_MAX_STEPS=<base+K> CP_REWARMUP=100 CP_REWARMUP_PEAK=0.3 make CP_FUSED_ROPE=1 run-bluscript-cp NP=1\\ndone\\n```\\n\\nand the eval loop uses the same `${RUN[$F]}`.\\n\\n## Two things to check before running\\n- **Confirm the base's top step** (`ls checkpoints_bluscriptcp/blumodelcp_run<BASE>_*`) and set `CP_MAX_STEPS` above it, or the resumed arm has nothing to train.\\n- **Pick RUN numbers not already on disk.** Do `ls checkpoints_bluscriptcp/ | grep -o 'run[0-9]*' | sort -u` first so you don't clobber base/101/102 or an earlier arm.\\n\\nWant me to write this as `cream_extension_sweep.sh` with `BASE`, the `RUN` map, the `F` list, and `CP_MAX_STEPS` all as editable variables at the top, plus a guard that refuses to run if a chosen RUN number already exists? That removes the paste-mangling and the collision risk in one shot.\",\"background_tasks\":[],\"session_crons\":[]}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"node \"${CLAUDE_PLUGI