{"parentUuid":"1c545923-f8ad-4e56-8e89-beb3b9f550ac","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"Stop","toolUseID":"ede9d3f5-b59e-45f4-8bad-f8be9a3217f7","hookEvent":"Stop","content":"","stdout":"{\"session_id\":\"7f89f0ac-da79-42e5-975f-b63bab3c65d2\",\"transcript_path\":\"/home/blu-bridge25/.claude/projects/-home-blu-bridge25-CP/7f89f0ac-da79-42e5-975f-b63bab3c65d2.jsonl\",\"cwd\":\"/home/blu-bridge25/CP\",\"prompt_id\":\"2eee14c2-20bc-4a72-b8ff-dca33e5c0876\",\"permission_mode\":\"acceptEdits\",\"effort\":{\"level\":\"low\"},\"hook_event_name\":\"Stop\",\"stop_hook_active\":false,\"last_assistant_message\":\"After the four training runs finish, you move to the **eval phase**: generate one PPL-vs-position CSV per arm (plus a base floor), then overlay them and read off the extension limit.\\n\\n## 1. (Re-)set the shared vars in your shell\\nIf this is a fresh login, re-declare them:\\n```bash\\nARCH=\\\"CP_SIZE=1 CP_N_EMBD=384 CP_N_LAYER=6 CP_N_HEAD=6 CP_N_KVHEAD=2 CP_FFN=1024 CP_WEIGHT_TYING=0 CP_GLOBAL_BATCH=131072 CP_DATA_ROOT=$HOME/Downloads\\\"\\nBASE=<run that tops at 917>\\ndeclare -A RUN=( [2]=31 [4]=32 [8]=33 [16]=34 )\\n```\\n(Adjust `CP_DATA_ROOT` if the data lives elsewhere on this mount.)\\n\\n## 2. Eval each arm at its target length\\n```bash\\nfor F in 2 4 8 16; do R=${RUN[$F]}; T=$((4096*F)); env $ARCH CP_EVAL_PPL=1 CP_CREAM_MODE=off CP_T=$T CP_B=1 CP_EVAL_WINDOWS=32 YARN_SCALE=$F YARN_ORIG_MAXPOS=4096 CP_CKPT_RESUME=$R CP_EVAL_OUT=ppl_cream_x${F}.csv make CP_FUSED_ROPE=1 run-bluscript-cp NP=1; done\\n```\\nEach writes `ppl_cream_x{2,4,8,16}.csv` and exits. Note: `CP_CREAM_MODE=off` for all (CREAM is training-only), `YARN_SCALE=F` must match how that arm was trained.\\n\\n## 3. Add the 4k in-distribution floor (reference line)\\n```bash\\nenv $ARCH CP_EVAL_PPL=1 CP_CREAM_MODE=off CP_T=4096 CP_B=1 CP_EVAL_WINDOWS=32 YARN_SCALE=1 CP_CKPT_RESUME=$BASE CP_EVAL_OUT=ppl_base_4096.csv make CP_FUSED_ROPE=1 run-bluscript-cp NP=1\\n```\\nThis is the PPL level you're trying not to exceed.\\n\\n## 4. Plot/overlay in the notebook\\nIn [graph_bluscript_cp.ipynb](graph_bluscript_cp.ipynb), set:\\n```python\\nORIG_CTX = 4096\\nCURVES = {\\n    'x2  (8k)':   'ppl_cream_x2.csv',\\n    'x4  (16k)':  'ppl_cream_x4.csv',\\n    'x8  (32k)':  'ppl_cream_x8.csv',\\n    'x16 (64k)':  'ppl_cream_x16.csv',\\n    'base 4k floor': 'ppl_base_4096.csv',\\n}\\n```\\nand run the cell.\\n\\n## 5. Read the answer\\n- **Curve stays flat to the end** (tracks the 4k floor, no upward slope past 4096) → that factor extended cleanly.\\n- **Curve starts climbing** past some position → that's where it breaks.\\n- The **verdict table** (`ratio = PPL≥4096 / PPL<4096 ≈ 1`) quantifies it: the **largest F whose ratio stays ≈1 and whose absolute PPL stays near the floor** = how far 4k-physical CREAM+YaRN extends without PPL degradation.\\n\\n## Heads-up\\n- The **32k (x8) and 64k (x16) evals are the risky ones** for single-GPU memory — the `[B,T,V]` logits dominate. If they OOM, that's the test-rig ceiling, not the model; drop to a shorter target or that's where multi-GPU eval (deferred CP path) would be needed.\\n- Keep `CP_EVAL_WINDOWS=32` identical across all arms so the curves are equally smooth/comparable.\\n- Flat PPL = tolerates the length. If you later care about *using* long range, follow up with a retrieval/needle eval at the winning F.\\n\\nWant me to drop steps 1–3 into a `cream_eval_sweep.sh` (with the `RUN` map and `BASE` at the top) so it's one command instead of a paste?\",\"background_tasks\":[],\"session_crons\":[]}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"node \"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/scripts/hooks/run-with-flags.js\" \"stop:check-console-log\" \"scripts/hooks/check-console-log.js\" \"standard,strict\"","durationMs":46},"type":"attachment","uuid":"0c4a198d-51cd-485c-aac7-48d7717d9baf","timestamp":"2026-07-22T19:12:36.105Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d9