{"parentUuid":"364e9b3b-defb-43a5-b86e-ec546cd10012","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CdJQhydToYSncJjUY5g5p","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2734,"cache_read_input_tokens":372768,"output_tokens":4077,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2734,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":4077,"cache_read_input_tokens":372768,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2734,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2734},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdJQhuRxAmeUhsF9ee71h","type":"assistant","uuid":"da923baf-96aa-40c6-ae5f-0a546dd96576","timestamp":"2026-07-23T05:08:16.680Z","effort":"low","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.216","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
{"parentUuid":"da923baf-96aa-40c6-ae5f-0a546dd96576","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CdJQhydToYSncJjUY5g5p","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For a base reference, don't eval at 4k (that's the one-point/`nan` curve you got). Eval the **base checkpoint at the same long lengths, YaRN on, CREAM off** — that's the \"naive extrapolation\" curve you overlay against the CREAM arms:\n\n```bash\nfor F in 2 4 8; do T=$((4096*F)); env $ARCH CP_EVAL_PPL=1 CP_CREAM_MODE=off CP_T=$T CP_B=1 CP_EVAL_WINDOWS=32 YARN_SCALE=$F YARN_ORIG_MAXPOS=4096 CP_CKPT_RESUME=$BASE CP_EVAL_OUT=ppl_naive_x${F}.csv make CP_FUSED_ROPE=1 run-bluscript-cp NP=1; done\n```\n\nThis gives `ppl_naive_x{2,4,8}.csv` — the base model (no long fine-tune) stretched to 8k/16k/32k via YaRN-at-inference. Overlay them with the CREAM arms; the gap between them is what the CREAM fine-tune bought.\n\n**But keep the one caveat in mind:** the base is at step **917**, your CREAM arms at **1222** (+305 steps). So a raw naive-vs-CREAM gap conflates *\"CREAM helped\"* with *\"trained 305 steps longer.\"* Two ways to handle it:\n\n**Quick (accept the caveat):** run the above, label it \"base @917, YaRN inference-only.\" Good enough to see whether the un-extended model blows up or holds.\n\n**Rigorous (isolates CREAM):** add a **matched-step, no-CREAM control** — continue-train the base to step 1222 at 4k with CREAM *off*, then eval at the long lengths. Same training budget as the arms, but the model never saw >4k positions during training:\n```bash\n# branch base -> run 40, train to 1222 at 4k, CREAM OFF (plain)\nBASE=<base>; R=40; (cd checkpoints_bluscriptcp && for f in blumodelcp_run${BASE}_*; do cp \"$f\" \"${f/run${BASE}/run${R}}\"; done); env $ARCH CP_T=4096 CP_B=2 CP_CREAM_MODE=off CP_CKPT=1 CP_CKPT_RESUME=40 CP_MAX_STEPS=1222 CP_REWARMUP=100 CP_REWARMUP_PEAK=0.3 make CP_FUSED_ROPE=1 run-bluscript-cp NP=1\n# then eval run 40 at the long lengths, YaRN on, CREAM off:\nfor F in 2 4 8; do T=$((4096*F)); env $ARCH CP_EVAL_PPL=1 CP_CREAM_MODE=off CP_T=$T CP_B=1 CP_EVAL_WINDOWS=32 YARN_SCALE=$F YARN_ORIG_MAXPOS=4096 CP_CKPT_RESUME=40 CP_EVAL_OUT=ppl_ctrl_x${F}.csv make CP_FUSED_ROPE=1 run-bluscript-cp NP=1; done\n```\nNow `ctrl` vs `cream` is apples-to-apples (same 1222 steps; only difference is CREAM's relabeling), so any gap is **purely CREAM's contribution**, not training length.\n\nMy recommendation: run the **quick naive** first (no new training) to get the reference on the plot now; if the naive curve looks close to the CREAM arms, run the **matched-step control** to settle whether CREAM added anything beyond YaRN-at-inference + extra steps.\n\nOne more: for a true absolute floor of *these* checkpoints, the `PPL<4096` values you already have (126/126/134) are the right per-arm floor — you don't need a separate base run for that."}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2734,"cache_read_input_tokens":372768,"output_tokens":4077,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2734,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":4077,"cache_read_input_tokens":372768,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2734,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2734},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdJQhuRxAmeUhsF9ee71h","type":"assistant","uuid":"7da6e189-0312-456f-a634-5ecb000a9644","timestamp":"2026-07-23T05:08:31.670Z","effort":"low","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.216","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
{"parentUuid":"7da6e189-0312-456f-a634-5ecb000a9644","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"Stop","toolUseID":"268c598e-d09e-404e-9323-79be9a486e4d","hookEvent":"Stop","content":"","stdout":"{\"session_id\":\"7f89f0ac-da79-42e5-975f-b63bab3c65d2\",\"transcript_path\":\"/home/blu-bridge25/.claude/projects/-home-blu-bridge25-CP/7f89f0ac-da79-42e5-975f-b63bab3c65d2.jsonl\",\"cwd\":\"/home/blu-bridge25/CP\",\"prompt_id\":\"7cecec92-32c0-42c9-9506-35d43576f644\",\"permission_mode\":\"acceptEdits\",\"effort\":{\"level\":\"low\"},\"hook_event_name\":\"Stop\",\"stop_hook_active\":false,\"last_assistant_message\":\"For a base reference, don't eval at 4k (that's the one-point/`nan` curve you got). Eval the **base checkpoint at the same long lengths, YaRN on, CREAM off** — that's the \\\"naive extrapolation\\\" curve you overlay against the CREAM arms:\\n\\n```bash\\nfor F in 2 4 8; do T=$((4096*F)); env $ARCH CP_EVAL_PPL=1 CP_CREAM_MODE=off CP_T=$T CP_B=1 CP_EVAL_WINDOWS=32 YARN_SCALE=$F YARN_ORIG_MAXPOS=4096 CP_CKPT_RESUME=$BASE CP_EVAL_OUT=ppl_naive_x${F}.csv make CP_FUSED_ROPE=1 run-bluscript-cp NP=1; done\\n```\\n\\nThis gives `ppl_naive_x{2,4,8}.csv` — the base model (no long fine-tune) stretched to 8k/16k/32k via YaRN-at-inference. Overlay them with the CREAM arms; the gap between them is what the CREAM fine-tune bought.\\n\\n**But keep the one caveat in mind:** the base is at step **917**, your CREAM arms at **1222** (+305 steps). So a raw naive-vs-CREAM gap conflates *\\\"CREAM helped\\\"* with *\\\"trained 305 steps longer.\\\"* Two ways to handle it:\\n\\n**Quick (accept the caveat):** run the above, label it \\\"base @917, YaRN inference-only.\\\" Good enough to see whether the un-extended model blows up or holds.\\n\\n**Rigorous (isolates CREAM):** add a **matched-step, no-CREAM control** — continue-train the base to step 1222 at 4k with CREAM *off*, then eval at the long lengths. Same training budget as the arms, but the model never saw >4k positions during training:\\n```bash\\n# branch base -> run 40, train to 1222 at 4k, CREAM OFF (plain)\\nBASE=<base>; R=40; (cd checkpoints_bluscriptcp && for f in blumodelcp_run${BASE}_*; do cp \\\"$f\\\" \\\"${f/run${BASE}/run${R}}\\\"; done); env $ARCH CP_T=4096 CP_B=2 CP_CREAM_MODE=off CP_CKPT=1 CP_CKPT_RESUME=40 CP_MAX_STEPS=1222 CP_REWARMUP=100 CP_REWARMUP_PEAK=0.3 make CP_FUSED_ROPE=1 run-bluscript-cp NP=1\\n# then eval run 40 at the long lengths, YaRN on, CREAM off:\\nfor F in 2 4 8; do T=$((4096*F)); env $ARCH CP_EVAL_PPL=1 CP_CREAM_MODE=off CP_T=$T CP_B=1 CP_EVAL_WINDOWS=32 YARN_SCALE=$F YARN_ORIG_MAXPOS=4096 CP_CKPT_RESUME=40 CP_EVAL_OUT=ppl_ctrl_x${F}.csv make CP_FUSED_ROPE=1 run-bluscript-cp NP=1; done\\n```\\nNow `ctrl` vs `cream` is apples-to-apples (same 1222 steps; only difference is CREAM's relabeling), so any gap is **purely CREAM's contribution**, not training length.\\n\\nMy recommendation: run the **quick naive** first (no new training) to get the reference on the plot now; if the naive curve looks close to the CREAM arms, run the **matched-step control** to settle whether CREAM added anything beyond YaRN-at-inference + extra steps.\\n\\nOne more: for a true absolute floor of *these* checkpoints, the `PPL<4096` values you already have (126/126/134) are the right per-arm floor — you don't need a separate base run for that.\",\"background_tasks\":[],\"session_crons\":[]}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"node \"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/scripts/hooks/run-with-flags.js\" \"stop:check-console-log\" \"scripts/hooks/check-console-log.js\" \"standard,strict\"","durationMs":47},"type":"attachment","uuid":"ce463346-a95e-4464-9316-22135dda8591","timestamp":"2026-07-23T05:08:31.753Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.216","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
