{"parentUuid":"7fce820a-7f6d-4fe3-8780-0dd9edd9c695","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CdJR7sAwbheBC3Ri26uHU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Let me walk through exactly what happens when you run that naive eval — it's the `CP_EVAL_PPL` forward-only path in [bluscriptCP.cpp](Scripts/Blutrain/bluscriptCP.cpp) (~lines 1075–1145).\n\n## The eval mechanically, step by step\n\n1. **Load weights.** `CP_CKPT_RESUME=$BASE` loads the base checkpoint into the model. No optimizer, no training — `NoGradGuard` wraps the whole thing.\n\n2. **Build the RoPE cache with YaRN.** Because `YARN_SCALE=F` and `CP_T=T`, the cos/sin cache is built YaRN-interpolated out to the long length `T`. So RoPE rotations at large positions are non-aliased.\n\n3. **Require `CP_SIZE=1`.** So `T_local == T` — the *entire* real sequence sits on one GPU, and position `t` in the output maps directly to global position `t` (no cross-rank NLL gather needed). This is why eval is single-GPU.\n\n4. **Loop over `CP_EVAL_WINDOWS` windows.** Each `val_loader.next_batch()` is a real length-`T` slice of held-out text (`[B, T]` tokens), teacher-forced. For each:\n   - `logits = model.forward(b.input)` → `[B, T, V]`. This is a **real full-`T` forward** — genuine `T×T` causal attention over actual tokens.\n   - **Per position `t`**, compute next-token negative log-likelihood:\n     ```\n     NLL[b,t] = logsumexp(logits[b,t,:]) − logits[b,t, target[b,t]]\n     ```\n     i.e. how surprised the model is by the true token at position `t`, given tokens `0..t−1` (causal).\n   - Accumulate `sum_nll[t] += NLL[b,t]` and `count[t] += 1` across all windows and batch rows.\n\n5. **Write the CSV.** After all windows: `mean_nll[t] = sum_nll[t]/count[t]`, `ppl[t] = exp(mean_nll[t])`, and it writes `pos,count,mean_nll,ppl` — one row per position `t ∈ [0,T)`. That's the PPL-vs-position curve you plot. `count[t] = CP_EVAL_WINDOWS × B` (your sample size per position).\n\n## What makes it \"naive\"\n\nMechanically the naive eval is **identical** to the CREAM-arm eval — same forward, same per-position NLL, `CP_CREAM_MODE=off`, YaRN on, same `T`. The **only** difference is *which weights* are loaded:\n\n- **Naive:** the **base** checkpoint — weights that were *never trained on positions beyond 4k*. It's fed a real long sequence with **natural** positions `0..T−1`, and asked to predict each token. It's \"naive\" because it relies purely on YaRN's frequency fix to cope with positions it never saw in training — pure extrapolation.\n- **CREAM arm:** the fine-tuned checkpoint — weights that *were* exposed to those large positions (via CREAM's relabeling during training). Same eval, better-prepared weights.\n\nSo the eval isn't doing anything special for \"naive\" vs \"CREAM\" — you're running the same measurement on two different sets of weights. That's exactly what makes the overlay a fair comparison: **identical test, the gap = what the weights learned.**\n\n## Two things to note\n\n- **CREAM is off here on purpose.** You do *not* relabel positions at eval — the model gets the true sequence `0,1,2,…,T−1`. CREAM only ever manipulated positions during *training*. (If you turned CREAM on at eval, you'd be feeding synthetic positions on short data — meaningless for this test.)\n- **YaRN is what keeps naive from exploding.** With `YARN_SCALE=1` (no YaRN), the base at 32k would produce aliased rotations past 4k and PPL would blow up hard. With YaRN on, the naive curve is \"extrapolation done as gently as possible\" — so if it *still* underperforms the CREAM arm, that gap is what training-on-long-positions bought (modulo the step-count caveat).\n\nIn one line: **the eval teacher-forces real long sequences through the loaded weights and records per-position next-token perplexity; \"naive\" just means those weights are the un-fine-tuned base, tested the exact same way as the CREAM arms.**"}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":948,"cache_read_input_tokens":380129,"output_tokens":3076,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":948,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3076,"cache_read_input_tokens":380129,"cache_creation_input_tokens":948,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":948},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdJR7p1C9AG5YStXhHKRF","type":"assistant","uuid":"7a5eb164-df97-4a77-b363-0eee80392c15","timestamp":"2026-07-23T05:13:35.856Z","effort":"low","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.216","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
