{"parentUuid":"044b9ba7-4127-4c5a-a071-7d091e292d86","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"async_hook_response","processId":"async_hook_94279","hookName":"Stop","hookEvent":"Stop","response":{},"stdout":"","stderr":"[ContinuousLearning] Session has 156 messages - evaluate for extractable patterns\n[ContinuousLearning] Save learned skills to: /home/blu-bridge25/.claude/skills/learned/\n","exitCode":0},"type":"attachment","uuid":"cad22e02-2d69-4897-bbff-63276875b522","timestamp":"2026-07-23T06:16:18.175Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.216","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
{"parentUuid":"cad22e02-2d69-4897-bbff-63276875b522","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CdJVxSDMCg4QNcHNujyCd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1892,"cache_read_input_tokens":384191,"output_tokens":3295,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1892,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3295,"cache_read_input_tokens":384191,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1892,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1892},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdJVxNqSuBtyPhyaqQr3u","type":"assistant","uuid":"1360456c-2876-4c01-b325-e70931883578","timestamp":"2026-07-23T06:16:53.847Z","effort":"low","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.216","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
{"parentUuid":"1360456c-2876-4c01-b325-e70931883578","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CdJVxSDMCg4QNcHNujyCd","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This is a clean, publishable-looking result. Let me read it out.\n\n## The headline: CREAM beats naive at every length, and the gap *grows* with extension\n\n| factor | CREAM PPL (<4k) | naive PPL (<4k) | CREAM advantage |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| x2 (8k) | 126.6 | 174.4 | **−27%** |\n| x4 (16k) | 126.0 | 174.2 | **−28%** |\n| x8 (32k) | 134.2 | 193.1 | **−30%** |\n| x16 (64k) | 137.7 | 207.8 | **−34%** |\n\nCREAM sits ~30% lower in perplexity than the un-fine-tuned base at *every* factor, and the margin **widens** from 27% → 34% as you extend further. That's exactly the signature you want: the longer the target context, the more CREAM's long-position training pays off over naive extrapolation. The 4k-physical fine-tune did real work.\n\n## Both curves are flat — so the win is in the *level*, not the shape\n\nEvery ratio is ≈1 (CREAM 1.01–1.05, naive 0.98–1.02). Neither blows up past 4096 — because **YaRN is on**, and YaRN alone already prevents the catastrophic aliasing. So \"did it extend?\" isn't answered by flatness (both are flat); it's answered by the **absolute PPL level**, and CREAM is decisively lower. (The naive ratios dipping just under 1 at x8/x16 is noise — later positions have more context, marginally easier prediction.)\n\n## Where CREAM starts to strain — your extension limit\n\nWatch the CREAM column as F grows:\n\n| factor | ratio | floor (<4k) |\n|---|---|---|\n| x2 | 1.01 | 126.6 |\n| x4 | 1.01 | 126.0 |\n| x8 | 1.02 | 134.2 |\n| x16 | **1.05** | 137.7 |\n\n- **8k / 16k: essentially free** (ratio 1.01, flat floor).\n- **32k: negligible** (2%).\n- **64k: the first real strain** — ratio 1.05, and the after-4k PPL climbs to 144.7. Still good, but this is the knee appearing.\n\n**Answer to your original question:** 4k-physical CREAM+YaRN on this 48M model extends **cleanly to ~32k (8×) with ≤2% PPL cost**, and reaches **64k (16×) with a mild ~5% cost** — the breaking point is around/just past 16×, not before.\n\n## Two caveats before you call it final\n\n1. **The +305-step confound is still open.** CREAM arms trained to step 1222, base to 917. The gap is *large* (~30%), so it's very unlikely to be all training-length — but to attribute it cleanly to CREAM, run the **matched-step control** (base continued to 1222 with CREAM off, run 40) and eval it. If `ctrl` lands between naive and CREAM but much closer to naive, CREAM owns the gap.\n2. **Flat PPL ≠ long-range *use*.** This proves the model tolerates 64k positions; it doesn't prove it retrieves across them. And confirm your val docs are actually ≥64k tokens, or the x16 tail is measuring padding. For a capability claim, add a needle/retrieval eval at 32k.\n\nNet: strong evidence that **CREAM extends context 8–16× from 4k-only training, ~30% better PPL than naive YaRN**, with the clean limit around 32k. Run the matched-step control to lock the attribution, and you've got a solid result."}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1892,"cache_read_input_tokens":384191,"output_tokens":3295,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1892,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3295,"cache_read_input_tokens":384191,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1892,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1892},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdJVxNqSuBtyPhyaqQr3u","type":"assistant","uuid":"e99cf7ea-15ff-4653-854b-9192038baddd","timestamp":"2026-07-23T06:17:08.604Z","effort":"low","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.216","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
{"parentUuid":"e99cf7ea-15ff-4653-854b-9192038baddd","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"Stop","toolUseID":"3167a591-5285-4146-ba04-9e1c8b269405","hookEvent":"Stop","content":"","stdout":"{\"session_id\":\"7f89f0ac-da79-42e5-975f-b63bab3c65d2\",\"transcript_path\":\"/home/blu-bridge25/.claude/projects/-home-blu-bridge25-CP/7f89f0ac-da79-42e5-975f-b63bab3c65d2.jsonl\",\"cwd\":\"/home/blu-bridge25/CP\",\"prompt_id\":\"411a7f6d-eba3-4bda-8d21-fb18691c8236\",\"permission_mode\":\"acceptEdits\",\"effort\":{\"level\":\"low\"},\"hook_event_name\":\"Stop\",\"stop_hook_active\":false,\"last_assistant_message\":\"This is a clean, publishable-looking result. Let me read it out.\\n\\n## The headline: CREAM beats naive at every length, and the gap *grows* with extension\\n\\n| factor | CREAM PPL (<4k) | naive PPL (<4k) | CREAM advantage |\\n|---|---|---|---|\\n| x2 (8k) | 126.6 | 174.4 | **−27%** |\\n| x4 (16k) | 126.0 | 174.2 | **−28%** |\\n| x8 (32k) | 134.2 | 193.1 | **−30%** |\\n| x16 (64k) | 137.7 | 207.8 | **−34%** |\\n\\nCREAM sits ~30% lower in perplexity than the un-fine-tuned base at *every* factor, and the margin **widens** from 27% → 34% as you extend further. That's exactly the signature you want: the longer the target context, the more CREAM's long-position training pays off over naive extrapolation. The 4k-physical fine-tune did real work.\\n\\n## Both curves are flat — so the win is in the *level*, not the shape\\n\\nEvery ratio is ≈1 (CREAM 1.01–1.05, naive 0.98–1.02). Neither blows up past 4096 — because **YaRN is on**, and YaRN alone already prevents the catastrophic aliasing. So \\\"did it extend?\\\" isn't answered by flatness (both are flat); it's answered by the **absolute PPL level**, and CREAM is decisively lower. (The naive ratios dipping just under 1 at x8/x16 is noise — later positions have more context, marginally easier prediction.)\\n\\n## Where CREAM starts to strain — your extension limit\\n\\nWatch the CREAM column as F grows:\\n\\n| factor | ratio | floor (<4k) |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| x2 | 1.01 | 126.6 |\\n| x4 | 1.01 | 126.0 |\\n| x8 | 1.02 | 134.2 |\\n| x16 | **1.05** | 137.7 |\\n\\n- **8k / 16k: essentially free** (ratio 1.01, flat floor).\\n- **32k: negligible** (2%).\\n- **64k: the first real strain** — ratio 1.05, and the after-4k PPL climbs to 144.7. Still good, but this is the knee appearing.\\n\\n**Answer to your original question:** 4k-physical CREAM+YaRN on this 48M model extends **cleanly to ~32k (8×) with ≤2% PPL cost**, and reaches **64k (16×) with a mild ~5% cost** — the breaking point is around/just past 16×, not before.\\n\\n## Two caveats before you call it final\\n\\n1. **The +305-step confound is still open.** CREAM arms trained to step 1222, base to 917. The gap is *large* (~30%), so it's very unlikely to be all training-length — but to attribute it cleanly to CREAM, run the **matched-step control** (base continued to 1222 with CREAM off, run 40) and eval it. If `ctrl` lands between naive and CREAM but much closer to naive, CREAM owns the gap.\\n2. **Flat PPL ≠ long-range *use*.** This proves the model tolerates 64k positions; it doesn't prove it retrieves across them. And confirm your val docs are actually ≥64k tokens, or the x16 tail is measuring padding. For a capability claim, add a needle/retrieval eval at 32k.\\n\\nNet: strong evidence that **CREAM extends context 8–16× from 4k-only training, ~30% better PPL than naive YaRN**, with the clean limit around 32k. Run the matched-step control to lock the attribution, and you've got a solid result.\",\"background_tasks\":[],\"session_crons\":[]}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"node \"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/scripts/hooks/run-with-flags.js\" \"stop:check-console-log\" \"scripts/hooks/check-console-log.js\" \"standard,strict\"","durationMs":47},"type":"attachment","uuid":"fa12435c-9b89-4dd1-9a42-1ce63b5d149c","timestamp":"2026-07-23T06:17:08.681Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.216","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
