predictions everywhere → higher PPL even at pos <4096.\\n\\n## The numbers confirm it\\n\\nLook at your two base measurements:\\n- base @ 4k, **plain** RoPE (`YARN_SCALE=1`): **168.6** — its true native floor.\\n- base @ long, **YaRN** (`YARN_SCALE=F`): **~174** at <4096.\\n\\nThat ~6-PPL bump (168 → 174) *is* the YaRN train/eval mismatch, measured directly. And the ~168 baseline itself is just the base being **undertrained** (step 917).\\n\\nSo naive's high <4096 PPL decomposes as:\\n- **~168** = base's inherent level (undertrained small model, 917 steps), plus\\n- **~6** = penalty for evaluating it under a YaRN cache it never adapted to.\\n\\n## Why CREAM is low there instead\\n\\nCREAM's arm was **fine-tuned with the YaRN scale-F cache** (that's what it gathers from) — so at eval with the same YaRN cache, its weights **match** what they're tested on. Matched rotations + matched temperature → low PPL, including at <4096. On top of that it had +305 training steps. Both effects push CREAM down to ~126.\\n\\n## The real takeaway\\n\\nThis is exactly *why you must fine-tune with YaRN* rather than just switch it on at inference: **YaRN is a global reparametrization the model has to adapt to**, even for short positions. A model that never trained under YaRN pays a penalty *everywhere*, not just in the extended region. The <4096 gap you're seeing (174 vs 126) is mostly \\\"base never adapted to YaRN + fewer steps,\\\" and the >4096 behavior (both flat) is YaRN doing its anti-aliasing job.\\n\\nIf you want to *see* the mismatch isolated: eval the base at **4k with `YARN_SCALE=1`** (168.6, matched) vs **4k with `YARN_SCALE=8`** (should jump to ~174+, mismatched, same positions, same weights) — the only thing changing is whether the cache matches training, and PPL rises purely from that. That's the cleanest demonstration that the <4096 penalty is RoPE mismatch, not context length.\",\"background_tasks\":[],\"session_crons\":[]}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"node \"