{"parentUuid":"cb3f0765-3c5a-4fe5-8752-561be918111f","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"async_hook_response","processId":"async_hook_2901106","hookName":"Stop","hookEvent":"Stop","response":{},"stdout":"","stderr":"[SessionEnd] Updated session file: /home/blu-bridge25/.claude/sessions/2026-07-31-CP-session.tmp\n","exitCode":0},"type":"attachment","uuid":"051c71f4-f582-4c0d-8ec1-c5ef47cb6784","timestamp":"2026-07-31T09:43:08.654Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
{"parentUuid":"051c71f4-f582-4c0d-8ec1-c5ef47cb6784","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CdZvCc9PFBAoapAGbEw8L","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":390991,"cache_read_input_tokens":21078,"output_tokens":3202,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":390991,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3202,"cache_read_input_tokens":21078,"cache_creation_input_tokens":390991,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":390991},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":{"cache_miss_reason":{"type":"unavailable"}}},"requestId":"req_011CdZvCXogmEGn2ESMoHAgF","type":"assistant","uuid":"40099157-9d08-4721-8e96-7baf596e1669","timestamp":"2026-07-31T09:43:41.266Z","effort":"low","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
{"parentUuid":"40099157-9d08-4721-8e96-7baf596e1669","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CdZvCc9PFBAoapAGbEw8L","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I understood 1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8\n\nBoth are **RoPE frequency-reparametrization** methods — same family as YaRN, i.e. pure `build_rope_cache` changes. They sit on a spectrum from \"crudest scalar\" to \"SOTA searched.\"\n\n## ABF — Adjusted Base Frequency\n\nThe simplest idea in the whole family. RoPE's rotation frequency for dimension `i` is `θ^(-2i/d)`, where `θ` is the **base** (default 10000). **ABF just raises `θ`** (e.g. 10000 → 500000 → 1e6) and continue-pretrains at the longer length.\n\n- **Why it works:** a bigger base *lengthens the wavelength* of the low-frequency dimensions, so a given position `m` produces a *smaller* rotation angle. The dims that would otherwise spin through many cycles at large `m` (the aliasing that breaks extrapolation) now stay within a range the model can represent. One scalar, applied uniformly to all dims.\n- **Who uses it:** Meta's Llama-2-Long (\"Effective Long-Context Scaling…\"), CodeLlama (`θ=1e6`).\n- **In your stack — you're already doing a form of it:** `bluscriptCP` uses `rope_theta = 500000`, which *is* ABF relative to vanilla 10000. So implementing \"ABF as an extension method\" = sweep `rope_theta` (like your `YARN_SCALE` sweep, but varying the base) and fine-tune. **Trivial** — one config knob.\n- **Limitation:** it's uniform (one scalar for every dimension), so it's cruder than YaRN, which treats high- and low-frequency dims *differently*.\n\n## LongRoPE (Microsoft, Ding et al. 2024)\n\nThe current SOTA cache-only method — it extended LLaMA/Mistral to **2 million tokens**. Its insight: the per-dimension interpolation shouldn't follow a *fixed formula* (PI = uniform, YaRN = a fixed heuristic ramp) — it should be **searched**. Three parts:\n\n1. **Evolutionary-searched non-uniform interpolation** — instead of YaRN's fixed NTK-by-parts ramp, it runs an evolutionary search over the *per-dimension* rescale factors, using perplexity on a small calibration set as the fitness function. Finds a better-fit rescaling than any hand-derived formula.\n2. **Keep initial tokens** — it also searches a rescaling that leaves the first few positions *unscaled* (initial tokens matter disproportionately, akin to attention sinks).\n3. **Progressive extension** — it doesn't jump straight to 2M: fine-tune to an intermediate length (e.g. 256k), then use the search alone to push much further *without more training* (a curriculum).\n\n- **In your stack:** the *application* is identical to YaRN — you plug per-dimension frequency factors into `build_rope_cache`. The new work is the **search harness**: generate candidate per-dim rescale vectors, evaluate each by PPL on a calibration slice (your existing `CP_EVAL_PPL` rig is literally the fitness function), evolve, bake the winner into the cache. **Medium-high effort** — the search loop is the cost; the cache machinery is reused.\n\n## Where they sit on the spectrum\n\n```\nPI            → divide all positions by s          (uniform scalar, crudest)\nABF / NTK     → change the base θ                  (uniform scalar, one knob)   ← you already do this\nYaRN          → per-dim ramp (NTK-by-parts) + temp (fixed heuristic)            ← you have this\nLongRoPE      → per-dim factors found by SEARCH    (optimized, SOTA)            ← the next rung up\n```\n\nSo the natural progression from where you are: you already have **ABF** (rope_theta=500000) and **YaRN**. **LongRoPE is the upgrade path** — same cache application, but replace YaRN's fixed heuristic with a PPL-guided search over per-dim factors. It's the method most likely to beat CREAM+YaRN on your sweep, and it reuses your eval rig as the search objective.\n\nWant me to pull the LongRoPE paper + reference repo so you can see the exact search algorithm (population, mutation on the rescale vector, the fitness eval) before deciding to implement it?"}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":390991,"cache_read_input_tokens":21078,"output_tokens":3202,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":390991,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3202,"cache_read_input_tokens":21078,"cache_creation_input_tokens":390991,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":390991},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":{"cache_miss_reason":{"type":"unavailable"}}},"requestId":"req_011CdZvCXogmEGn2ESMoHAgF","type":"assistant","uuid":"a05d7203-b2b6-4fa3-9528-f65554147ba8","timestamp":"2026-07-31T09:43:59.498Z","effort":"low","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
{"parentUuid":"a05d7203-b2b6-4fa3-9528-f65554147ba8","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"Stop","toolUseID":"6c54aa29-9f4b-484a-90c4-8f8d77f74764","hookEvent":"Stop","content":"","stdout":"{\"session_id\":\"3d0a11bb-de89-4095-8132-fc46c7f9936b\",\"transcript_path\":\"/home/blu-bridge25/.claude/projects/-home-blu-bridge25-CP/3d0a11bb-de89-4095-8132-fc46c7f9936b.jsonl\",\"cwd\":\"/home/blu-bridge25/CP\",\"prompt_id\":\"cd1346af-6130-414e-bf17-dd87bc3fa2b2\",\"permission_mode\":\"acceptEdits\",\"effort\":{\"level\":\"low\"},\"hook_event_name\":\"Stop\",\"stop_hook_active\":false,\"last_assistant_message\":\"I understood 1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8\\n\\nBoth are **RoPE frequency-reparametrization** methods — same family as YaRN, i.e. pure `build_rope_cache` changes. They sit on a spectrum from \\\"crudest scalar\\\" to \\\"SOTA searched.\\\"\\n\\n## ABF — Adjusted Base Frequency\\n\\nThe simplest idea in the whole family. RoPE's rotation frequency for dimension `i` is `θ^(-2i/d)`, where `θ` is the **base** (default 10000). **ABF just raises `θ`** (e.g. 10000 → 500000 → 1e6) and continue-pretrains at the longer length.\\n\\n- **Why it works:** a bigger base *lengthens the wavelength* of the low-frequency dimensions, so a given position `m` produces a *smaller* rotation angle. The dims that would otherwise spin through many cycles at large `m` (the aliasing that breaks extrapolation) now stay within a range the model can represent. One scalar, applied uniformly to all dims.\\n- **Who uses it:** Meta's Llama-2-Long (\\\"Effective Long-Context Scaling…\\\"), CodeLlama (`θ=1e6`).\\n- **In your stack — you're already doing a form of it:** `bluscriptCP` uses `rope_theta = 500000`, which *is* ABF relative to vanilla 10000. So implementing \\\"ABF as an extension method\\\" = sweep `rope_theta` (like your `YARN_SCALE` sweep, but varying the base) and fine-tune. **Trivial** — one config knob.\\n- **Limitation:** it's uniform (one scalar for every dimension), so it's cruder than YaRN, which treats high- and low-frequency dims *differently*.\\n\\n## LongRoPE (Microsoft, Ding et al. 2024)\\n\\nThe current SOTA cache-only method — it extended LLaMA/Mistral to **2 million tokens**. Its insight: the per-dimension interpolation shouldn't follow a *fixed formula* (PI = uniform, YaRN = a fixed heuristic ramp) — it should be **searched**. Three parts:\\n\\n1. **Evolutionary-searched non-uniform interpolation** — instead of YaRN's fixed NTK-by-parts ramp, it runs an evolutionary search over the *per-dimension* rescale factors, using perplexity on a small calibration set as the fitness function. Finds a better-fit rescaling than any hand-derived formula.\\n2. **Keep initial tokens** — it also searches a rescaling that leaves the first few positions *unscaled* (initial tokens matter disproportionately, akin to attention sinks).\\n3. **Progressive extension** — it doesn't jump straight to 2M: fine-tune to an intermediate length (e.g. 256k), then use the search alone to push much further *without more training* (a curriculum).\\n\\n- **In your stack:** the *application* is identical to YaRN — you plug per-dimension frequency factors into `build_rope_cache`. The new work is the **search harness**: generate candidate per-dim rescale vectors, evaluate each by PPL on a calibration slice (your existing `CP_EVAL_PPL` rig is literally the fitness function), evolve, bake the winner into the cache. **Medium-high effort** — the search loop is the cost; the cache machinery is reused.\\n\\n## Where they sit on the spectrum\\n\\n```\\nPI            → divide all positions by s          (uniform scalar, crudest)\\nABF / NTK     → change the base θ                  (uniform scalar, one knob)   ← you already do this\\nYaRN          → per-dim ramp (NTK-by-parts) + temp (fixed heuristic)            ← you have this\\nLongRoPE      → per-dim factors found by SEARCH    (optimized, SOTA)            ← the next rung up\\n```\\n\\nSo the natural progression from where you are: you already have **ABF** (rope_theta=500000) and **YaRN**. **LongRoPE is the upgrade path** — same cache application, but replace YaRN's fixed heuristic with a PPL-guided search over per-dim factors. It's the method most likely to beat CREAM+YaRN on your sweep, and it reuses your eval rig as the search objective.\\n\\nWant me to pull the LongRoPE paper + reference repo so you can see the exact search algorithm (population, mutation on the rescale vector, the fitness eval) before deciding to implement it?\",\"background_tasks\":[],\"session_crons\":[]}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"node \"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/scripts/hooks/run-with-flags.js\" \"stop:check-console-log\" \"scripts/hooks/check-console-log.js\" \"standard,strict\"","durationMs":42},"type":"attachment","uuid":"fe0dff1c-400a-4336-b563-28f1b8d629b7","timestamp":"2026-07-31T09:43:59.567Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
