{"parentUuid":"8d51e3f2-856a-4a13-8ecc-75f339c717df","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"async_hook_response","processId":"async_hook_2907661","hookName":"Stop","hookEvent":"Stop","response":{},"stdout":"","stderr":"[ContinuousLearning] Session has 163 messages - evaluate for extractable patterns\n[ContinuousLearning] Save learned skills to: /home/blu-bridge25/.claude/skills/learned/\n","exitCode":0},"type":"attachment","uuid":"3dbd4768-f665-49b8-8fd9-705939eb7722","timestamp":"2026-07-31T10:02:54.451Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
{"parentUuid":"3dbd4768-f665-49b8-8fd9-705939eb7722","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CdZwi4hj7KTiZbHVLpzDW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5141,"cache_read_input_tokens":412069,"output_tokens":4794,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5141,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":4794,"cache_read_input_tokens":412069,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5141,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5141},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdZwhx9acJcU3EHcMkRwZ","type":"assistant","uuid":"309d9b4e-3dde-42af-8a72-d62f300efcf0","timestamp":"2026-07-31T10:03:37.743Z","effort":"low","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
{"parentUuid":"309d9b4e-3dde-42af-8a72-d62f300efcf0","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CdZwi4hj7KTiZbHVLpzDW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I understood 1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8\n\n## 1. Why one naive curve *climbs with position* and the other stays *flat*\n\nThe two \"naive\" curves differ by **one flag: YaRN on/off.** That single difference produces the two shapes:\n\n| | Plot 1 naive (blue) | Plot 2 naive C (blue) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Setting | `YARN_SCALE=1` — **no YaRN**, plain RoPE | `YARN_SCALE=F` — **YaRN on**, no fine-tune |\n| Shape | **climbs** past 4096 | **flat** across 16k |\n| Level <4096 | **low** (matches training) | **elevated** |\n\n**Your intuition is exactly right — but only for the no-YaRN case (Plot 1).** Look at Plot 1: below ~4096 the blue (naive) tracks orange closely and even dips low around 3000–4000 — because there, plain RoPE **matches** what the model trained on, so PPL is low *within the pretraining range*. Only *after* 4096 does plain RoPE extrapolate into aliasing, and PPL **climbs with distance** (the further past 4k, the more aliased → the blow-up). That's the position-dependent failure you expected.\n\n**Plot 2's naive is elevated everywhere because YaRN is a *global* reparametrization.** When you turn YaRN on to prevent the blow-up, it rewrites the RoPE cache at **all** positions — the per-dim frequencies *and* the baked-in attention-temperature `m` change even at position 100. The base model trained on *plain* RoPE, so under the YaRN cache it has a train/eval mismatch **at every position, not just past 4096** → uniformly higher PPL. But YaRN kills the aliasing, so the curve is **flat** (no position-dependent growth), just shifted up.\n\nSo the trade is:\n- **No YaRN** → low in-range, **explodes** out-of-range (position-dependent).\n- **YaRN on** → **flat** everywhere, but **uniformly raised** (position-independent penalty from the reparametrization the base never adapted to).\n\nPlot 1 shows the aliasing failure; Plot 2 shows YaRN curing the *shape* but exposing the *adaptation gap* (which fine-tuning — A/B — then closes). They're complementary, not contradictory.\n\n## 2. LongRoPE — where it lives, the search, and training\n\n**Where:** in the **build-sincos-cache**, *not* the RoPE kernel. Exactly like YaRN — the kernel just indexes whatever cache you hand it; LongRoPE only changes *what values go into the cache*. In your code it'd live in `YARNOps.cpp` / `build_rope_cache`; `GQA_fused_*` stays untouched.\n\n**The search (this is the novel part):** YaRN uses a *fixed formula* for the per-dimension interpolation. LongRoPE **searches** for the best per-dimension rescale factors instead, via evolutionary search:\n\n1. A candidate = a vector of interpolation factors, **one per RoPE frequency/dimension** (length ~`d/2`), plus \"how many initial tokens to leave unscaled.\"\n2. For each candidate: build the cache with those factors → run **forward-only PPL** on a small calibration set at the target length. Fitness = lower PPL.\n3. Evolve: keep the best, **mutate** (perturb some dims' factors) and **crossover** (mix factors across candidates), repeat for N generations.\n4. The winning vector becomes your final cache.\n\nIt also does **progressive extension** — fine-tune to an intermediate length, then search-extend further without more training.\n\n**Training requirement — and a correction to your framing:** you have it slightly flipped. **CREAM *requires* training** (it's fundamentally a training recipe). **YaRN does *not* require training** — it works inference-only (that's your Plot 2 naive C), just better with a short fine-tune. So:\n\n- **The LongRoPE *search* is training-free** — pure forward-pass PPL evaluation, like a hyperparameter search. No gradients.\n- **The full LongRoPE recipe optionally adds a fine-tune** (the progressive stage) for best results.\n\nSo LongRoPE is a **hybrid**: training-free search + optional fine-tune. Compared to your two axes:\n\n```\nYaRN      : cache from a FIXED formula      — no training needed (better with FT)\nLongRoPE  : cache from a SEARCHED formula   — search is training-free; + optional FT\nCREAM     : positions RELABELED in training — training REQUIRED (cache is YaRN-built)\n```\n\nNote LongRoPE and CREAM are **orthogonal**: LongRoPE builds a better *cache*, CREAM is a *training recipe* — you could run CREAM fine-tuning *on top of* a LongRoPE cache.\n\n**In your rig:** implementing it = (a) let `build_rope_cache` take an explicit per-dim factor vector, and (b) an offline search harness (a Python loop that mutates the vector, calls your `CP_EVAL_PPL` binary as the fitness function, and evolves). The kernel and training loop don't change. Your existing eval *is* the search objective — that's why it's a natural next step.\n\nWant me to pull the LongRoPE paper + repo so you can see the exact evolutionary operators (population size, mutation scheme on the factor vector, the initial-token search) before you build the harness?"}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5141,"cache_read_input_tokens":412069,"output_tokens":4794,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5141,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":4794,"cache_read_input_tokens":412069,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5141,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5141},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdZwhx9acJcU3EHcMkRwZ","type":"assistant","uuid":"4954d6e7-d9cd-4e42-a762-91041b8495f9","timestamp":"2026-07-31T10:04:01.474Z","effort":"low","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
