{"parentUuid":"9d44bbb9-edb7-4122-9388-a4c34200ea70","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"async_hook_response","processId":"async_hook_3006120","hookName":"Stop","hookEvent":"Stop","response":{},"stdout":"","stderr":"[ContinuousLearning] Session has 166 messages - evaluate for extractable patterns\n[ContinuousLearning] Save learned skills to: /home/blu-bridge25/.claude/skills/learned/\n","exitCode":0},"type":"attachment","uuid":"357c9ff9-eb75-4456-ad20-10a8209bd7d6","timestamp":"2026-07-31T10:28:06.602Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
{"parentUuid":"357c9ff9-eb75-4456-ad20-10a8209bd7d6","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CdZydXiXN6VdkHvVcLWkQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3420,"cache_read_input_tokens":425704,"output_tokens":3362,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3420,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3362,"cache_read_input_tokens":425704,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3420,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3420},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdZydQj65VL3H6WLqLT7g","type":"assistant","uuid":"de65e5e3-f149-4cb7-9d6a-f43d0871d6c1","timestamp":"2026-07-31T10:28:36.827Z","effort":"low","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
{"parentUuid":"de65e5e3-f149-4cb7-9d6a-f43d0871d6c1","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CdZydXiXN6VdkHvVcLWkQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I understood 1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8\n\nThe evolutionary search is essentially **hyperparameter optimization over the RoPE interpolation factors, using perplexity as the objective**. Let me walk through the pieces.\n\n## What's being searched — the \"genome\"\n\nRoPE has `d/2` frequency components (one per dimension pair). Every extension method rescales them:\n- **PI**: one factor `s` for *all* dims (uniform).\n- **YaRN/NTK**: a *formula* gives a different factor per dim.\n- **LongRoPE**: **searches** a free per-dim vector.\n\nSo an **individual** = a vector of `d/2` rescale factors `λ = (λ₀, λ₁, …, λ_{d/2−1})`, plus one scalar `n̂` = **how many initial tokens to leave unscaled** (initial tokens matter, like attention sinks). That vector, plugged into the cache builder, fully defines a candidate RoPE reparametrization.\n\n## The fitness function\n\nFor a candidate `λ`:\n1. Build the RoPE cache using those per-dim factors.\n2. Run the model **forward-only** on a small calibration corpus at the **target length** (e.g. 64k).\n3. Measure **perplexity**. Lower PPL = fitter.\n\nNo gradients, no weight updates — just forward passes. **This is exactly your `CP_EVAL_PPL` rig**: it *is* the fitness function.\n\n## The genetic-algorithm loop\n\n```\n1. INITIALIZE population (P individuals)\n     seed with KNOWN-GOOD vectors: the PI, NTK, and YaRN factor\n     vectors + random mutations of them   ← not random from scratch\n2. EVALUATE: build cache + measure PPL for each individual\n3. repeat for N generations:\n     a. SELECT  — keep the top-k lowest-PPL individuals (elitism = \"parents\")\n     b. MUTATE  — make children by perturbing a parent's λ:\n                  for some dims, λ_i *= (1 ± small random)\n     c. CROSSOVER — make children by mixing two parents:\n                  child takes some dims' λ from A, the rest from B\n     d. form next population = elites + mutated + crossover children\n     e. EVALUATE the new individuals (PPL)\n4. RETURN the best λ ever seen → bake it into the final cache\n```\n\n## The constraints that keep the search sane\n\nTwo things prune the space so it doesn't wander:\n1. **Monotonicity** — the factors are constrained so lower-frequency dims (long-range) get interpolated *at least as much* as higher-frequency dims (local). This respects RoPE's structure and cuts the search space hugely.\n2. **Bounds** — each `λ_i` stays within a valid interpolation range (roughly between \"no scaling\" and the full PI scale), and `n̂` within a small window.\n\n## Why search beats a formula\n\nYaRN's per-dim ramp is a *hand-derived heuristic* — a proxy for the real objective. The optimal interpolation actually depends on the specific model, the data, and the target length, and has no clean closed form. The search **optimizes the true objective (PPL) directly** over the `d/2` knobs, so it finds a fit no fixed formula matches. It's the same reason learned/searched hyperparameters beat rules of thumb.\n\n## The progressive part\n\nThey don't search once for 2M tokens. They:\n1. Search + fine-tune to an intermediate length (e.g. 256k).\n2. From *that* model, re-run the search to extend further (e.g. → 2048k), fine-tuning again.\n\nEach stage: search (training-free) → short fine-tune (weight adaptation) → repeat. That curriculum is what lets it reach millions of tokens.\n\n## How it maps to your stack\n\nThree pieces, and two already exist:\n1. **`build_rope_cache` takes an explicit per-dim `λ` vector** (small change to `YARNOps.cpp` — right now it computes the factors from the YaRN formula; you'd let it accept a passed-in vector instead). *New, small.*\n2. **Fitness = your `CP_EVAL_PPL` binary** run on a calibration slice at the target length. *Already exists.*\n3. **The GA driver** — an offline Python loop that holds the population, mutates/crosses the vectors, calls the eval binary per candidate, and evolves. *New, but pure orchestration — no CUDA.*\n\nThe kernel and training loop never change. The search is entirely \"build a cache → measure PPL → mutate → repeat,\" which is why it's a natural bolt-on to what you already have.\n\nWant me to pull the LongRoPE paper so you get the exact operators — population size, mutation probability, the monotonic-init scheme, and how `n̂` is searched — before you write the GA driver?"}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3420,"cache_read_input_tokens":425704,"output_tokens":3362,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3420,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3362,"cache_read_input_tokens":425704,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3420,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3420},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdZydQj65VL3H6WLqLT7g","type":"assistant","uuid":"4d59d905-bf72-4ae4-b509-9a843d80d36e","timestamp":"2026-07-31T10:28:58.560Z","effort":"low","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
{"parentUuid":"4d59d905-bf72-4ae4-b509-9a843d80d36e","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"Stop","toolUseID":"6a3bb271-57a5-4613-be0f-21c1d2dcc54b","hookEvent":"Stop","content":"","stdout":"{\"session_id\":\"3d0a11bb-de89-4095-8132-fc46c7f9936b\",\"transcript_path\":\"/home/blu-bridge25/.claude/projects/-home-blu-bridge25-CP/3d0a11bb-de89-4095-8132-fc46c7f9936b.jsonl\",\"cwd\":\"/home/blu-bridge25/CP\",\"prompt_id\":\"89fb5ec9-6d30-4fdc-bb5a-d06240c9c4cb\",\"permission_mode\":\"acceptEdits\",\"effort\":{\"level\":\"low\"},\"hook_event_name\":\"Stop\",\"stop_hook_active\":false,\"last_assistant_message\":\"I understood 1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8\\n\\nThe evolutionary search is essentially **hyperparameter optimization over the RoPE interpolation factors, using perplexity as the objective**. Let me walk through the pieces.\\n\\n## What's being searched — the \\\"genome\\\"\\n\\nRoPE has `d/2` frequency components (one per dimension pair). Every extension method rescales them:\\n- **PI**: one factor `s` for *all* dims (uniform).\\n- **YaRN/NTK**: a *formula* gives a different factor per dim.\\n- **LongRoPE**: **searches** a free per-dim vector.\\n\\nSo an **individual** = a vector of `d/2` rescale factors `λ = (λ₀, λ₁, …, λ_{d/2−1})`, plus one scalar `n̂` = **how many initial tokens to leave unscaled** (initial tokens matter, like attention sinks). That vector, plugged into the cache builder, fully defines a candidate RoPE reparametrization.\\n\\n## The fitness function\\n\\nFor a candidate `λ`:\\n1. Build the RoPE cache using those per-dim factors.\\n2. Run the model **forward-only** on a small calibration corpus at the **target length** (e.g. 64k).\\n3. Measure **perplexity**. Lower PPL = fitter.\\n\\nNo gradients, no weight updates — just forward passes. **This is exactly your `CP_EVAL_PPL` rig**: it *is* the fitness function.\\n\\n## The genetic-algorithm loop\\n\\n```\\n1. INITIALIZE population (P individuals)\\n     seed with KNOWN-GOOD vectors: the PI, NTK, and YaRN factor\\n     vectors + random mutations of them   ← not random from scratch\\n2. EVALUATE: build cache + measure PPL for each individual\\n3. repeat for N generations:\\n     a. SELECT  — keep the top-k lowest-PPL individuals (elitism = \\\"parents\\\")\\n     b. MUTATE  — make children by perturbing a parent's λ:\\n                  for some dims, λ_i *= (1 ± small random)\\n     c. CROSSOVER — make children by mixing two parents:\\n                  child takes some dims' λ from A, the rest from B\\n     d. form next population = elites + mutated + crossover children\\n     e. EVALUATE the new individuals (PPL)\\n4. RETURN the best λ ever seen → bake it into the final cache\\n```\\n\\n## The constraints that keep the search sane\\n\\nTwo things prune the space so it doesn't wander:\\n1. **Monotonicity** — the factors are constrained so lower-frequency dims (long-range) get interpolated *at least as much* as higher-frequency dims (local). This respects RoPE's structure and cuts the search space hugely.\\n2. **Bounds** — each `λ_i` stays within a valid interpolation range (roughly between \\\"no scaling\\\" and the full PI scale), and `n̂` within a small window.\\n\\n## Why search beats a formula\\n\\nYaRN's per-dim ramp is a *hand-derived heuristic* — a proxy for the real objective. The optimal interpolation actually depends on the specific model, the data, and the target length, and has no clean closed form. The search **optimizes the true objective (PPL) directly** over the `d/2` knobs, so it finds a fit no fixed formula matches. It's the same reason learned/searched hyperparameters beat rules of thumb.\\n\\n## The progressive part\\n\\nThey don't search once for 2M tokens. They:\\n1. Search + fine-tune to an intermediate length (e.g. 256k).\\n2. From *that* model, re-run the search to extend further (e.g. → 2048k), fine-tuning again.\\n\\nEach stage: search (training-free) → short fine-tune (weight adaptation) → repeat. That curriculum is what lets it reach millions of tokens.\\n\\n## How it maps to your stack\\n\\nThree pieces, and two already exist:\\n1. **`build_rope_cache` takes an explicit per-dim `λ` vector** (small change to `YARNOps.cpp` — right now it computes the factors from the YaRN formula; you'd let it accept a passed-in vector instead). *New, small.*\\n2. **Fitness = your `CP_EVAL_PPL` binary** run on a calibration slice at the target length. *Already exists.*\\n3. **The GA driver** — an offline Python loop that holds the population, mutates/crosses the vectors, calls the eval binary per candidate, and evolves. *New, but pure orchestration — no CUDA.*\\n\\nThe kernel and training loop never change. The search is entirely \\\"build a cache → measure PPL → mutate → repeat,\\\" which is why it's a natural bolt-on to what you already have.\\n\\nWant me to pull the LongRoPE paper so you get the exact operators — population size, mutation probability, the monotonic-init scheme, and how `n̂` is searched — before you write the GA driver?\",\"background_tasks\":[],\"session_crons\":[]}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"node \"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/scripts/hooks/run-with-flags.js\" \"stop:check-console-log\" \"scripts/hooks/check-console-log.js\" \"standard,strict\"","durationMs":56},"type":"attachment","uuid":"c43c264d-8f3a-4329-aea1-3ec124d03a51","timestamp":"2026-07-31T10:28:58.651Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
