{"parentUuid":"131f7823-aaa0-4b59-80fe-bea44af12571","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"async_hook_response","processId":"async_hook_3028228","hookName":"Stop","hookEvent":"Stop","response":{},"stdout":"","stderr":"[ContinuousLearning] Session has 168 messages - evaluate for extractable patterns\n[ContinuousLearning] Save learned skills to: /home/blu-bridge25/.claude/skills/learned/\n","exitCode":0},"type":"attachment","uuid":"abdd9c3e-d7f1-4ea6-910a-84fb25838f7b","timestamp":"2026-07-31T10:39:17.157Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
{"parentUuid":"abdd9c3e-d7f1-4ea6-910a-84fb25838f7b","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CdZzUuT6iYSHBdkU8LePQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3281,"cache_read_input_tokens":432516,"output_tokens":3184,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3281,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3184,"cache_read_input_tokens":432516,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3281,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3281},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdZzUqkbmRAvUy3mEERWB","type":"assistant","uuid":"afef5c11-10a2-4048-a22b-a1de7536cdb6","timestamp":"2026-07-31T10:39:48.742Z","effort":"low","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
{"parentUuid":"afef5c11-10a2-4048-a22b-a1de7536cdb6","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CdZzUuT6iYSHBdkU8LePQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I understood 1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8\n\nThis is the conceptual heart of it, and the answer is probably not what you'd expect: **the amount each `λ_i` changes is *random*, not computed from the PPL.** That's the defining difference between evolutionary search and gradient descent.\n\n## The change is a random draw, not a calculated step\n\nIn gradient descent you'd compute `∂PPL/∂λ_i` and step *in that direction, by that magnitude* (× learning rate). Evolutionary search has **no gradient** — it never computes \"which way should `λ_i` go.\" Instead:\n\n- **How much:** drawn from a fixed distribution, e.g. `λ_i · (1 + ε)` with `ε ~ Uniform[−δ, +δ]` (or Gaussian `N(0, σ)`). The **width** `δ`/`σ` is a preset hyperparameter.\n- **Which direction:** the sign of `ε` is random too. The mutant might be better or worse — **you don't know until you build the cache and measure PPL.**\n\nSo the PPL value of the current candidate does **not** decide the perturbation. It's a blind guess.\n\n## What actually makes it improve: selection, not mutation\n\nThe trick is **\"blind variation + selective retention.\"** Mutation proposes random changes; the **fitness evaluation + selection** filters them. Beneficial random changes survive; harmful ones die. Over generations the *population* drifts toward lower PPL, even though every individual change was random.\n\nMicro-walkthrough (say each `λ` scored by PPL):\n```\nGen 0: parent PPL 40. Make 4 random mutants → PPL 42, 38, 45, 39\n        keep top 2 (38, 39); discard 42, 45\nGen 1: mutate those 2 → 37, 40, 36, 38\n        keep top 2 (36, 37)\nGen 2: mutate → 36, 35, 37, 34 ...\nbest PPL:  40 → 38 → 36 → 34 → ...\n```\nEach jitter was random ±. Selection kept the *lucky-good* ones and threw away the rest. The **cumulative direction** (downward PPL) emerges from selection across many random samples — no single step \"knew\" the right direction.\n\n## So \"by how much\" is decided by two things\n\n1. **The width of the mutation distribution** (`δ`/`σ`) — a hyperparameter you set:\n   - **Large** → big jumps, explores widely, but overshoots good solutions (many bad mutants).\n   - **Small** → fine local refinement, but slow and can get stuck.\n2. **The random draw** within that width — decides the actual amount for each mutation event.\n\n## Can the magnitude adapt? (partial \"yes\")\n\nBasic GAs use a **fixed or annealed** width — start large to explore, shrink over generations to refine (like cooling in simulated annealing). More advanced evolution strategies (e.g. **CMA-ES**) go further: they **learn** a good step size and even per-dimension correlations *from which past mutations succeeded* — so the \"how much\" is tuned by feedback, but still **indirectly** (from the population's success history), never from a gradient.\n\n## Why do it this way at all\n\nBecause `∂PPL/∂λ` through a discrete \"rebuild the cache → forward pass → measure PPL\" pipeline is awkward to compute — so you replace \"compute the right step\" with \"try many random steps and keep what works.\" It's slower per-improvement than a gradient, but it needs only a **black-box PPL evaluation** — which is exactly the cheap forward-only thing you already have.\n\nSo: **the perturbation size is a preset random distribution (optionally annealed/self-adapted); the *direction of improvement* is never computed — it's discovered by evaluating many random mutants and keeping the low-PPL survivors.**\n\nOne honest caveat: whether LongRoPE uses a fixed width, an annealing schedule, or a self-adaptive scheme is a specific implementation choice — want me to pull the paper and give you their exact mutation distribution and whether/how they anneal it?"}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3281,"cache_read_input_tokens":432516,"output_tokens":3184,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3281,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3184,"cache_read_input_tokens":432516,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3281,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3281},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdZzUqkbmRAvUy3mEERWB","type":"assistant","uuid":"19672872-94ce-4df8-a2f6-e42477a56fd7","timestamp":"2026-07-31T10:40:07.670Z","effort":"low","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
{"parentUuid":"19672872-94ce-4df8-a2f6-e42477a56fd7","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"Stop","toolUseID":"8107dd04-19ce-4c2a-8b65-b38d4b78ff0e","hookEvent":"Stop","content":"","stdout":"{\"session_id\":\"3d0a11bb-de89-4095-8132-fc46c7f9936b\",\"transcript_path\":\"/home/blu-bridge25/.claude/projects/-home-blu-bridge25-CP/3d0a11bb-de89-4095-8132-fc46c7f9936b.jsonl\",\"cwd\":\"/home/blu-bridge25/CP\",\"prompt_id\":\"41a94491-3a32-4744-bdf9-002684956d64\",\"permission_mode\":\"acceptEdits\",\"effort\":{\"level\":\"low\"},\"hook_event_name\":\"Stop\",\"stop_hook_active\":false,\"last_assistant_message\":\"I understood 1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8\\n\\nThis is the conceptual heart of it, and the answer is probably not what you'd expect: **the amount each `λ_i` changes is *random*, not computed from the PPL.** That's the defining difference between evolutionary search and gradient descent.\\n\\n## The change is a random draw, not a calculated step\\n\\nIn gradient descent you'd compute `∂PPL/∂λ_i` and step *in that direction, by that magnitude* (× learning rate). Evolutionary search has **no gradient** — it never computes \\\"which way should `λ_i` go.\\\" Instead:\\n\\n- **How much:** drawn from a fixed distribution, e.g. `λ_i · (1 + ε)` with `ε ~ Uniform[−δ, +δ]` (or Gaussian `N(0, σ)`). The **width** `δ`/`σ` is a preset hyperparameter.\\n- **Which direction:** the sign of `ε` is random too. The mutant might be better or worse — **you don't know until you build the cache and measure PPL.**\\n\\nSo the PPL value of the current candidate does **not** decide the perturbation. It's a blind guess.\\n\\n## What actually makes it improve: selection, not mutation\\n\\nThe trick is **\\\"blind variation + selective retention.\\\"** Mutation proposes random changes; the **fitness evaluation + selection** filters them. Beneficial random changes survive; harmful ones die. Over generations the *population* drifts toward lower PPL, even though every individual change was random.\\n\\nMicro-walkthrough (say each `λ` scored by PPL):\\n```\\nGen 0: parent PPL 40. Make 4 random mutants → PPL 42, 38, 45, 39\\n        keep top 2 (38, 39); discard 42, 45\\nGen 1: mutate those 2 → 37, 40, 36, 38\\n        keep top 2 (36, 37)\\nGen 2: mutate → 36, 35, 37, 34 ...\\nbest PPL:  40 → 38 → 36 → 34 → ...\\n```\\nEach jitter was random ±. Selection kept the *lucky-good* ones and threw away the rest. The **cumulative direction** (downward PPL) emerges from selection across many random samples — no single step \\\"knew\\\" the right direction.\\n\\n## So \\\"by how much\\\" is decided by two things\\n\\n1. **The width of the mutation distribution** (`δ`/`σ`) — a hyperparameter you set:\\n   - **Large** → big jumps, explores widely, but overshoots good solutions (many bad mutants).\\n   - **Small** → fine local refinement, but slow and can get stuck.\\n2. **The random draw** within that width — decides the actual amount for each mutation event.\\n\\n## Can the magnitude adapt? (partial \\\"yes\\\")\\n\\nBasic GAs use a **fixed or annealed** width — start large to explore, shrink over generations to refine (like cooling in simulated annealing). More advanced evolution strategies (e.g. **CMA-ES**) go further: they **learn** a good step size and even per-dimension correlations *from which past mutations succeeded* — so the \\\"how much\\\" is tuned by feedback, but still **indirectly** (from the population's success history), never from a gradient.\\n\\n## Why do it this way at all\\n\\nBecause `∂PPL/∂λ` through a discrete \\\"rebuild the cache → forward pass → measure PPL\\\" pipeline is awkward to compute — so you replace \\\"compute the right step\\\" with \\\"try many random steps and keep what works.\\\" It's slower per-improvement than a gradient, but it needs only a **black-box PPL evaluation** — which is exactly the cheap forward-only thing you already have.\\n\\nSo: **the perturbation size is a preset random distribution (optionally annealed/self-adapted); the *direction of improvement* is never computed — it's discovered by evaluating many random mutants and keeping the low-PPL survivors.**\\n\\nOne honest caveat: whether LongRoPE uses a fixed width, an annealing schedule, or a self-adaptive scheme is a specific implementation choice — want me to pull the paper and give you their exact mutation distribution and whether/how they anneal it?\",\"background_tasks\":[],\"session_crons\":[]}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"node \"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/scripts/hooks/run-with-flags.js\" \"stop:check-console-log\" \"scripts/hooks/check-console-log.js\" \"standard,strict\"","durationMs":49},"type":"attachment","uuid":"694dad4c-7aee-4a68-aa71-57d182eb8817","timestamp":"2026-07-31T10:40:07.758Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
