{"parentUuid":"266c26b6-cc86-47ff-a6f5-ad1e51082bab","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011Cda1Fqm6jmRxNws7U97xT","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_019ZqK8H4Bh8isg5XQNo8Yo6","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP/.mdfiles/LongRoPEexplanasion.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9223,"cache_read_input_tokens":448342,"output_tokens":119,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9223,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":119,"cache_read_input_tokens":448342,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9223,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9223},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011Cda1FmuRqytKkqCLWAFkS","type":"assistant","uuid":"f4d5b463-1314-4d99-a435-211ccedfe872","timestamp":"2026-07-31T10:49:30.751Z","effort":"low","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
{"parentUuid":"f4d5b463-1314-4d99-a435-211ccedfe872","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"453acb7c-0f8d-4fcf-81a8-ade807583aa6","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019ZqK8H4Bh8isg5XQNo8Yo6","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tThe trick is \"blind variation + selective retention.\" Mutation proposes random changes; the fitness evaluation + selection filters them. Beneficial random changes survive; harmful ones die. Over generations the population drifts toward lower PPL, even though every individual change was random.\n2\t\n3\tMicro-walkthrough (say each λ scored by PPL):\n4\t\n5\t\n6\tGen 0: parent PPL 40. Make 4 random mutants → PPL 42, 38, 45, 39\n7\t        keep top 2 (38, 39); discard 42, 45\n8\tGen 1: mutate those 2 → 37, 40, 36, 38\n9\t        keep top 2 (36, 37)\n10\tGen 2: mutate → 36, 35, 37, 34 ...\n11\tbest PPL:  40 → 38 → 36 → 34 → ...\n12\tEach jitter was random ±. Selection kept the lucky-good ones and threw away the rest. The cumulative direction (downward PPL) emerges from selection across many random samples — no single step \"knew\" the right direction.\n13\t\n14\tSo \"by how much\" is decided by two things\n15\tThe width of the mutation distribution (δ/σ) — a hyperparameter you set:\n16\tLarge → big jumps, explores widely, but overshoots good solutions (many bad mutants).\n17\tSmall → fine local refinement, but slow and can get stuck.\n18\tThe random draw within that width — decides the actual amount for each mutation event.\n19\tCan the magnitude adapt? (partial \"yes\")\n20\tBasic GAs use a fixed or annealed width — start large to explore, shrink over generations to refine (like cooling in simulated annealing). More advanced evolution strategies (e.g. CMA-ES) go further: they learn a good step size and even per-dimension correlations from which past mutations succeeded — so the \"how much\" is tuned by feedback, but still indirectly (from the population's success history), never from a gradient.\n21\t\n22\t"}]},"uuid":"065eaa1e-b25e-4439-9f04-c1f1f2b136f6","timestamp":"2026-07-31T10:49:30.760Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP/.mdfiles/LongRoPEexplanasion.md","content":"The trick is \"blind variation + selective retention.\" Mutation proposes random changes; the fitness evaluation + selection filters them. Beneficial random changes survive; harmful ones die. Over generations the population drifts toward lower PPL, even though every individual change was random.\n\nMicro-walkthrough (say each λ scored by PPL):\n\n\nGen 0: parent PPL 40. Make 4 random mutants → PPL 42, 38, 45, 39\n        keep top 2 (38, 39); discard 42, 45\nGen 1: mutate those 2 → 37, 40, 36, 38\n        keep top 2 (36, 37)\nGen 2: mutate → 36, 35, 37, 34 ...\nbest PPL:  40 → 38 → 36 → 34 → ...\nEach jitter was random ±. Selection kept the lucky-good ones and threw away the rest. The cumulative direction (downward PPL) emerges from selection across many random samples — no single step \"knew\" the right direction.\n\nSo \"by how much\" is decided by two things\nThe width of the mutation distribution (δ/σ) — a hyperparameter you set:\nLarge → big jumps, explores widely, but overshoots good solutions (many bad mutants).\nSmall → fine local refinement, but slow and can get stuck.\nThe random draw within that width — decides the actual amount for each mutation event.\nCan the magnitude adapt? (partial \"yes\")\nBasic GAs use a fixed or annealed width — start large to explore, shrink over generations to refine (like cooling in simulated annealing). More advanced evolution strategies (e.g. CMA-ES) go further: they learn a good step size and even per-dimension correlations from which past mutations succeeded — so the \"how much\" is tuned by feedback, but still indirectly (from the population's success history), never from a gradient.\n\n","numLines":22,"startLine":1,"totalLines":22}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"a3f6e462-335b-4403-8aed-6a13d8b9b9c0","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
{"parentUuid":"065eaa1e-b25e-4439-9f04-c1f1f2b136f6","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011Cda1GAMnfi84EmEYyGN7U","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":771,"cache_read_input_tokens":457565,"output_tokens":6623,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":771,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":6623,"cache_read_input_tokens":457565,"cache_creation_input_tokens":771,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":771},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011Cda1G5QtTWxkp3Q5bU1XS","type":"assistant","uuid":"d3f68074-efdd-4506-9041-cb09f1517f2c","timestamp":"2026-07-31T10:49:37.968Z","effort":"low","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
{"parentUuid":"d3f68074-efdd-4506-9041-cb09f1517f2c","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011Cda1GAMnfi84EmEYyGN7U","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01EFYvayLC1eUCWiDtjuKvVt","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP/.mdfiles/LongRoPEexplanasion.md","content":"# LongRoPE — Explanation + Implementation Plan for bluscriptCP\n\n> Source: Ding et al., *LongRoPE: Extending LLM Context Window Beyond 2 Million Tokens*\n> (Microsoft, 2024). arXiv:2402.13753 — https://arxiv.org/html/2402.13753v1\n\n---\n\n## 1. What LongRoPE is\n\nLongRoPE is a **RoPE frequency-reparametrization** method (same family as PI / NTK / YaRN):\nit only changes how the cos/sin cache is built — the attention kernel is untouched. Its thesis:\nthe optimal positional interpolation is **non-uniform** in two ways that fixed formulas (PI, NTK,\nYaRN) cannot express, so it **searches** for them instead:\n\n1. **Per-dimension non-uniformity** — each RoPE frequency dim `i` gets its own rescale factor\n   `λ_i` (not one global scale, not a fixed heuristic ramp).\n2. **Per-token (initial-token) non-uniformity** — the first `n̂` tokens keep their *original*\n   (unscaled) positions; rescaling only applies to positions `n ≥ n̂`.\n\nThe rescale formula it optimizes (Eq. 3), for position `n`, dim `i` (with `β^i = base^{2i/d}`):\n\n```\nangle(n, i) = n / β^i             if n <  n̂     (initial tokens: original RoPE)\nangle(n, i) = n / (λ_i · β^i)     if n >= n̂     (rescaled: λ_i divides the frequency)\n```\n\nLarger `λ_i` ⇒ slower rotation ⇒ more interpolation for that dim. `λ_i = 1` ⇒ no interpolation\n(pure extrapolation) for that dim.\n\n**Training requirement.** The *search* for `(λ, n̂)` is **training-free** (forward-only PPL). The\nfull recipe still uses a short **fine-tune / annealing** stage for the best results (progressive\nextension). So LongRoPE = trained annealing (like YaRN/CREAM) **plus** a training-free search that\nfinds a better-than-formula cache. LongRoPE and CREAM are **orthogonal**: LongRoPE builds a better\n*cache*; CREAM is a *training recipe*. You can run CREAM fine-tuning on top of a LongRoPE cache.\n\n---\n\n## 2. The evolutionary search (exact, from the paper)\n\n**Genome** = a vector of `d/2` per-dim factors `λ = (λ_0 … λ_{d/2-1})` **plus** `n̂`.\nFor head_dim 128 → `d/2 = 64` factors.\n\n**Search space (Table 4):**\n- `λ_i ∈ [1.0, s × 1.25]`, discretized in **steps of 0.01** (`s` = extension ratio, e.g. 8 for 4k→32k).\n- `n̂ ∈ {0, 1, 2, 4, 8, 12, 16, 20, 24, 28, 32, 64, 128, 256}`.\n\n**Constraint:** monotonic non-decreasing `λ_0 ≤ λ_1 ≤ … ≤ λ_{d/2-1}`. Candidates that violate it\nare **discarded without evaluation** (this is the key search-cost reduction).\n\n**GA parameters (Table 4 / Alg. 1):**\n\n| Param | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Population `P` | 64 |\n| Mutation size `N₁` | 16 |\n| Crossover size `N₂` | 16 |\n| Iterations `𝒯` | 40 |\n| Parents kept (top-k) | 32 |\n| Mutation probability `p` | 0.3 |\n| (>512k target: halve `P`, `N₁`, `N₂`) | — |\n\n**Initialization:** seed with **PI, NTK, YaRN** (3 individuals) + `P−3` made by mutating those three.\n\n**Mutation operator (the \"how much\" — corrected):** it is NOT a small ±jitter. For each dimension,\n**with probability `p=0.3`, RESAMPLE `λ_i` fresh from the grid** `[1.0, s×1.25]` (step 0.01); with\nprob 0.7 keep the parent's value. There is **no magnitude / annealing schedule** — the \"how much\"\nis \"anywhere in the legal grid,\" a random re-draw, and **selection** (keep the low-PPL survivors)\ndoes the optimization. \"Blind variation + selective retention.\"\n\n**Crossover:** combine two parents into a child (per-dim pick from either parent) subject to the\nmonotonicity constraint (`Crossover_with_mono_constraint`).\n\n**Fitness:** validation perplexity, forward-only — 5 random PG19 samples (≤256k) or 3 Pile-Books3\nsamples (>512k), each ≥ target length. Overall PPL per candidate (lower = fitter).\n\n**Progressive extension:**\n1. Search on the pretrained model for the intermediate target.\n2. Fine-tune to 256k (400 steps @128k, then 600 @256k).\n3. Second search on the fine-tuned model → 2048k with **no** further fine-tune.\n4. Readjust with a 4k/8k search (reduced max `λ`) to recover short-context performance.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Mapping to the bluscriptCP stack\n\nThree pieces; two already exist:\n\n| Piece | Status | Where |\n|---|---|---|\n| Cache builder that applies `(λ, n̂)` | **new (small)** | `context_parallel/YARNOps.cpp` (`build_rope_cache`) |\n| Forward-only PPL fitness | **exists** | `CP_EVAL_PPL` path in `bluscriptCP.cpp` |\n| The GA driver | **new (Python, offline)** | `Tests/bluscriptcp/longrope_search.py` |\n\nThe fused kernel (`GQA_fused_*`) and the training loop **do not change** — they index whatever\ncache they are given, exactly as with YaRN/CREAM. Every change is **additive and gated** behind a\nnew `CP_LONGROPE_FACTORS` env (mirrors the CREAM `CP_CREAM_MODE` discipline): unset ⇒ byte-identical\nto today.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Implementation plan (phased, additive)\n\n### Phase 0 — Cache builder accepts explicit `(λ, n̂)`  *(C++, additive)*\n\nAdd a new function next to `build_rope_cache` in `context_parallel/YARNOps.cpp`. Do NOT modify the\nexisting YaRN builder; this is a separate path selected only when LongRoPE is enabled.\n\n```cpp\n// NEW, additive. lambda has length head_dim/2 (one factor per freq dim); n_hat = initial\n// unscaled token count. Applies angle(n,i) = n*inv_freq_i / (n<n_hat ? 1 : lambda_i).\nTensor build_rope_cache_longrope(int64_t seq_len, int64_t head_dim, float base,\n                                 const std::vector<float>& lambda, int64_t n_hat,\n                                 DeviceIndex device) {\n    assert((int64_t)lambda.size() == head_dim/2);\n    Tensor cache(Shape{{seq_len, head_dim}}, fp32, CPU);   // build on CPU, then .to(device)\n    for (int64_t n = 0; n < seq_len; ++n) {\n        for (int64_t i = 0; i < head_dim/2; ++i) {\n            double inv_freq = pow((double)base, -2.0*i/head_dim);   // = 1/β^i\n            double scale    = (n < n_hat) ? 1.0 : (double)lambda[i];\n            double ang      = (double)n * inv_freq / scale;\n            cache[n][i]            = cos(ang);   // cos half\n            cache[n][i+head_dim/2] = sin(ang);   // sin half  (NeoX layout, matches YaRN)\n        }\n    }\n    return cache.to(device);   // grad-free\n}\n```\n\nNotes:\n- Layout MUST match the existing YaRN cache (cos in `[0,hd/2)`, sin in `[hd/2,hd)`) so the fused\n  kernel indexes it identically. Cross-check against `YARNOps.cpp` current layout.\n- LongRoPE does **not** use YaRN's attention-temperature `m` — this builder omits it.\n- `λ_i` here is the paper's factor: it **divides** the frequency. Verify sign/convention with the\n  Phase-3 parity test.\n\n### Phase 1 — Wire bluscriptCP to load `(λ, n̂)` from a file  *(C++, additive, gated)*\n\nMirror the CREAM integration in `Scripts/Blutrain/bluscriptCP.cpp`:\n\n- New env: `CP_LONGROPE_FACTORS=<path>` (a text/JSON file with the `λ` vector + `n̂`). Unset ⇒ off.\n- In the model constructor, when set, parse the file and build the cache via\n  `build_rope_cache_longrope(...)` instead of the YaRN `build_rope_cache(...)`. Keep the base\n  (YaRN) path verbatim when unset.\n- Applies to BOTH training (fine-tune) and the `CP_EVAL_PPL` eval path — same as CREAM.\n- Startup asserts: file exists, `|λ| == head_dim/2`, `λ` monotonic non-decreasing, `λ_i ∈ [1, s·1.25]`.\n\n```\nparse_longrope_file(path) -> (lambda[hd/2], n_hat)\nif CP_LONGROPE_FACTORS set:\n    cache = build_rope_cache_longrope(seq_len=CP_T, head_dim, base=rope_theta, lambda, n_hat, dev)\n    install as the RoPE cache (same slot YaRN/CREAM use)\nelse:\n    existing YaRN build_rope_cache(...)   # unchanged\n```\n\n### Phase 2 — The GA search driver  *(Python, offline, no CUDA)*\n\n`Tests/bluscriptcp/longrope_search.py`. Uses the eval binary as the fitness function.\n\n```python\n# ---- config ----\nD_HALF   = head_dim // 2          # 64 for hd=128\nS        = extension_ratio        # e.g. 8  (4k -> 32k)\nLAM_MIN, LAM_MAX, STEP = 1.0, S*1.25, 0.01\nN_HAT_SET = [0,1,2,4,8,12,16,20,24,28,32,64,128,256]\nP, N1, N2, ITERS, TOPK, PMUT = 64, 16, 16, 40, 32, 0.3\nGRID = np.arange(LAM_MIN, LAM_MAX + 1e-9, STEP)\n\ndef grid_snap(x):                      # clamp + snap to 0.01 grid\n    x = min(max(x, LAM_MIN), LAM_MAX)\n    return round(x/STEP)*STEP\n\ndef is_monotone(lam):\n    return all(lam[i] <= lam[i+1] for i in range(len(lam)-1))\n\ndef repair_monotone(lam):              # enforce non-decreasing (running max)\n    out, m = [], LAM_MIN\n    for v in lam: m = max(m, v); out.append(grid_snap(m))\n    return out\n\n# ---- seeds: PI / NTK / YaRN factor vectors as length-D_HALF lambda vectors ----\ndef seed_PI():   return [grid_snap(S)]*D_HALF                       # uniform full scale\ndef seed_NTK():  return [grid_snap(ntk_lambda(i)) for i in range(D_HALF)]\ndef seed_YaRN(): return [grid_snap(yarn_lambda(i)) for i in range(D_HALF)]\n# (ntk_lambda / yarn_lambda reuse the formulas already in YARNOps.cpp, ported to py)\n\ndef random_individual(base):           # mutate a seed to fill the population\n    lam = [ grid_snap(np.random.choice(GRID)) if random()<PMUT else base[i]\n            for i in range(D_HALF) ]\n    return repair_monotone(lam), random.choice(N_HAT_SET)\n\ndef mutate(parent_lam, parent_nhat):   # <-- the operator: RESAMPLE per dim at p=0.3\n    child = [ grid_snap(np.random.choice(GRID)) if random()<PMUT else parent_lam[i]\n              for i in range(D_HALF) ]\n    nhat  = random.choice(N_HAT_SET) if random()<PMUT else parent_nhat\n    return repair_monotone(child), nhat\n\ndef crossover(a, b):                    # per-dim pick from either parent, then repair\n    lam  = [ a.lam[i] if random()<0.5 else b.lam[i] for i in range(D_HALF) ]\n    nhat = a.nhat if random()<0.5 else b.nhat\n    return repair_monotone(lam), nhat\n\n# ---- fitness: write factors -> run CP_EVAL_PPL -> read overall PPL from CSV ----\ndef fitness(lam, nhat):\n    write_factors_file(\"cand.txt\", lam, nhat)\n    run(f\"CP_SIZE=1 CP_EVAL_PPL=1 CP_LONGROPE_FACTORS=cand.txt \"\n        f\"CP_T={TARGET_T} CP_B=1 CP_EVAL_WINDOWS={CALIB_WINDOWS} \"\n        f\"CP_CKPT_RESUME={CKPT} CP_EVAL_OUT=cand.csv make CP_FUSED_ROPE=1 run-bluscript-cp NP=1\")\n    df = read_csv(\"cand.csv\"); df = df[df[\"count\"] > 0]\n    overall_nll = (df[\"mean_nll\"]*df[\"count\"]).sum() / df[\"count\"].sum()\n    return math.exp(overall_nll)        # lower = better\n\n# ---- GA loop ----\npop  = [ (seed_PI(), N0), (seed_NTK(), N0), (seed_YaRN(), N0) ]\npop += [ random_individual(random.choice([seed_PI(),seed_NTK(),seed_YaRN()])) for _ in range(P-3) ]\nscored = [ (fitness(l,n), l, n) for (l,n) in pop ]\n\nfor it in range(ITERS):\n    scored.sort(key=lambda x: x[0])            # ascending PPL\n    parents = scored[:TOPK]\n    children = []\n    for _ in range(N1):                        # mutation\n        _, l, n = random.choice(parents); children.append(mutate(l, n))\n    for _ in range(N2):                        # crossover\n        pa, pb = random.sample(parents, 2)\n        children.append(crossover(pa, pb))\n    children = [ (l,n) for (l,n) in children if is_monotone(l) ]   # cheap assert after repair\n    scored += [ (fitness(l,n), l, n) for (l,n) in children ]\n    scored = sorted(scored, key=lambda x: x[0])[:P]                # keep best P\n    log(f\"iter {it}: best PPL {scored[0][0]:.3f}\")\n\nbest_ppl, best_lam, best_nhat = scored[0]\nwrite_factors_file(\"longrope_best.txt\", best_lam, best_nhat)\n```\n\nDetails / decisions:\n- **Fitness = overall PPL** aggregated from the per-position CSV: `exp( Σ nll·count / Σ count )`.\n- **Calibration windows** small (paper uses 5 samples) → each fitness call is a few forward passes.\n  40 iters × (16 mut + 16 cross) ≈ ~1300 evals worst-case; monotonicity pruning + memoization cut this.\n- **Memoize** identical `(λ, n̂)` tuples to skip re-evaluating duplicates.\n- `repair_monotone` makes crossover/mutation always produce a legal vector, so the explicit\n  `is_monotone` filter becomes a cheap assertion.\n\n### Phase 3 — Parity + progressive extension  *(reuse existing infra)*\n\n1. **Parity test** `Tests/cp_rope_longrope_parity.cpp`: with `λ_i = 1 ∀i, n̂ = 0`, the LongRoPE\n   cache must equal plain RoPE bit-for-bit; with `λ_i = s ∀i, n̂ = 0` it must equal PI\n   (positions/s). Confirms sign/convention and the `n̂` threshold. Mirror\n   `Tests/cream_cache_gather_parity.cpp`.\n2. **Progressive**: search at the target → **fine-tune** with the found cache using the existing\n   YaRN/CREAM fine-tune + `CP_REWARMUP` path (point `CP_LONGROPE_FACTORS` at `longrope_best.txt`)\n   → optionally re-search on the fine-tuned model for a larger target.\n\n### Phase 4 — Comparison eval\n\nRun the same PPL-vs-position sweep you used for CREAM, adding a LongRoPE arm:\n`{ naive-noYaRN, naive-YaRN, YaRN-LCE, CREAM, LongRoPE }` at each target length. Overlay in\n`graph_bluscript_cp.ipynb`. Expect LongRoPE ≤ YaRN on the flat floor.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Non-destructive invariant\n\nEverything is gated behind `CP_LONGROPE_FACTORS` (and the new C++ function is separate from the\nYaRN builder). Unset ⇒ the program is **byte-identical** to today — same discipline as the CREAM\nintegration. No existing path is modified; only new functions, a new env, and\n`if (longrope_on) {...} else {<existing>}` branches.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Effort summary\n\n| Phase | Effort | Risk |\n|---|---|---|\n| 0 cache builder | small (C++) | low (parity-tested) |\n| 1 wiring | small (mirror CREAM) | low |\n| 2 GA driver | medium (Python orchestration) | low (no CUDA) |\n| 3 parity + progressive | small (reuse) | low |\n| 4 comparison | trivial (existing rig) | — |\n\nThe novel work is the **GA driver**; the cache math is a parity-checkable formula; the fitness and\nfine-tune are already built. LongRoPE is the natural next rung above the existing YaRN + CREAM.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":771,"cache_read_input_tokens":457565,"output_tokens":6623,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":771,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":6623,"cache_read_input_tokens":457565,"cache_creation_input_tokens":771,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":771},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011Cda1G5QtTWxkp3Q5bU1XS","type":"assistant","uuid":"2aeaa414-d053-4afe-8efe-ba8f30c844d8","timestamp":"2026-07-31T10:50:38.418Z","effort":"low","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
{"parentUuid":"2aeaa414-d053-4afe-8efe-ba8f30c844d8","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"453acb7c-0f8d-4fcf-81a8-ade807583aa6","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EFYvayLC1eUCWiDtjuKvVt","type":"tool_result","content":"The file /home/blu-bridge25/CP/.mdfiles/LongRoPEexplanasion.md has been updated successfully. (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"8958e542-af9c-4cfd-a3f2-0e9b64066237","timestamp":"2026-07-31T10:50:39.484Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"update","filePath":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP/.mdfiles/LongRoPEexplanasion.md","content":"# LongRoPE — Explanation + Implementation Plan for bluscriptCP\n\n> Source: Ding et al., *LongRoPE: Extending LLM Context Window Beyond 2 Million Tokens*\n> (Microsoft, 2024). arXiv:2402.13753 — https://arxiv.org/html/2402.13753v1\n\n---\n\n## 1. What LongRoPE is\n\nLongRoPE is a **RoPE frequency-reparametrization** method (same family as PI / NTK / YaRN):\nit only changes how the cos/sin cache is built — the attention kernel is untouched. Its thesis:\nthe optimal positional interpolation is **non-uniform** in two ways that fixed formulas (PI, NTK,\nYaRN) cannot express, so it **searches** for them instead:\n\n1. **Per-dimension non-uniformity** — each RoPE frequency dim `i` gets its own rescale factor\n   `λ_i` (not one global scale, not a fixed heuristic ramp).\n2. **Per-token (initial-token) non-uniformity** — the first `n̂` tokens keep their *original*\n   (unscaled) positions; rescaling only applies to positions `n ≥ n̂`.\n\nThe rescale formula it optimizes (Eq. 3), for position `n`, dim `i` (with `β^i = base^{2i/d}`):\n\n```\nangle(n, i) = n / β^i             if n <  n̂     (initial tokens: original RoPE)\nangle(n, i) = n / (λ_i · β^i)     if n >= n̂     (rescaled: λ_i divides the frequency)\n```\n\nLarger `λ_i` ⇒ slower rotation ⇒ more interpolation for that dim. `λ_i = 1` ⇒ no interpolation\n(pure extrapolation) for that dim.\n\n**Training requirement.** The *search* for `(λ, n̂)` is **training-free** (forward-only PPL). The\nfull recipe still uses a short **fine-tune / annealing** stage for the best results (progressive\nextension). So LongRoPE = trained annealing (like YaRN/CREAM) **plus** a training-free search that\nfinds a better-than-formula cache. LongRoPE and CREAM are **orthogonal**: LongRoPE builds a better\n*cache*; CREAM is a *training recipe*. You can run CREAM fine-tuning on top of a LongRoPE cache.\n\n---\n\n## 2. The evolutionary search (exact, from the paper)\n\n**Genome** = a vector of `d/2` per-dim factors `λ = (λ_0 … λ_{d/2-1})` **plus** `n̂`.\nFor head_dim 128 → `d/2 = 64` factors.\n\n**Search space (Table 4):**\n- `λ_i ∈ [1.0, s × 1.25]`, discretized in **steps of 0.01** (`s` = extension ratio, e.g. 8 for 4k→32k).\n- `n̂ ∈ {0, 1, 2, 4, 8, 12, 16, 20, 24, 28, 32, 64, 128, 256}`.\n\n**Constraint:** monotonic non-decreasing `λ_0 ≤ λ_1 ≤ … ≤ λ_{d/2-1}`. Candidates that violate it\nare **discarded without evaluation** (this is the key search-cost reduction).\n\n**GA parameters (Table 4 / Alg. 1):**\n\n| Param | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Population `P` | 64 |\n| Mutation size `N₁` | 16 |\n| Crossover size `N₂` | 16 |\n| Iterations `𝒯` | 40 |\n| Parents kept (top-k) | 32 |\n| Mutation probability `p` | 0.3 |\n| (>512k target: halve `P`, `N₁`, `N₂`) | — |\n\n**Initialization:** seed with **PI, NTK, YaRN** (3 individuals) + `P−3` made by mutating those three.\n\n**Mutation operator (the \"how much\" — corrected):** it is NOT a small ±jitter. For each dimension,\n**with probability `p=0.3`, RESAMPLE `λ_i` fresh from the grid** `[1.0, s×1.25]` (step 0.01); with\nprob 0.7 keep the parent's value. There is **no magnitude / annealing schedule** — the \"how much\"\nis \"anywhere in the legal grid,\" a random re-draw, and **selection** (keep the low-PPL survivors)\ndoes the optimization. \"Blind variation + selective retention.\"\n\n**Crossover:** combine two parents into a child (per-dim pick from either parent) subject to the\nmonotonicity constraint (`Crossover_with_mono_constraint`).\n\n**Fitness:** validation perplexity, forward-only — 5 random PG19 samples (≤256k) or 3 Pile-Books3\nsamples (>512k), each ≥ target length. Overall PPL per candidate (lower = fitter).\n\n**Progressive extension:**\n1. Search on the pretrained model for the intermediate target.\n2. Fine-tune to 256k (400 steps @128k, then 600 @256k).\n3. Second search on the fine-tuned model → 2048k with **no** further fine-tune.\n4. Readjust with a 4k/8k search (reduced max `λ`) to recover short-context performance.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Mapping to the bluscriptCP stack\n\nThree pieces; two already exist:\n\n| Piece | Status | Where |\n|---|---|---|\n| Cache builder that applies `(λ, n̂)` | **new (small)** | `context_parallel/YARNOps.cpp` (`build_rope_cache`) |\n| Forward-only PPL fitness | **exists** | `CP_EVAL_PPL` path in `bluscriptCP.cpp` |\n| The GA driver | **new (Python, offline)** | `Tests/bluscriptcp/longrope_search.py` |\n\nThe fused kernel (`GQA_fused_*`) and the training loop **do not change** — they index whatever\ncache they are given, exactly as with YaRN/CREAM. Every change is **additive and gated** behind a\nnew `CP_LONGROPE_FACTORS` env (mirrors the CREAM `CP_CREAM_MODE` discipline): unset ⇒ byte-identical\nto today.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Implementation plan (phased, additive)\n\n### Phase 0 — Cache builder accepts explicit `(λ, n̂)`  *(C++, additive)*\n\nAdd a new function next to `build_rope_cache` in `context_parallel/YARNOps.cpp`. Do NOT modify the\nexisting YaRN builder; this is a separate path selected only when LongRoPE is enabled.\n\n```cpp\n// NEW, additive. lambda has length head_dim/2 (one factor per freq dim); n_hat = initial\n// unscaled token count. Applies angle(n,i) = n*inv_freq_i / (n<n_hat ? 1 : lambda_i).\nTensor build_rope_cache_longrope(int64_t seq_len, int64_t head_dim, float base,\n                                 const std::vector<float>& lambda, int64_t n_hat,\n                                 DeviceIndex device) {\n    assert((int64_t)lambda.size() == head_dim/2);\n    Tensor cache(Shape{{seq_len, head_dim}}, fp32, CPU);   // build on CPU, then .to(device)\n    for (int64_t n = 0; n < seq_len; ++n) {\n        for (int64_t i = 0; i < head_dim/2; ++i) {\n            double inv_freq = pow((double)base, -2.0*i/head_dim);   // = 1/β^i\n            double scale    = (n < n_hat) ? 1.0 : (double)lambda[i];\n            double ang      = (double)n * inv_freq / scale;\n            cache[n][i]            = cos(ang);   // cos half\n            cache[n][i+head_dim/2] = sin(ang);   // sin half  (NeoX layout, matches YaRN)\n        }\n    }\n    return cache.to(device);   // grad-free\n}\n```\n\nNotes:\n- Layout MUST match the existing YaRN cache (cos in `[0,hd/2)`, sin in `[hd/2,hd)`) so the fused\n  kernel indexes it identically. Cross-check against `YARNOps.cpp` current layout.\n- LongRoPE does **not** use YaRN's attention-temperature `m` — this builder omits it.\n- `λ_i` here is the paper's factor: it **divides** the frequency. Verify sign/convention with the\n  Phase-3 parity test.\n\n### Phase 1 — Wire bluscriptCP to load `(λ, n̂)` from a file  *(C++, additive, gated)*\n\nMirror the CREAM integration in `Scripts/Blutrain/bluscriptCP.cpp`:\n\n- New env: `CP_LONGROPE_FACTORS=<path>` (a text/JSON file with the `λ` vector + `n̂`). Unset ⇒ off.\n- In the model constructor, when set, parse the file and build the cache via\n  `build_rope_cache_longrope(...)` instead of the YaRN `build_rope_cache(...)`. Keep the base\n  (YaRN) path verbatim when unset.\n- Applies to BOTH training (fine-tune) and the `CP_EVAL_PPL` eval path — same as CREAM.\n- Startup asserts: file exists, `|λ| == head_dim/2`, `λ` monotonic non-decreasing, `λ_i ∈ [1, s·1.25]`.\n\n```\nparse_longrope_file(path) -> (lambda[hd/2], n_hat)\nif CP_LONGROPE_FACTORS set:\n    cache = build_rope_cache_longrope(seq_len=CP_T, head_dim, base=rope_theta, lambda, n_hat, dev)\n    install as the RoPE cache (same slot YaRN/CREAM use)\nelse:\n    existing YaRN build_rope_cache(...)   # unchanged\n```\n\n### Phase 2 — The GA search driver  *(Python, offline, no CUDA)*\n\n`Tests/bluscriptcp/longrope_search.py`. Uses the eval binary as the fitness function.\n\n```python\n# ---- config ----\nD_HALF   = head_dim // 2          # 64 for hd=128\nS        = extension_ratio        # e.g. 8  (4k -> 32k)\nLAM_MIN, LAM_MAX, STEP = 1.0, S*1.25, 0.01\nN_HAT_SET = [0,1,2,4,8,12,16,20,24,28,32,64,128,256]\nP, N1, N2, ITERS, TOPK, PMUT = 64, 16, 16, 40, 32, 0.3\nGRID = np.arange(LAM_MIN, LAM_MAX + 1e-9, STEP)\n\ndef grid_snap(x):                      # clamp + snap to 0.01 grid\n    x = min(max(x, LAM_MIN), LAM_MAX)\n    return round(x/STEP)*STEP\n\ndef is_monotone(lam):\n    return all(lam[i] <= lam[i+1] for i in range(len(lam)-1))\n\ndef repair_monotone(lam):              # enforce non-decreasing (running max)\n    out, m = [], LAM_MIN\n    for v in lam: m = max(m, v); out.append(grid_snap(m))\n    return out\n\n# ---- seeds: PI / NTK / YaRN factor vectors as length-D_HALF lambda vectors ----\ndef seed_PI():   return [grid_snap(S)]*D_HALF                       # uniform full scale\ndef seed_NTK():  return [grid_snap(ntk_lambda(i)) for i in range(D_HALF)]\ndef seed_YaRN(): return [grid_snap(yarn_lambda(i)) for i in range(D_HALF)]\n# (ntk_lambda / yarn_lambda reuse the formulas already in YARNOps.cpp, ported to py)\n\ndef random_individual(base):           # mutate a seed to fill the population\n    lam = [ grid_snap(np.random.choice(GRID)) if random()<PMUT else base[i]\n            for i in range(D_HALF) ]\n    return repair_monotone(lam), random.choice(N_HAT_SET)\n\ndef mutate(parent_lam, parent_nhat):   # <-- the operator: RESAMPLE per dim at p=0.3\n    child = [ grid_snap(np.random.choice(GRID)) if random()<PMUT else parent_lam[i]\n              for i in range(D_HALF) ]\n    nhat  = random.choice(N_HAT_SET) if random()<PMUT else parent_nhat\n    return repair_monotone(child), nhat\n\ndef crossover(a, b):                    # per-dim pick from either parent, then repair\n    lam  = [ a.lam[i] if random()<0.5 else b.lam[i] for i in range(D_HALF) ]\n    nhat = a.nhat if random()<0.5 else b.nhat\n    return repair_monotone(lam), nhat\n\n# ---- fitness: write factors -> run CP_EVAL_PPL -> read overall PPL from CSV ----\ndef fitness(lam, nhat):\n    write_factors_file(\"cand.txt\", lam, nhat)\n    run(f\"CP_SIZE=1 CP_EVAL_PPL=1 CP_LONGROPE_FACTORS=cand.txt \"\n        f\"CP_T={TARGET_T} CP_B=1 CP_EVAL_WINDOWS={CALIB_WINDOWS} \"\n        f\"CP_CKPT_RESUME={CKPT} CP_EVAL_OUT=cand.csv make CP_FUSED_ROPE=1 run-bluscript-cp NP=1\")\n    df = read_csv(\"cand.csv\"); df = df[df[\"count\"] > 0]\n    overall_nll = (df[\"mean_nll\"]*df[\"count\"]).sum() / df[\"count\"].sum()\n    return math.exp(overall_nll)        # lower = better\n\n# ---- GA loop ----\npop  = [ (seed_PI(), N0), (seed_NTK(), N0), (seed_YaRN(), N0) ]\npop += [ random_individual(random.choice([seed_PI(),seed_NTK(),seed_YaRN()])) for _ in range(P-3) ]\nscored = [ (fitness(l,n), l, n) for (l,n) in pop ]\n\nfor it in range(ITERS):\n    scored.sort(key=lambda x: x[0])            # ascending PPL\n    parents = scored[:TOPK]\n    children = []\n    for _ in range(N1):                        # mutation\n        _, l, n = random.choice(parents); children.append(mutate(l, n))\n    for _ in range(N2):                        # crossover\n        pa, pb = random.sample(parents, 2)\n        children.append(crossover(pa, pb))\n    children = [ (l,n) for (l,n) in children if is_monotone(l) ]   # cheap assert after repair\n    scored += [ (fitness(l,n), l, n) for (l,n) in children ]\n    scored = sorted(scored, key=lambda x: x[0])[:P]                # keep best P\n    log(f\"iter {it}: best PPL {scored[0][0]:.3f}\")\n\nbest_ppl, best_lam, best_nhat = scored[0]\nwrite_factors_file(\"longrope_best.txt\", best_lam, best_nhat)\n```\n\nDetails / decisions:\n- **Fitness = overall PPL** aggregated from the per-position CSV: `exp( Σ nll·count / Σ count )`.\n- **Calibration windows** small (paper uses 5 samples) → each fitness call is a few forward passes.\n  40 iters × (16 mut + 16 cross) ≈ ~1300 evals worst-case; monotonicity pruning + memoization cut this.\n- **Memoize** identical `(λ, n̂)` tuples to skip re-evaluating duplicates.\n- `repair_monotone` makes crossover/mutation always produce a legal vector, so the explicit\n  `is_monotone` filter becomes a cheap assertion.\n\n### Phase 3 — Parity + progressive extension  *(reuse existing infra)*\n\n1. **Parity test** `Tests/cp_rope_longrope_parity.cpp`: with `λ_i = 1 ∀i, n̂ = 0`, the LongRoPE\n   cache must equal plain RoPE bit-for-bit; with `λ_i = s ∀i, n̂ = 0` it must equal PI\n   (positions/s). Confirms sign/convention and the `n̂` threshold. Mirror\n   `Tests/cream_cache_gather_parity.cpp`.\n2. **Progressive**: search at the target → **fine-tune** with the found cache using the existing\n   YaRN/CREAM fine-tune + `CP_REWARMUP` path (point `CP_LONGROPE_FACTORS` at `longrope_best.txt`)\n   → optionally re-search on the fine-tuned model for a larger target.\n\n### Phase 4 — Comparison eval\n\nRun the same PPL-vs-position sweep you used for CREAM, adding a LongRoPE arm:\n`{ naive-noYaRN, naive-YaRN, YaRN-LCE, CREAM, LongRoPE }` at each target length. Overlay in\n`graph_bluscript_cp.ipynb`. Expect LongRoPE ≤ YaRN on the flat floor.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Non-destructive invariant\n\nEverything is gated behind `CP_LONGROPE_FACTORS` (and the new C++ function is separate from the\nYaRN builder). Unset ⇒ the program is **byte-identical** to today — same discipline as the CREAM\nintegration. No existing path is modified; only new functions, a new env, and\n`if (longrope_on) {...} else {<existing>}` branches.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Effort summary\n\n| Phase | Effort | Risk |\n|---|---|---|\n| 0 cache builder | small (C++) | low (parity-tested) |\n| 1 wiring | small (mirror CREAM) | low |\n| 2 GA driver | medium (Python orchestration) | low (no CUDA) |\n| 3 parity + progressive | small (reuse) | low |\n| 4 comparison | trivial (existing rig) | — |\n\nThe novel work is the **GA driver**; the cache math is a parity-checkable formula; the fitness and\nfine-tune are already built. LongRoPE is the natural next rung above the existing YaRN + CREAM.\n","structuredPatch":[{"oldStart":1,"oldLines":21,"newStart":1,"newLines":282,"lines":["-The trick is \"blind variation + selective retention.\" Mutation proposes random changes; the fitness evaluation + selection filters them. Beneficial random changes survive; harmful ones die. Over generations the population drifts toward lower PPL, even though every individual change was random.","+# LongRoPE — Explanation + Implementation Plan for bluscriptCP"," ","-Micro-walkthrough (say each λ scored by PPL):","+> Source: Ding et al., *LongRoPE: Extending LLM Context Window Beyond 2 Million Tokens*","+> (Microsoft, 2024). arXiv:2402.13753 — https://arxiv.org/html/2402.13753v1"," ","+---"," ","-Gen 0: parent PPL 40. Make 4 random mutants → PPL 42, 38, 45, 39","-        keep top 2 (38, 39); discard 42, 45","-Gen 1: mutate those 2 → 37, 40, 36, 38","-        keep top 2 (36, 37)","-Gen 2: mutate → 36, 35, 37, 34 ...","-best PPL:  40 → 38 → 36 → 34 → ...","-Each jitter was random ±. Selection kept the lucky-good ones and threw away the rest. The cumulative direction (downward PPL) emerges from selection across many random samples — no single step \"knew\" the right direction.","+## 1. What LongRoPE is"," ","-So \"by how much\" is decided by two things","-The width of the mutation distribution (δ/σ) — a hyperparameter you set:","-Large → big jumps, explores widely, but overshoots good solutions (many bad mutants).","-Small → fine local refinement, but slow and can get stuck.","-The random draw within that width — decides the actual amount for each mutation event.","-Can the magnitude adapt? (partial \"yes\")","-Basic GAs use a fixed or annealed width — start large to explore, shrink over generations to refine (like cooling in simulated annealing). More advanced evolution strategies (e.g. CMA-ES) go further: they learn a good step size and even per-dimension correlations from which past mutations succeeded — so the \"how much\" is tuned by feedback, but still indirectly (from the population's success history), never from a gradient.","+LongRoPE is a **RoPE frequency-reparametrization** method (same family as PI / NTK / YaRN):","+it only changes how the cos/sin cache is built — the attention kernel is untouched. Its thesis:","+the optimal positional interpolation is **non-uniform** in two ways that fixed formulas (PI, NTK,","+YaRN) cannot express, so it **searches** for them instead:"," ","+1. **Per-dimension non-uniformity** — each RoPE frequency dim `i` gets its own rescale factor","+   `λ_i` (not one global scale, not a fixed heuristic ramp).","+2. **Per-token (initial-token) non-uniformity** — the first `n̂` tokens keep their *original*","+   (unscaled) positions; rescaling only applies to positions `n ≥ n̂`.","+","+The rescale formula it optimizes (Eq. 3), for position `n`, dim `i` (with `β^i = base^{2i/d}`):","+","+```","+angle(n, i) = n / β^i             if n <  n̂     (initial tokens: original RoPE)","+angle(n, i) = n / (λ_i · β^i)     if n >= n̂     (rescaled: λ_i divides the frequency)","+```","+","+Larger `λ_i` ⇒ slower rotation ⇒ more interpolation for that dim. `λ_i = 1` ⇒ no interpolation","+(pure extrapolation) for that dim.","+","+**Training requirement.** The *search* for `(λ, n̂)` is **training-free** (forward-only PPL). The","+full recipe still uses a short **fine-tune / annealing** stage for the best results (progressive","+extension). So LongRoPE = trained annealing (like YaRN/CREAM) **plus** a training-free search that","+finds a better-than-formula cache. LongRoPE and CREAM are **orthogonal**: LongRoPE builds a better","+*cache*; CREAM is a *training recipe*. You can run CREAM fine-tuning on top of a LongRoPE cache.","+","+---","+","+## 2. The evolutionary search (exact, from the paper)","+","+**Genome** = a vector of `d/2` per-dim factors `λ = (λ_0 … λ_{d/2-1})` **plus** `n̂`.","+For head_dim 128 → `d/2 = 64` factors.","+","+**Search space (Table 4):**","+- `λ_i ∈ [1.0, s × 1.25]`, discretized in **steps of 0.01** (`s` = extension ratio, e.g. 8 for 4k→32k).","+- `n̂ ∈ {0, 1, 2, 4, 8, 12, 16, 20, 24, 28, 32, 64, 128, 256}`.","+","+**Constraint:** monotonic non-decreasing `λ_0 ≤ λ_1 ≤ … ≤ λ_{d/2-1}`. Candidates that violate it","+are **discarded without evaluation** (this is the key search-cost reduction).","+","+**GA parameters (Table 4 / Alg. 1):**","+","+| Param | Value |","+|---|---|","+| Population `P` | 64 |","+| Mutation size `N₁` | 16 |","+| Crossover size `N₂` | 16 |","+| Iterations `𝒯` | 40 |","+| Parents kept (top-k) | 32 |","+| Mutation probability `p` | 0.3 |","+| (>512k target: halve `P`, `N₁`, `N₂`) | — |","+","+**Initialization:** seed with **PI, NTK, YaRN** (3 individuals) + `P−3` made by mutating those three.","+","+**Mutation operator (the \"how much\" — corrected):** it is NOT a small ±jitter. For each dimension,","+**with probability `p=0.3`, RESAMPLE `λ_i` fresh from the grid** `[1.0, s×1.25]` (step 0.01); with","+prob 0.7 keep the parent's value. There is **no magnitude / annealing schedule** — the \"how much\"","+is \"anywhere in the legal grid,\" a random re-draw, and **selection** (keep the low-PPL survivors)","+does the optimization. \"Blind variation + selective retention.\"","+","+**Crossover:** combine two parents into a child (per-dim pick from either parent) subject to the","+monotonicity constraint (`Crossover_with_mono_constraint`).","+","+**Fitness:** validation perplexity, forward-only — 5 random PG19 samples (≤256k) or 3 Pile-Books3","+samples (>512k), each ≥ target length. Overall PPL per candidate (lower = fitter).","+","+**Progressive extension:**","+1. Search on the pretrained model for the intermediate target.","+2. Fine-tune to 256k (400 steps @128k, then 600 @256k).","+3. Second search on the fine-tuned model → 2048k with **no** further fine-tune.","+4. Readjust with a 4k/8k search (reduced max `λ`) to recover short-context performance.","+","+---","+","+## 3. Mapping to the bluscriptCP stack","+","+Three pieces; two already exist:","+","+| Piece | Status | Where |","+|---|---|---|","+| Cache builder that applies `(λ, n̂)` | **new (small)** | `context_parallel/YARNOps.cpp` (`build_rope_cache`) |","+| Forward-only PPL fitness | **exists** | `CP_EVAL_PPL` path in `bluscriptCP.cpp` |","+| The GA driver | **new (Python, offline)** | `Tests/bluscriptcp/longrope_search.py` |","+","+The fused kernel (`GQA_fused_*`) and the training loop **do not change** — they index whatever","+cache they are given, exactly as with YaRN/CREAM. Every change is **additive and gated** behind a","+new `CP_LONGROPE_FACTORS` env (mirrors the CREAM `CP_CREAM_MODE` discipline): unset ⇒ byte-identical","+to today.","+","+---","+","+## 4. Implementation plan (phased, additive)","+","+### Phase 0 — Cache builder accepts explicit `(λ, n̂)`  *(C++, additive)*","+","+Add a new function next to `build_rope_cache` in `context_parallel/YARNOps.cpp`. Do NOT modify the","+existing YaRN builder; this is a separate path selected only when LongRoPE is enabled.","+","+```cpp","+// NEW, additive. lambda has length head_dim/2 (one factor per freq dim); n_hat = initial","+// unscaled token count. Applies angle(n,i) = n*inv_freq_i / (n<n_hat ? 1 : lambda_i).","+Tensor build_rope_cache_longrope(int64_t seq_len, int64_t head_dim, float base,","+                                 const std::vector<float>& lambda, int64_t n_hat,","+                                 DeviceIndex device) {","+    assert((int64_t)lambda.size() == head_dim/2);","+    Tensor cache(Shape{{seq_len, head_dim}}, fp32, CPU);   // build on CPU, then .to(device)","+    for (int64_t n = 0; n < seq_len; ++n) {","+        for (int64_t i = 0; i < head_dim/2; ++i) {","+            double inv_freq = pow((double)base, -2.0*i/head_dim);   // = 1/β^i","+            double scale    = (n < n_hat) ? 1.0 : (double)lambda[i];","+            double ang      = (double)n * inv_freq / scale;","+            cache[n][i]            = cos(ang);   // cos half","+            cache[n][i+head_dim/2] = sin(ang);   // sin half  (NeoX layout, matches YaRN)","+        }","+    }","+    return cache.to(device);   // grad-free","+}","+```","+","+Notes:","+- Layout MUST match the existing YaRN cache (cos in `[0,hd/2)`, sin in `[hd/2,hd)`) so the fused","+  kernel indexes it identically. Cross-check against `YARNOps.cpp` current layout.","+- LongRoPE does **not** use YaRN's attention-temperature `m` — this builder omits it.","+- `λ_i` here is the paper's factor: it **divides** the frequency. Verify sign/convention with the","+  Phase-3 parity test.","+","+### Phase 1 — Wire bluscriptCP to load `(λ, n̂)` from a file  *(C++, additive, gated)*","+","+Mirror the CREAM integration in `Scripts/Blutrain/bluscriptCP.cpp`:","+","+- New env: `CP_LONGROPE_FACTORS=<path>` (a text/JSON file with the `λ` vector + `n̂`). Unset ⇒ off.","+- In the model constructor, when set, parse the file and build the cache via","+  `build_rope_cache_longrope(...)` instead of the YaRN `build_rope_cache(...)`. Keep the base","+  (YaRN) path verbatim when unset.","+- Applies to BOTH training (fine-tune) and the `CP_EVAL_PPL` eval path — same as CREAM.","+- Startup asserts: file exists, `|λ| == head_dim/2`, `λ` monotonic non-decreasing, `λ_i ∈ [1, s·1.25]`.","+","+```","+parse_longrope_file(path) -> (lambda[hd/2], n_hat)","+if CP_LONGROPE_FACTORS set:","+    cache = build_rope_cache_longrope(seq_len=CP_T, head_dim, base=rope_theta, lambda, n_hat, dev)","+    install as the RoPE cache (same slot YaRN/CREAM use)","+else:","+    existing YaRN build_rope_cache(...)   # unchanged","+```","+","+### Phase 2 — The GA search driver  *(Python, offline, no CUDA)*","+","+`Tests/bluscriptcp/longrope_search.py`. Uses the eval binary as the fitness function.","+","+```python","+# ---- config ----","+D_HALF   = head_dim // 2          # 64 for hd=128","+S        = extension_ratio        # e.g. 8  (4k -> 32k)","+LAM_MIN, LAM_MAX, STEP = 1.0, S*1.25, 0.01","+N_HAT_SET = [0,1,2,4,8,12,16,20,24,28,32,64,128,256]","+P, N1, N2, ITERS, TOPK, PMUT = 64, 16, 16, 40, 32, 0.3","+GRID = np.arange(LAM_MIN, LAM_MAX + 1e-9, STEP)","+","+def grid_snap(x):                      # clamp + snap to 0.01 grid","+    x = min(max(x, LAM_MIN), LAM_MAX)","+    return round(x/STEP)*STEP","+","+def is_monotone(lam):","+    return all(lam[i] <= lam[i+1] for i in range(len(lam)-1))","+","+def repair_monotone(lam):              # enforce non-decreasing (running max)","+    out, m = [], LAM_MIN","+    for v in lam: m = max(m, v); out.append(grid_snap(m))","+    return out","+","+# ---- seeds: PI / NTK / YaRN factor vectors as length-D_HALF lambda vectors ----","+def seed_PI():   return [grid_snap(S)]*D_HALF                       # uniform full scale","+def seed_NTK():  return [grid_snap(ntk_lambda(i)) for i in range(D_HALF)]","+def seed_YaRN(): return [grid_snap(yarn_lambda(i)) for i in range(D_HALF)]","+# (ntk_lambda / yarn_lambda reuse the formulas already in YARNOps.cpp, ported to py)","+","+def random_individual(base):           # mutate a seed to fill the population","+    lam = [ grid_snap(np.random.choice(GRID)) if random()<PMUT else base[i]","+            for i in range(D_HALF) ]","+    return repair_monotone(lam), random.choice(N_HAT_SET)","+","+def mutate(parent_lam, parent_nhat):   # <-- the operator: RESAMPLE per dim at p=0.3","+    child = [ grid_snap(np.random.choice(GRID)) if random()<PMUT else parent_lam[i]","+              for i in range(D_HALF) ]","+    nhat  = random.choice(N_HAT_SET) if random()<PMUT else parent_nhat","+    return repair_monotone(child), nhat","+","+def crossover(a, b):                    # per-dim pick from either parent, then repair","+    lam  = [ a.lam[i] if random()<0.5 else b.lam[i] for i in range(D_HALF) ]","+    nhat = a.nhat if random()<0.5 else b.nhat","+    return repair_monotone(lam), nhat","+","+# ---- fitness: write factors -> run CP_EVAL_PPL -> read overall PPL from CSV ----","+def fitness(lam, nhat):","+    write_factors_file(\"cand.txt\", lam, nhat)","+    run(f\"CP_SIZE=1 CP_EVAL_PPL=1 CP_LONGROPE_FACTORS=cand.txt \"","+        f\"CP_T={TARGET_T} CP_B=1 CP_EVAL_WINDOWS={CALIB_WINDOWS} \"","+        f\"CP_CKPT_RESUME={CKPT} CP_EVAL_OUT=cand.csv make CP_FUSED_ROPE=1 run-bluscript-cp NP=1\")","+    df = read_csv(\"cand.csv\"); df = df[df[\"count\"] > 0]","+    overall_nll = (df[\"mean_nll\"]*df[\"count\"]).sum() / df[\"count\"].sum()","+    return math.exp(overall_nll)        # lower = better","+","+# ---- GA loop ----","+pop  = [ (seed_PI(), N0), (seed_NTK(), N0), (seed_YaRN(), N0) ]","+pop += [ random_individual(random.choice([seed_PI(),seed_NTK(),seed_YaRN()])) for _ in range(P-3) ]","+scored = [ (fitness(l,n), l, n) for (l,n) in pop ]","+","+for it in range(ITERS):","+    scored.sort(key=lambda x: x[0])            # ascending PPL","+    parents = scored[:TOPK]","+    children = []","+    for _ in range(N1):                        # mutation","+        _, l, n = random.choice(parents); children.append(mutate(l, n))","+    for _ in range(N2):                        # crossover","+        pa, pb = random.sample(parents, 2)","+        children.append(crossover(pa, pb))","+    children = [ (l,n) for (l,n) in children if is_monotone(l) ]   # cheap assert after repair","+    scored += [ (fitness(l,n), l, n) for (l,n) in children ]","+    scored = sorted(scored, key=lambda x: x[0])[:P]                # keep best P","+    log(f\"iter {it}: best PPL {scored[0][0]:.3f}\")","+","+best_ppl, best_lam, best_nhat = scored[0]","+write_factors_file(\"longrope_best.txt\", best_lam, best_nhat)","+```","+","+Details / decisions:","+- **Fitness = overall PPL** aggregated from the per-position CSV: `exp( Σ nll·count / Σ count )`.","+- **Calibration windows** small (paper uses 5 samples) → each fitness call is a few forward passes.","+  40 iters × (16 mut + 16 cross) ≈ ~1300 evals worst-case; monotonicity pruning + memoization cut this.","+- **Memoize** identical `(λ, n̂)` tuples to skip re-evaluating duplicates.","+- `repair_monotone` makes crossover/mutation always produce a legal vector, so the explicit","+  `is_monotone` filter becomes a cheap assertion.","+","+### Phase 3 — Parity + progressive extension  *(reuse existing infra)*","+","+1. **Parity test** `Tests/cp_rope_longrope_parity.cpp`: with `λ_i = 1 ∀i, n̂ = 0`, the LongRoPE","+   cache must equal plain RoPE bit-for-bit; with `λ_i = s ∀i, n̂ = 0` it must equal PI","+   (positions/s). Confirms sign/convention and the `n̂` threshold. Mirror","+   `Tests/cream_cache_gather_parity.cpp`.","+2. **Progressive**: search at the target → **fine-tune** with the found cache using the existing","+   YaRN/CREAM fine-tune + `CP_REWARMUP` path (point `CP_LONGROPE_FACTORS` at `longrope_best.txt`)","+   → optionally re-search on the fine-tuned model for a larger target.","+","+### Phase 4 — Comparison eval","+","+Run the same PPL-vs-position sweep you used for CREAM, adding a LongRoPE arm:","+`{ naive-noYaRN, naive-YaRN, YaRN-LCE, CREAM, LongRoPE }` at each target length. Overlay in","+`graph_bluscript_cp.ipynb`. Expect LongRoPE ≤ YaRN on the flat floor.","+","+---","+","+## 5. Non-destructive invariant","+","+Everything is gated behind `CP_LONGROPE_FACTORS` (and the new C++ function is separate from the","+YaRN builder). Unset ⇒ the program is **byte-identical** to today — same discipline as the CREAM","+integration. No existing path is modified; only new functions, a new env, and","+`if (longrope_on) {...} else {<existing>}` branches.","+","+---","+","+## 6. Effort summary","+","+| Phase | Effort | Risk |","+|---|---|---|","+| 0 cache builder | small (C++) | low (parity-tested) |","+| 1 wiring | small (mirror CREAM) | low |","+| 2 GA driver | medium (Python orchestration) | low (no CUDA) |","+| 3 parity + progressive | small (reuse) | low |","+| 4 comparison | trivial (existing rig) | — |","+","+The novel work is the **GA driver**; the cache math is a parity-checkable formula; the fitness and","+fine-tune are already built. LongRoPE is the natural next rung above the existing YaRN + CREAM."]}],"originalFile":"The trick is \"blind variation + selective retention.\" Mutation proposes random changes; the fitness evaluation + selection filters them. Beneficial random changes survive; harmful ones die. Over generations the population drifts toward lower PPL, even though every individual change was random.\n\nMicro-walkthrough (say each λ scored by PPL):\n\n\nGen 0: parent PPL 40. Make 4 random mutants → PPL 42, 38, 45, 39\n        keep top 2 (38, 39); discard 42, 45\nGen 1: mutate those 2 → 37, 40, 36, 38\n        keep top 2 (36, 37)\nGen 2: mutate → 36, 35, 37, 34 ...\nbest PPL:  40 → 38 → 36 → 34 → ...\nEach jitter was random ±. Selection kept the lucky-good ones and threw away the rest. The cumulative direction (downward PPL) emerges from selection across many random samples — no single step \"knew\" the right direction.\n\nSo \"by how much\" is decided by two things\nThe width of the mutation distribution (δ/σ) — a hyperparameter you set:\nLarge → big jumps, explores widely, but overshoots good solutions (many bad mutants).\nSmall → fine local refinement, but slow and can get stuck.\nThe random draw within that width — decides the actual amount for each mutation event.\nCan the magnitude adapt? (partial \"yes\")\nBasic GAs use a fixed or annealed width — start large to explore, shrink over generations to refine (like cooling in simulated annealing). More advanced evolution strategies (e.g. CMA-ES) go further: they learn a good step size and even per-dimension correlations from which past mutations succeeded — so the \"how much\" is tuned by feedback, but still indirectly (from the population's success history), never from a gradient.\n\n","userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"63154cab-8fcb-4d8c-bbc1-0554069532a1","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
{"parentUuid":"8958e542-af9c-4cfd-a3f2-0e9b64066237","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011Cda1MCcNGGkzdVNyY4Y8T","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6689,"cache_read_input_tokens":458336,"output_tokens":1226,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6689,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1226,"cache_read_input_tokens":458336,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6689,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6689},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011Cda1M9TcFN9yHsafmdvmz","type":"assistant","uuid":"2865b401-8e36-466b-8472-2b11bf439dbf","timestamp":"2026-07-31T10:50:46.724Z","effort":"low","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
{"parentUuid":"2865b401-8e36-466b-8472-2b11bf439dbf","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011Cda1MCcNGGkzdVNyY4Y8T","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I understood 1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8\n\nWritten to [.mdfiles/LongRoPEexplanasion.md](.mdfiles/LongRoPEexplanasion.md). It contains both the explanation and the full implementation plan with pseudocode:\n\n**Explanation (grounded in the paper):**\n- What LongRoPE is (per-dim `λ_i` + initial-token `n̂` non-uniformity), the exact rescale formula, and the training story (search is training-free, recipe still fine-tunes).\n- The **exact** search: genome, discrete grid `[1, s×1.25]` step 0.01, `n̂` set, monotonicity constraint, `P=64 / N₁=16 / N₂=16 / 40 iters / top-32 / p=0.3`, PI/NTK/YaRN seeds, and the corrected mutation (\"**resample from grid at p=0.3**,\" not a small jitter).\n\n**Plan (phased, additive, mirrors your CREAM integration):**\n- **Phase 0** — new `build_rope_cache_longrope(λ, n̂)` in `YARNOps.cpp` (C++ pseudocode included; additive, no YaRN touch).\n- **Phase 1** — gate it behind `CP_LONGROPE_FACTORS=<file>` in `bluscriptCP.cpp`, for both train and eval (byte-identical when unset).\n- **Phase 2** — the GA driver `longrope_search.py` (full Python pseudocode: seeds, `mutate`/`crossover` with `repair_monotone`, `fitness` calling `CP_EVAL_PPL` and reading overall PPL from the CSV, the 40-generation loop).\n- **Phase 3** — parity test (`λ=1,n̂=0`→plain RoPE; `λ=s`→PI) + progressive fine-tune via your existing `CP_REWARMUP` path.\n- **Phase 4** — add a LongRoPE arm to the same PPL-vs-position sweep as CREAM.\n\nPlus the non-destructive invariant and an effort/risk table. The novel work is the Python GA driver; the cache math is parity-checkable and the fitness/fine-tune already exist.\n\nTwo things to confirm before Phase 0 if you want me to start implementing:\n1. **Cache layout / convention** — I should read `YARNOps.cpp` to match its exact cos/sin layout and confirm the `λ`-divides-frequency sign against your NeoX kernel (the parity test enforces it, but I'd verify first).\n2. **Fitness metric** — I assumed overall PPL `exp(Σ nll·count / Σ count)` from the per-position CSV; if you'd rather the search optimize PPL only in the extended region (>orig_ctx), that's a one-line change in `fitness()`.\n\nWant me to kick off Phase 0 (the cache builder + parity test) — that's the self-contained, low-risk core — or refine the plan further first?"}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6689,"cache_read_input_tokens":458336,"output_tokens":1226,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6689,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1226,"cache_read_input_tokens":458336,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6689,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6689},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011Cda1M9TcFN9yHsafmdvmz","type":"assistant","uuid":"b7a786c7-08ed-4685-b086-89d0fca701e5","timestamp":"2026-07-31T10:50:59.584Z","effort":"low","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
{"parentUuid":"b7a786c7-08ed-4685-b086-89d0fca701e5","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"Stop","toolUseID":"3a19fe1f-c65b-4b59-83dd-aeed7a431e16","hookEvent":"Stop","content":"","stdout":"{\"session_id\":\"3d0a11bb-de89-4095-8132-fc46c7f9936b\",\"transcript_path\":\"/home/blu-bridge25/.claude/projects/-home-blu-bridge25-CP/3d0a11bb-de89-4095-8132-fc46c7f9936b.jsonl\",\"cwd\":\"/home/blu-bridge25/CP\",\"prompt_id\":\"453acb7c-0f8d-4fcf-81a8-ade807583aa6\",\"permission_mode\":\"acceptEdits\",\"effort\":{\"level\":\"low\"},\"hook_event_name\":\"Stop\",\"stop_hook_active\":false,\"last_assistant_message\":\"I understood 1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8\\n\\nWritten to [.mdfiles/LongRoPEexplanasion.md](.mdfiles/LongRoPEexplanasion.md). It contains both the explanation and the full implementation plan with pseudocode:\\n\\n**Explanation (grounded in the paper):**\\n- What LongRoPE is (per-dim `λ_i` + initial-token `n̂` non-uniformity), the exact rescale formula, and the training story (search is training-free, recipe still fine-tunes).\\n- The **exact** search: genome, discrete grid `[1, s×1.25]` step 0.01, `n̂` set, monotonicity constraint, `P=64 / N₁=16 / N₂=16 / 40 iters / top-32 / p=0.3`, PI/NTK/YaRN seeds, and the corrected mutation (\\\"**resample from grid at p=0.3**,\\\" not a small jitter).\\n\\n**Plan (phased, additive, mirrors your CREAM integration):**\\n- **Phase 0** — new `build_rope_cache_longrope(λ, n̂)` in `YARNOps.cpp` (C++ pseudocode included; additive, no YaRN touch).\\n- **Phase 1** — gate it behind `CP_LONGROPE_FACTORS=<file>` in `bluscriptCP.cpp`, for both train and eval (byte-identical when unset).\\n- **Phase 2** — the GA driver `longrope_search.py` (full Python pseudocode: seeds, `mutate`/`crossover` with `repair_monotone`, `fitness` calling `CP_EVAL_PPL` and reading overall PPL from the CSV, the 40-generation loop).\\n- **Phase 3** — parity test (`λ=1,n̂=0`→plain RoPE; `λ=s`→PI) + progressive fine-tune via your existing `CP_REWARMUP` path.\\n- **Phase 4** — add a LongRoPE arm to the same PPL-vs-position sweep as CREAM.\\n\\nPlus the non-destructive invariant and an effort/risk table. The novel work is the Python GA driver; the cache math is parity-checkable and the fitness/fine-tune already exist.\\n\\nTwo things to confirm before Phase 0 if you want me to start implementing:\\n1. **Cache layout / convention** — I should read `YARNOps.cpp` to match its exact cos/sin layout and confirm the `λ`-divides-frequency sign against your NeoX kernel (the parity test enforces it, but I'd verify first).\\n2. **Fitness metric** — I assumed overall PPL `exp(Σ nll·count / Σ count)` from the per-position CSV; if you'd rather the search optimize PPL only in the extended region (>orig_ctx), that's a one-line change in `fitness()`.\\n\\nWant me to kick off Phase 0 (the cache builder + parity test) — that's the self-contained, low-risk core — or refine the plan further first?\",\"background_tasks\":[],\"session_crons\":[]}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"node \"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/scripts/hooks/run-with-flags.js\" \"stop:check-console-log\" \"scripts/hooks/check-console-log.js\" \"standard,strict\"","durationMs":46},"type":"attachment","uuid":"c79b2326-0a83-4201-bd54-0112bee83569","timestamp":"2026-07-31T10:50:59.918Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
