{"parentUuid":"c8815fe4-c4dc-4e5a-b9b5-30e392d35191","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011Cda2f9EpvZykgDLzGLFyX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01TjU75MdDd4NVVdvnZGM9N3","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP/.mdfiles/plancritique copy 6.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1704,"cache_read_input_tokens":498975,"output_tokens":156,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1704,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":156,"cache_read_input_tokens":498975,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1704,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1704},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011Cda2f5d3Vp3ab4SrkpPim","type":"assistant","uuid":"2d2389d6-7462-447e-9d3c-d75f54548fd4","timestamp":"2026-07-31T11:07:54.185Z","effort":"low","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
{"parentUuid":"2d2389d6-7462-447e-9d3c-d75f54548fd4","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"5e2595fa-ad58-4dbd-9b87-ff4a7e3e30bb","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TjU75MdDd4NVVdvnZGM9N3","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tEvery item from the previous round is resolved, and resolved with the same rigor as the fixes throughout this whole series — actual mechanisms and asserts, not restated assurances. Quick verification on the one I could check by direct derivation: `λ_NTK[i] = base_freq_i / ntk_inv_freq_i = (base/base')^(-2i/d)` where `base' = base·s^(d/(d-2))`, which works out to `s^(2i/(d-2))` — matches the plan's stated closed form exactly, and `λ_NTK[0] = s^0 = 1` checks out too. The `repair_monotone` clarification, the memoization key, the resident-evaluator design, and the CREAM-coupling fix (with its own dedicated verification item, 2b) are all specified precisely enough that I don't have a correctness objection to any of them. Two narrower things worth flagging, in the same \"test the test\" spirit as the rest of this series, plus one small documentation reconciliation.\n2\t\n3\t**The progressive-pipeline's example numbers now silently conflict with the coupling assert you just added, and that's worth reconciling explicitly rather than leaving as an implicit trap.** The fix correctly requires `S_search == cream_scaled_max` (i.e., `factor · T_finetune`) whenever LongRoPE composes with CREAM. But the pipeline section still reads \"Stage A: search at `T1` (e.g. 16k)... Stage B: search at `T2` (e.g. 64k)\" as if `T1`/`T2` were freely chosen round numbers. In the composed path they aren't free anymore — they're *determined* by whatever `YARN_SCALE`/`T_finetune` CREAM is configured with, and will only equal a tidy \"16k\" if someone happens to pick CREAM's config to produce that product. As written, this is likely to cost someone a wasted setup cycle: pick \"T1=16k\" because the example says so, pick CREAM's physical `T`/`YARN_SCALE` independently because they seem reasonable, hit the (correctly implemented) startup assert, and have to work backward to figure out why. Not a correctness gap — the assert is doing exactly its job — but worth a one-line fix to the pipeline section: state explicitly that in the composed path, `T1`/`T2` are *derived* as `factor · T_finetune` from whatever CREAM config is chosen, not picked independently, so the example numbers read as \"one consistent worked example\" rather than two free parameters that happen to need to agree.\n4\t\n5\t**The new resident search-evaluator mode introduces a genuinely new usage pattern for `set_rope_cache` that hasn't been exercised before, and deserves its own small confirmation.** Every prior use of `set_rope_cache` in this project (CREAM's per-step install, LongRoPE's direct install) swaps the cache once (or once per training step) within an otherwise-normal forward/backward cycle. `CP_LONGROPE_SEARCH=1` introduces something different: one long-lived process, loading the model once, then swapping the cache in a tight loop across potentially thousands of candidates and reading back a fresh PPL each time. That's exactly the kind of new execution shape where a stale-reference bug could hide — if anything downstream of the swap (a cached pointer taken at first use, a lazily-initialized view, anything that isn't a live re-read of the `Tensor` each forward call) doesn't actually pick up the Nth candidate's cache, the loop would keep silently scoring the *first* candidate's cache 2560 times, producing a perfectly plausible-looking, monotonically-uninteresting search trace (flat or noisy PPL, no real signal) that could easily be misread as \"the search isn't finding anything better\" rather than \"the mechanism isn't swapping.\" Worth an explicit smoke check before trusting real search results: feed two candidates with deliberately very different `λ`/`n̂` (e.g., one near-identity, one aggressively rescaled) through the resident evaluator back-to-back and confirm the reported PPL actually differs between them — a direct test that the swap takes effect on each iteration, not just that the loop runs without error.\n6\t\n7\t**Small clarification, not a gap:** worth one line stating that Stage C (recovery, at 4k/8k) is LongRoPE-alone and not composed with CREAM, since it runs at a length the GPU can already handle directly — otherwise a reader might wonder whether the new `S_search == cream_scaled_max` coupling needs to be re-derived for a third stage, when it simply doesn't apply there.\n8\t\n9\tEverything else holds up as fully resolved. This is ready to build."}]},"uuid":"a34a1fa9-cb91-4bda-9083-246fc68bb31a","timestamp":"2026-07-31T11:07:54.191Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP/.mdfiles/plancritique copy 6.md","content":"Every item from the previous round is resolved, and resolved with the same rigor as the fixes throughout this whole series — actual mechanisms and asserts, not restated assurances. Quick verification on the one I could check by direct derivation: `λ_NTK[i] = base_freq_i / ntk_inv_freq_i = (base/base')^(-2i/d)` where `base' = base·s^(d/(d-2))`, which works out to `s^(2i/(d-2))` — matches the plan's stated closed form exactly, and `λ_NTK[0] = s^0 = 1` checks out too. The `repair_monotone` clarification, the memoization key, the resident-evaluator design, and the CREAM-coupling fix (with its own dedicated verification item, 2b) are all specified precisely enough that I don't have a correctness objection to any of them. Two narrower things worth flagging, in the same \"test the test\" spirit as the rest of this series, plus one small documentation reconciliation.\n\n**The progressive-pipeline's example numbers now silently conflict with the coupling assert you just added, and that's worth reconciling explicitly rather than leaving as an implicit trap.** The fix correctly requires `S_search == cream_scaled_max` (i.e., `factor · T_finetune`) whenever LongRoPE composes with CREAM. But the pipeline section still reads \"Stage A: search at `T1` (e.g. 16k)... Stage B: search at `T2` (e.g. 64k)\" as if `T1`/`T2` were freely chosen round numbers. In the composed path they aren't free anymore — they're *determined* by whatever `YARN_SCALE`/`T_finetune` CREAM is configured with, and will only equal a tidy \"16k\" if someone happens to pick CREAM's config to produce that product. As written, this is likely to cost someone a wasted setup cycle: pick \"T1=16k\" because the example says so, pick CREAM's physical `T`/`YARN_SCALE` independently because they seem reasonable, hit the (correctly implemented) startup assert, and have to work backward to figure out why. Not a correctness gap — the assert is doing exactly its job — but worth a one-line fix to the pipeline section: state explicitly that in the composed path, `T1`/`T2` are *derived* as `factor · T_finetune` from whatever CREAM config is chosen, not picked independently, so the example numbers read as \"one consistent worked example\" rather than two free parameters that happen to need to agree.\n\n**The new resident search-evaluator mode introduces a genuinely new usage pattern for `set_rope_cache` that hasn't been exercised before, and deserves its own small confirmation.** Every prior use of `set_rope_cache` in this project (CREAM's per-step install, LongRoPE's direct install) swaps the cache once (or once per training step) within an otherwise-normal forward/backward cycle. `CP_LONGROPE_SEARCH=1` introduces something different: one long-lived process, loading the model once, then swapping the cache in a tight loop across potentially thousands of candidates and reading back a fresh PPL each time. That's exactly the kind of new execution shape where a stale-reference bug could hide — if anything downstream of the swap (a cached pointer taken at first use, a lazily-initialized view, anything that isn't a live re-read of the `Tensor` each forward call) doesn't actually pick up the Nth candidate's cache, the loop would keep silently scoring the *first* candidate's cache 2560 times, producing a perfectly plausible-looking, monotonically-uninteresting search trace (flat or noisy PPL, no real signal) that could easily be misread as \"the search isn't finding anything better\" rather than \"the mechanism isn't swapping.\" Worth an explicit smoke check before trusting real search results: feed two candidates with deliberately very different `λ`/`n̂` (e.g., one near-identity, one aggressively rescaled) through the resident evaluator back-to-back and confirm the reported PPL actually differs between them — a direct test that the swap takes effect on each iteration, not just that the loop runs without error.\n\n**Small clarification, not a gap:** worth one line stating that Stage C (recovery, at 4k/8k) is LongRoPE-alone and not composed with CREAM, since it runs at a length the GPU can already handle directly — otherwise a reader might wonder whether the new `S_search == cream_scaled_max` coupling needs to be re-derived for a third stage, when it simply doesn't apply there.\n\nEverything else holds up as fully resolved. This is ready to build.","numLines":9,"startLine":1,"totalLines":9}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"6cbfb60b-0cbb-4d5b-852c-5554bb97703d","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
