{"parentUuid":"a739f6a4-2874-4865-b4e1-5427148af8b4","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"async_hook_response","processId":"async_hook_1371308","hookName":"Stop","hookEvent":"Stop","response":{},"stdout":"","stderr":"[ContinuousLearning] Session has 293 messages - evaluate for extractable patterns\n[ContinuousLearning] Save learned skills to: /home/blu-bridge25/.claude/skills/learned/\n","exitCode":0},"type":"attachment","uuid":"7b580a05-b80a-4aa7-ad3a-26fc97822b75","timestamp":"2026-08-01T05:11:20.662Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
{"parentUuid":"7b580a05-b80a-4aa7-ad3a-26fc97822b75","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CdbTHPAY3rutKLTRau8vP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1638,"cache_read_input_tokens":683251,"output_tokens":3889,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1638,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3889,"cache_read_input_tokens":683251,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1638,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1638},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdbTHLDBfKFZiepmb4Qcc","type":"assistant","uuid":"faf4e8d0-408b-4bd1-b64c-97ea8909365f","timestamp":"2026-08-01T05:11:58.474Z","effort":"low","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
{"parentUuid":"faf4e8d0-408b-4bd1-b64c-97ea8909365f","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CdbTHPAY3rutKLTRau8vP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Good questions — let me separate them, because memory and speed pull in different directions.\n\n## Local 3060 vs 1× RTX 6000 Ada (48 GB, sm_89)\n\n| | 3060 (12 GB) | 1× 6000 Ada (48 GB) |\n|---|---|---|\n| **s=4 (16k)** total | ~2–3h (fits) | ~1.5–2h (faster GPU) |\n| **s=16 (64k)** | **OOM** — 64k logits = 13.2 GB > 12 GB | **fits easily** (~3–6h) |\n| per-eval @16k | 26s (measured) | ~12–15s (est.) |\n\nThe 6000 Ada's real advantage isn't just speed — it's **48 GB of memory**, which is what lets you run **s=16 at all**. The 3060 can't hold a 64k forward.\n\n## What s=16 (4k→64k) costs\n\n- **Fine-tune stays cheap** — CREAM-composed fine-tune is always **4k physical**, so it's ~50 min regardless of `s`. That's the whole point of the composition: `s` only widens the *cache*, not the training sequence.\n- **Search + eval get ~4× heavier** — per-eval scales with target length (NLL and forward are ~O(T)). So @64k, per-eval ≈ 4× @16k → ~50–60s on the 6000 Ada.\n- Right-sized search (~150–350 evals with early-stop) → **~2.5–5h search** + ~1h fine-tune/eval ≈ **~3.5–6h total on one 6000 Ada**.\n- Needs val docs ≥64k tokens — your val shard (100M tokens) has ~1500 such windows, so fine.\n\n## More than 1 GPU?\n\n**As shipped: no** — everything is `CP_SIZE=1`. But the split matters:\n\n- **The search is embarrassingly parallel** — candidates are independent. Sharding them across N GPUs (N resident evaluators, each scoring a slice) gives **~N× search speedup**. This is the big multi-GPU win, and it's the dominant cost. **Not implemented yet** — the driver uses one GPU.\n- **Fine-tune + eval stay single-GPU** (CREAM is single-GPU; `CP_EVAL_PPL` needs `CP_SIZE=1`). But they're small (~1h total), so that's fine.\n\n**One caveat for multi-GPU:** part of per-eval is the **CPU log-sum-exp NLL** over the 50k vocab. If you run N evaluators on one node, they contend for CPU cores → sub-linear speedup. The fix that makes multi-GPU scale cleanly is **moving the NLL to the GPU** (a small kernel), which also gives ~3–4× per-eval on a single GPU.\n\nSo on **4× 6000 Ada** with both extensions: search ~4× faster + ~3× from GPU-NLL → **s=16 search in ~30–60 min, total ~1.5–2.5h**.\n\n## Recommendation\n- **s=4, quick check:** run locally now (~2–3h), or 6000 Ada (~1.5–2h).\n- **s=16, or a real result:** use the 6000 Ada(s) — you need the memory anyway.\n- **If you have multiple 6000 Adas:** the two high-value upgrades are (1) **GPU-NLL fitness** (~3–4× per-eval, both local and server) and (2) **multi-GPU search sharding** (~N×). Together they cut the s=16 search from hours to under an hour.\n\nWant me to implement those two — **GPU-side NLL** and **multi-GPU search sharding** in the driver (launch one resident evaluator per GPU, distribute candidates round-robin)? That's the single biggest speedup available and directly targets your multi-6000-Ada setup."}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1638,"cache_read_input_tokens":683251,"output_tokens":3889,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1638,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3889,"cache_read_input_tokens":683251,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1638,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1638},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdbTHLDBfKFZiepmb4Qcc","type":"assistant","uuid":"0ff42ab3-6f02-4087-bff5-bbccb89f2eab","timestamp":"2026-08-01T05:12:14.387Z","effort":"low","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
{"parentUuid":"0ff42ab3-6f02-4087-bff5-bbccb89f2eab","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"Stop","toolUseID":"feb6629b-1022-449c-a468-b212164c025e","hookEvent":"Stop","content":"","stdout":"{\"session_id\":\"3d0a11bb-de89-4095-8132-fc46c7f9936b\",\"transcript_path\":\"/home/blu-bridge25/.claude/projects/-home-blu-bridge25-CP/3d0a11bb-de89-4095-8132-fc46c7f9936b.jsonl\",\"cwd\":\"/home/blu-bridge25/CP\",\"prompt_id\":\"89685c77-e274-42b5-ad02-4dc9674d2cec\",\"permission_mode\":\"acceptEdits\",\"effort\":{\"level\":\"low\"},\"hook_event_name\":\"Stop\",\"stop_hook_active\":false,\"last_assistant_message\":\"Good questions — let me separate them, because memory and speed pull in different directions.\\n\\n## Local 3060 vs 1× RTX 6000 Ada (48 GB, sm_89)\\n\\n| | 3060 (12 GB) | 1× 6000 Ada (48 GB) |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| **s=4 (16k)** total | ~2–3h (fits) | ~1.5–2h (faster GPU) |\\n| **s=16 (64k)** | **OOM** — 64k logits = 13.2 GB > 12 GB | **fits easily** (~3–6h) |\\n| per-eval @16k | 26s (measured) | ~12–15s (est.) |\\n\\nThe 6000 Ada's real advantage isn't just speed — it's **48 GB of memory**, which is what lets you run **s=16 at all**. The 3060 can't hold a 64k forward.\\n\\n## What s=16 (4k→64k) costs\\n\\n- **Fine-tune stays cheap** — CREAM-composed fine-tune is always **4k physical**, so it's ~50 min regardless of `s`. That's the whole point of the composition: `s` only widens the *cache*, not the training sequence.\\n- **Search + eval get ~4× heavier** — per-eval scales with target length (NLL and forward are ~O(T)). So @64k, per-eval ≈ 4× @16k → ~50–60s on the 6000 Ada.\\n- Right-sized search (~150–350 evals with early-stop) → **~2.5–5h search** + ~1h fine-tune/eval ≈ **~3.5–6h total on one 6000 Ada**.\\n- Needs val docs ≥64k tokens — your val shard (100M tokens) has ~1500 such windows, so fine.\\n\\n## More than 1 GPU?\\n\\n**As shipped: no** — everything is `CP_SIZE=1`. But the split matters:\\n\\n- **The search is embarrassingly parallel** — candidates are independent. Sharding them across N GPUs (N resident evaluators, each scoring a slice) gives **~N× search speedup**. This is the big multi-GPU win, and it's the dominant cost. **Not implemented yet** — the driver uses one GPU.\\n- **Fine-tune + eval stay single-GPU** (CREAM is single-GPU; `CP_EVAL_PPL` needs `CP_SIZE=1`). But they're small (~1h total), so that's fine.\\n\\n**One caveat for multi-GPU:** part of per-eval is the **CPU log-sum-exp NLL** over the 50k vocab. If you run N evaluators on one node, they contend for CPU cores → sub-linear speedup. The fix that makes multi-GPU scale cleanly is **moving the NLL to the GPU** (a small kernel), which also gives ~3–4× per-eval on a single GPU.\\n\\nSo on **4× 6000 Ada** with both extensions: search ~4× faster + ~3× from GPU-NLL → **s=16 search in ~30–60 min, total ~1.5–2.5h**.\\n\\n## Recommendation\\n- **s=4, quick check:** run locally now (~2–3h), or 6000 Ada (~1.5–2h).\\n- **s=16, or a real result:** use the 6000 Ada(s) — you need the memory anyway.\\n- **If you have multiple 6000 Adas:** the two high-value upgrades are (1) **GPU-NLL fitness** (~3–4× per-eval, both local and server) and (2) **multi-GPU search sharding** (~N×). Together they cut the s=16 search from hours to under an hour.\\n\\nWant me to implement those two — **GPU-side NLL** and **multi-GPU search sharding** in the driver (launch one resident evaluator per GPU, distribute candidates round-robin)? That's the single biggest speedup available and directly targets your multi-6000-Ada setup.\",\"background_tasks\":[],\"session_crons\":[]}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"node \"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/scripts/hooks/run-with-flags.js\" \"stop:check-console-log\" \"scripts/hooks/check-console-log.js\" \"standard,strict\"","durationMs":55},"type":"attachment","uuid":"6ab485e1-f094-4509-9119-512a7a8c29b9","timestamp":"2026-08-01T05:12:14.500Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP",