{"parentUuid":"3cc79c4f-9c64-4197-9b21-8d8fc98b8743","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"async_hook_response","processId":"async_hook_1387801","hookName":"Stop","hookEvent":"Stop","response":{},"stdout":"","stderr":"[ContinuousLearning] Session has 300 messages - evaluate for extractable patterns\n[ContinuousLearning] Save learned skills to: /home/blu-bridge25/.claude/skills/learned/\n","exitCode":0},"type":"attachment","uuid":"d2a17338-1406-473d-93da-cbc271fccbf1","timestamp":"2026-08-01T05:26:38.540Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
{"parentUuid":"d2a17338-1406-473d-93da-cbc271fccbf1","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CdbUT5MsDu4XkSgKbiJa6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1900,"cache_read_input_tokens":697872,"output_tokens":8067,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1900,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":8067,"cache_read_input_tokens":697872,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1900,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1900},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdbUSyg4KDkM8hWki6Qmg","type":"assistant","uuid":"e50b0331-f580-42f1-916a-1321b34cef8e","timestamp":"2026-08-01T05:28:13.390Z","effort":"low","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
{"parentUuid":"e50b0331-f580-42f1-916a-1321b34cef8e","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CdbUT5MsDu4XkSgKbiJa6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Great question — and the answer flips what I said earlier, because **LongRoPE-alone is actually CP-compatible in a way CREAM is not.** Let me lay it out.\n\n## First: s=64 means 256k — and that genuinely *needs* CP\n`s=64` → `scaled_max = 64 × 4096 = 262144` (256k). The problem isn't speed, it's **memory**: the eval logits tensor alone is `[1, 262144, 50304]` fp32 ≈ **52 GB** — bigger than a single 6000 Ada's 48 GB. So 256k **cannot fit on one GPU**. Splitting the sequence across 2 GPUs (**CP=2** → 128k/rank ≈ 26 GB) is *exactly* what's needed. So your instinct is right: CP is the correct tool here.\n\n## Why the code says \"CP=1 only\" — and why that's an *implementation gap*, not a real limit\n\nI mirrored CREAM's `CP_SIZE=1` constraint onto LongRoPE, but that constraint is **real for CREAM and not for LongRoPE**:\n\n- **CREAM** assigns each token an *arbitrary, non-contiguous* position label. Under CP, the fused kernel reconstructs each token's position from **shard geometry** (`pos = local_idx + delta`) — which only produces *contiguous* positions. CREAM's arbitrary labels break that, so CREAM genuinely needs the deferred per-token-position kernel work.\n- **LongRoPE keeps positions contiguous** (0,1,2,…) — it only reparametrizes the *cache*. So the CP delta mechanism reconstructs each token's true global position correctly, and indexing `longrope_cache[global_pos]` gives the right rotation. **The existing CP ring machinery already handles this.**\n\nSo the **fine-tune** (training) at CP=2 with LongRoPE-alone should work with the current code — the cache is built at full length (256k, only 67 MB, resident on every rank), and the ring kernel indexes it correctly. It's not the CREAM-CP problem.\n\n## The actual blocker: the search/eval PPL path\n\nThe real reason CP=2 doesn't work *yet* is narrow:\n- **`CP_EVAL_PPL` and the resident evaluator are hardcoded `CP_SIZE=1`** because they compute per-position NLL assuming the *whole* sequence sits on one rank. At CP>1 the sequence is sharded, so you'd need a cross-rank reduction.\n- **Good news for the search:** the search fitness is *overall* PPL, not per-position. Each rank computes its shard's `nll`/`count`, then an **`MPI_Allreduce` of two scalars** gives the global PPL. That's a tiny change — remove the assert, add the allreduce.\n- **Only the final per-position CSV** (for the graph) needs a real cross-rank *gather* — that's the harder part, but it runs once at the end, not per candidate.\n\nSo enabling CP=2 for LongRoPE is **~a small change**, not the big deferred kernel work:\n1. Remove `cp_size==1` assert in the resident evaluator + `CP_EVAL_PPL`.\n2. `MPI_Allreduce(tot_nll)` + `MPI_Allreduce(tot_cnt)` for the fitness.\n3. Verify the LongRoPE-alone install runs under CP ring (should — untested).\n4. (Later) cross-rank gather for the per-position CSV.\n\n## Reality check on s=64 for this model\nTwo honest caveats before you commit:\n- **256k training is still tight even at CP=2** — forward-only search fits (~26 GB/rank), but the *fine-tune backward* roughly doubles that → you may need **CP=4**, smaller batch, or gradient checkpointing.\n- **64× extension on a 48M model trained at 4k is extreme** — quality will likely be poor (LongRoPE's 64×+ results are on 7B models with progressive extension). On 2×48 GB, **s=16 (64k)** or **s=32 (128k)** is a more realistic and informative target.\n\n## What I can do\nThe clean path to \"no CREAM, 2 GPU, LongRoPE at long context\" is to **wire CP>1 into the LongRoPE search/eval** (steps 1–3 above — the allreduce fitness). Want me to implement that? It's the right, non-hacky way to use both 6000 Adas *and* fit 256k, and it turns your `s=64` request into something that can actually run. I'd also add the `COMPOSE=alone` toggle so there's no CREAM in the path."}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1900,"cache_read_input_tokens":697872,"output_tokens":8067,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1900,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":8067,"cache_read_input_tokens":697872,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1900,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1900},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdbUSyg4KDkM8hWki6Qmg","type":"assistant","uuid":"a2104b46-5167-4c96-a4d8-6a7e50d9102b","timestamp":"2026-08-01T05:28:32.154Z","effort":"low","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
{"parentUuid":"a2104b46-5167-4c96-a4d8-6a7e50d9102b","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"Stop","toolUseID":"bb9c8de9-220a-4785-b5c7-e8042f00c649","hookEvent":"Stop","content":"","stdout":"{\"session_id\":\"3d0a11bb-de89-4095-8132-fc46c7f9936b\",\"transcript_path\":\"/home/blu-bridge25/.claude/projects/-home-blu-bridge25-CP/3d0a11bb-de89-4095-8132-fc46c7f9936b.jsonl\",\"cwd\":\"/home/blu-bridge25/CP\",\"prompt_id\":\"208e3078-21de-4476-84a2-700d1827b1fd\",\"permission_mode\":\"acceptEdits\",\"effort\":{\"level\":\"low\"},\"hook_event_name\":\"Stop\",\"stop_hook_active\":false,\"last_assistant_message\":\"Great question — and the answer flips what I said earlier, because **LongRoPE-alone is actually CP-compatible in a way CREAM is not.** Let me lay it out.\\n\\n## First: s=64 means 256k — and that genuinely *needs* CP\\n`s=64` → `scaled_max = 64 × 4096 = 262144` (256k). The problem isn't speed, it's **memory**: the eval logits tensor alone is `[1, 262144, 50304]` fp32 ≈ **52 GB** — bigger than a single 6000 Ada's 48 GB. So 256k **cannot fit on one GPU**. Splitting the sequence across 2 GPUs (**CP=2** → 128k/rank ≈ 26 GB) is *exactly* what's needed. So your instinct is right: CP is the correct tool here.\\n\\n## Why the code says \\\"CP=1 only\\\" — and why that's an *implementation gap*, not a real limit\\n\\nI mirrored CREAM's `CP_SIZE=1` constraint onto LongRoPE, but that constraint is **real for CREAM and not for LongRoPE**:\\n\\n- **CREAM** assigns each token an *arbitrary, non-contiguous* position label. Under CP, the fused kernel reconstructs each token's position from **shard geometry** (`pos = local_idx + delta`) — which only produces *contiguous* positions. CREAM's arbitrary labels break that, so CREAM genuinely needs the deferred per-token-position kernel work.\\n- **LongRoPE keeps positions contiguous** (0,1,2,…) — it only reparametrizes the *cache*. So the CP delta mechanism reconstructs each token's true global position correctly, and indexing `longrope_cache[global_pos]` gives the right rotation. **The existing CP ring machinery already handles this.**\\n\\nSo the **fine-tune** (training) at CP=2 with LongRoPE-alone should work with the current code — the cache is built at full length (256k, only 67 MB, resident on every rank), and the ring kernel indexes it correctly. It's not the CREAM-CP problem.\\n\\n## The actual blocker: the search/eval PPL path\\n\\nThe real reason CP=2 doesn't work *yet* is narrow:\\n- **`CP_EVAL_PPL` and the resident evaluator are hardcoded `CP_SIZE=1`** because they compute per-position NLL assuming the *whole* sequence sits on one rank. At CP>1 the sequence is sharded, so you'd need a cross-rank reduction.\\n- **Good news for the search:** the search fitness is *overall* PPL, not per-position. Each rank computes its shard's `nll`/`count`, then an **`MPI_Allreduce` of two scalars** gives the global PPL. That's a tiny change — remove the assert, add the allreduce.\\n- **Only the final per-position CSV** (for the graph) needs a real cross-rank *gather* — that's the harder part, but it runs once at the end, not per candidate.\\n\\nSo enabling CP=2 for LongRoPE is **~a small change**, not the big deferred kernel work:\\n1. Remove `cp_size==1` assert in the resident evaluator + `CP_EVAL_PPL`.\\n2. `MPI_Allreduce(tot_nll)` + `MPI_Allreduce(tot_cnt)` for the fitness.\\n3. Verify the LongRoPE-alone install runs under CP ring (should — untested).\\n4. (Later) cross-rank gather for the per-position CSV.\\n\\n## Reality check on s=64 for this model\\nTwo honest caveats before you commit:\\n- **256k training is still tight even at CP=2** — forward-only search fits (~26 GB/rank), but the *fine-tune backward* roughly doubles that → you may need **CP=4**, smaller batch, or gradient checkpointing.\\n- **64× extension on a 48M model trained at 4k is extreme** — quality will likely be poor (LongRoPE's 64×+ results are on 7B models with progressive extension). On 2×48 GB, **s=16 (64k)** or **s=32 (128k)** is a more realistic and informative target.\\n\\n## What I can do\\nThe clean path to \\\"no CREAM, 2 GPU, LongRoPE at long context\\\" is to **wire CP>1 into the LongRoPE search/eval** (steps 1–3 above — the allreduce fitness). Want me to implement that? It's the right, non-hacky way to use both 6000 Adas *and* fit 256k, and it turns your `s=64` request into something that can actually run. I'd also add the `COMPOSE=alone` toggle so there's no CREAM in the path.\",\"background_tasks\":[],\"session_crons\":[]}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"node \"${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/scripts/hooks/run-with-flags.js\" \"stop:check-console-log\" \"scripts/hooks/check-console-log.js\" \"standard,strict\"","durationMs":54},"type":"attachment","uuid":"7bdae4e9-7014-4cdb-96bf-65f72f8783f4","timestamp":"2026-08-01T05:28:32.311Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-4031-b9d2-02eae46f0296","version":"2.1.220","gitBranch":"main","slug":"polished-cuddling-graham"}
