{"parentUuid":"39aeabb2-8c7a-486f-84d3-ce9661d1c25d","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CdbbwW2XFftob6kKqLJYA","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The push actually went partway and the **`git pull` just needs you to pick a reconcile strategy** — that's the only blocker now. Your branch (server cleanup) and origin (`2615900`, your local work) diverged, and git won't guess merge-vs-rebase.\n\n**Tell it to merge (safe — keeps both sides), then pull:**\n```bash\ngit config pull.rebase false     # merge strategy (one-time)\ngit pull\n```\n\nThen one of two things happens:\n\n**A) It merges cleanly** → just push:\n```bash\ngit push\n```\n\n**B) It reports a CONFLICT** — almost certainly only on **`.gitignore` / `.gitattributes`** (both machines edited them). Resolve by **keeping all lines from both sides**:\n```bash\ngit status                       # shows conflicted files\n# open .gitignore and .gitattributes, delete the <<<<<<< ======= >>>>>>> marker\n# lines but KEEP all the actual rules from both sides (union), then:\ngit add .gitignore .gitattributes\ngit commit --no-edit             # merge has a prepared message, so this works\ngit push\n```\n\nSince this is a **merge** (not a stash pop), `git commit --no-edit` will work this time — no empty-message error.\n\nAfter the server pushes, on your **local** machine:\n```bash\ngit config pull.rebase false     # if not set\ngit pull                         # converges local with everything\n```\n\nTwo reassurances:\n- The 122 deletions in the server's cleanup are all **logs/pyc/mdfiles** (generated junk) — that's intended; nothing real is lost.\n- If `git status` after the pull shows a conflict on any **real source file** (a `.cpp`, `.cu`, `.py`, `.sh`), paste it and I'll tell you exactly which side to keep. But it'll most likely just be `.gitignore`/`.gitattributes`, which you resolve by keeping both sets of rules."}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_seq