{"parentUuid":"02c9de34-279f-4967-ad54-3077d20c8818","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CdbjCdTVAd9cL1kmGtBD7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Confirmed — `GIT_ASKPASS` points to VS Code's `askpass.sh`, which is triggering that stuck browser/device flow. Bypass it and prompt in the terminal instead. Two commands:\n\n```bash\ngit config credential.helper store\nenv -u GIT_ASKPASS GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=1 git push\n```\n\n- `env -u GIT_ASKPASS` removes VS Code's askpass for this push → git prompts **in the terminal**:\n  - `Username:` → `adhityablubridge`\n  - `Password:` → paste your **Personal Access Token** (github.com → Settings → Developer settings → Personal access tokens → `repo` scope). It won't echo — paste and Enter.\n- `credential.helper store` caches it in `~/.git-credentials` after this one success.\n\n**Why this permanently fixes it:** once the token is stored, git's credential helper supplies it *before* it ever calls askpass — so future plain `git push` works even in the VS Code terminal (which keeps re-injecting `GIT_ASKPASS`). You won't hit the hang again.\n\nIf you'd rather not deal with tokens at all, SSH is the other permanent fix:\n```bash\ngit remote set-url origin git@github.com:adhityablubridge/Context_Parallelism.git\ngit push    # uses your SSH key; no askpass, no token\n```\n\nRun the two-command block, enter your username + token once, and the push goes through. Then `git pull` on your local machine to converge — and you're done with git for good this session."}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1795,"cache_read_input_tokens":799047,"output_tokens":1914,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1795,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_token