{"parentUuid":"cf67851f-4db1-4ae1-ba54-dde2eae0d1d4","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CdfxYUTLeYWtKzS6FFpmP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Good instinct — but note: **\"naive + YaRN\" and \"YaRN-only, no LongRoPE\" are the same thing.** The naive-YaRN run I described *is* the YaRN-only eval: base run 30 + YaRN cache at 32k, no LongRoPE. So you already have that (inference-only, no fine-tune).\n\nThe thing you're probably missing is a **fine-tuned** YaRN baseline. Here's the full picture:\n\n| Arm | Fine-tuned? | Cache | You have it? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Naive + YaRN** (= YaRN-only, inference) | ❌ no | YaRN formula | ✓ (`ppl_naive_yarn_x8_32k.csv`) |\n| **YaRN-LCE** (fine-tuned with YaRN) | ✅ yes | YaRN formula | **← the one you likely still need** |\n| **LongRoPE-alone** (run 81) | ✅ yes | searched | ✓ |\n\nYour LongRoPE result (run 81) is **fine-tuned**. To compare it fairly against YaRN, you want YaRN with the **same fine-tuning** — that's **YaRN-LCE**. Otherwise you're comparing fine-tuned-LongRoPE vs inference-only-YaRN, which isn't apples-to-apples (the difference would partly be \"fine-tuned vs not,\" not \"searched cache vs formula cache\").\n\n## Generate YaRN-LCE at 32k (fine-tune with YaRN, no LongRoPE)\n```bash\nARCH=\"CP_SIZE=1 CP_N_EMBD=384 CP_N_LAYER=6 CP_N_HEAD=6 CP_N_KVHEAD=2 CP_FFN=1024 CP_WEIGHT_TYING=0\"\nDR=/mnt/volgrp03/3rd_floor/Adhitya/CP/Context_Parallelism/Data_Loader/Data\nLD=BluTrain/Tensor-Implementations/lib:BluTrain/Profiler/lib\n# branch base 30 -> free run 82, fine-tune at 32k WITH YaRN, NO LongRoPE:\n(cd checkpoints_bluscriptcp && for f in blumodelcp_run30_*; do cp \"$f\" \"${f/run30/run82}\"; done)\nenv $ARCH CP_T=32768 CP_B=1 CP_GLOBAL_BATCH=131072 YARN_SCALE=8 YARN_ORIG_MAXPOS=4096 \\\n    CP_CKPT=1 CP_CKPT_RESUME=82 CP_MAX_STEPS=1217 CP_REWARMUP=100 CP_REWARMUP_PEAK=0.3 \\\n    CP_DATA_ROOT=$DR CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=5 LD_LIBRARY_PATH=$LD \\\n    mpirun -np 1 ./build/bluscriptCP_exec\n# then eval it (YaRN, no LongRoPE):\nenv $ARCH CP_EVAL_PPL=1 CP_T=32768 CP_B=1 CP_EVAL_WINDOWS=32 YARN_SCALE=8 YARN_ORIG_MAXPOS=4096 \\\n    CP_CKPT_RESUME=82 CP_EVAL_OUT=ppl_yarn_lce_x8_32k.csv CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES=5 \\\n    CP_DATA_ROOT=$DR LD_LIBRARY_PATH=$LD mpirun -np 1 ./build/bluscriptCP_exec\n```\nKey: **no `CP_LONGROPE_FACTORS`** anywhere → it uses YaRN's formula cache, and the fine-tune adapts the weights to it. Same 32k, same steps as run 81, so the only difference vs LongRoPE is searched-cache vs formula-cache.\n\n## Your clean 32k comparison set\n- `ppl_naive_yarn_x8_32k.csv` — YaRN, **no** fine-tune (inference-only)\n- `ppl_yarn_lce_x8_32k.csv` — YaRN, **fine-tuned** (this new run)\n- `ppl_longrope_x8_alone.csv` — LongRoPE, **fine-tuned** (run 81)\n\nThe decisive comparison is **YaRN-LCE vs LongRoPE-alone** (both fine-tuned). Given your search stayed flat at the NTK seed, expect these two to be **very close** — which itself is the finding: on a 48M model, LongRoPE's searched cache ≈ YaRN's formula once you fine-tune."}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":96,"cache_read_input_tokens":899148,"output_tokens":4168,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":96,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":4168,"cache_read_input_tokens":899148,"cache_creation_input_tokens":96,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":96},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdfxYJTKxNAiukYDWmPbi","type":"assistant","uuid":"b1cfe646-f590-49e1-9ccb-b4ae7451e85b","timestamp":"2026-08-03T14:18:15.208Z","effort":"low","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/blu-bridge25/CP","sessionId":"3d913bc2-47ed-403